\begin{proof}
\textbf{Proof of (1):}  
The Puiseux series solution of the parameter flow is written as
\begin{equation}
\alpha_i(z_2) = (z_2 - \beta_0)^{-\kappa_i/\gamma} 
\left( \xi_i + \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} \eta_{i,j} (z_2 - \beta_0)^{j/\gamma} \right)
=: (z_2 - \beta_0)^{-\kappa_i/\gamma} y_i.
\label{f-9}
\end{equation}
Thus, \( x(z_1, z_2) \), satisfying both equations of (A1), is given by
\begin{align}
x_i &= (z_1 - \alpha_0)^{-a_i} \left( c_i + \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} d_{i,j}(\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_{m-1}) (z_1 - \alpha_0)^j \right) \nonumber \\
&= (z_1 - \alpha_0)^{-a_i} \left( c_i + \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} d_{i,j}(y_1, \dots, y_{m-1}) (z_2 - \beta_0)^{-j/\gamma} (z_1 - \alpha_0)^j \right),
\label{f-10}
\end{align}
where we used the quasi-homogeneous scaling property \( d_{i,j}(\lambda \cdot A) = \lambda^j d_{i,j}(A) \). This shows that the solution develops a movable branch point at \( z_2 = \beta_0 \) when \( \gamma > 1 \).

However, the Painlevé property requires that \( x_i(z_1, z_2) \) be meromorphic in both \( z_1 \) and \( z_2 \), i.e., the Laurent series should involve only integer powers of \( (z_1 - \alpha_0(z_2)) \) and \( (z_2 - \beta_0) \).

Hence, to avoid fractional powers and preserve the Painlevé property, it is necessary that \( \gamma = 1 \), which implies the resonance condition:
\[
\kappa_i - \kappa_j \in \mathbb{N} \quad \text{for all } i, j.
\]
In this case, we obtain
\begin{equation}
x_i(z_1, z_2) = (z_1 - \alpha_0)^{-a_i} \left( c_i + \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} d_{i,j}(y_1, \dots, y_{m-1}) \frac{(z_1 - \alpha_0)^j}{(z_2 - \beta_0)^j} \right).
\label{f-11}
\end{equation}

Suppose that \( \rho_2, \dots, \rho_{m-1} \) include \( m-2 \) nonnegative integers, and equation (\ref{f-9}) involves \( m-1 \) free parameters (namely \( \beta_0 \) and \( m-2 \) parameters in \( \eta_{i,j} \)). Then, (\ref{f-11}) represents an \( m \)-parameter family of solutions associated with the pair of indicial loci \( (c, \xi) \).

Now, assume \( \xi \) is a principal indicial locus of the parameter flow; that is, all \( \rho_2, \dots, \rho_{m-1} \) are positive integers, and the solution (\ref{f-9}) admits \( m-1 \) free parameters. The free parameters appear in \( \eta_{i,j} \) for \( j = \rho_2, \dots, \rho_{m-1} \).

Equation (\ref{f-11}) has two movable singularities: \( z_1 = \alpha_0 \) and \( z_2 = \beta_0 \). To analyze their local behavior, introduce the new variable
\[
X = \frac{z_2 - \beta_0}{z_1 - \alpha_0},
\]
which scales the singularities relative to each other. Rewriting (\ref{f-11}) in terms of \( X \), we get:
\begin{equation}
x_i(z_1, z_2) = (z_1 - \alpha_0)^{-a_i} \left( c_i + \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} d_{i,j}(y_1, \dots, y_{m-1}) X^{-j} \right),
\label{f-12}
\end{equation}
and
\[
y_i = \xi_i + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \eta_{i,k} X^k (z_1 - \alpha_0)^k.
\]

Hence,
\begin{align}
x_i &= (z_1-\alpha_0)^{-a_i} \left( c_i + \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} d_{i,j}(y_1, \dots, y_{m-1}) X^{-j} \right), \nonumber \\
y_i &= \xi_i + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \eta_{i,k} X^k (z_1 - \alpha_0)^k \nonumber \\
&= (z_1 - \alpha_0)^{-a_i} \left( c_i + \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} d_{i,j}(\xi) + O(z_1 - \alpha_0) \right), \quad z_1 \to \alpha_0.
\label{4-12}
\end{align}

Under the situation above, (\ref{4-12}) represents a family of Laurent series solutions with fewer than \( m-1 \) parameters, since \( G \) has two negative K-exponents \( \rho_0 = \rho_1 = -1 \), and the remaining free parameters arise from \( \rho_2, \dots, \rho_{m-1} \). In this way, the lower indicial locus and its corresponding solutions are derived from the principal one.
\\[0.2cm]
\textbf{Proof of (2):} Recall the solution is expressed as (\ref{f-10})
\[
x_i(z_1, z_2) = (z_1 - \alpha_0)^{-a_i} \left( c_i + \sum_{j=1}^\infty d_{i,j}(y(z_2)) (z_2 - \beta_0)^{-\frac{j}{\gamma}} (z_1 - \alpha_0)^j \right).
\]

By the property (\ref{2-9}), each coefficient \( d_{i,j} \) is a polynomial in the parameters \(\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_{m-1}\), whose monomials have the form
\[
\alpha_1^{n_1} \cdots \alpha_{m-1}^{n_{m-1}},
\]
with nonnegative integers \( n_k \) satisfying the quasi-homogeneous weight condition
\[
\sum_{k=1}^{m-1} \kappa_k n_k = j.
\]

By assumption, the system admits an \( m \)-parameter family of solutions that depends nontrivially on \( z_2 \). This implies that there must exist monomials in \( d_{i,j} \) with total weight \( j \in \gamma \mathbb{N} \) (since the powers of \( z_2 - \beta_0 \) appear with denominator \(\gamma\)).

If the weight lattice
\[
\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}} \{\kappa_1, \ldots, \kappa_{m-1}\}
\]
does not intersect \(\gamma \mathbb{N}\) except trivially, then no such monomial can exist. In that case,
\[
d_{i,j} \equiv 0 \quad \text{for all } j \in \gamma \mathbb{N},
\]
contradicting the existence of the full \( m \)-parameter family including nontrivial deformation with respect to \( z_2 \).

Therefore, by Lemma~5.5, there must exist indices \( i \neq j \) such that
\[
\kappa_i - \kappa_j \in \gamma \mathbb{N},
\]
which completes the proof.

\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The classical Painlevé property requires solutions to be meromorphic in all variables, corresponding here to \(\gamma = 1\) and expansions in \(z_2\) with integer powers only, thus avoiding movable branch points at \(z_2 = \beta_0\).

For \(\gamma > 1\), Puiseux expansions with fractional powers arise in the parameter flow \(\alpha_i(z_2)\) and solution \(x_i(z_1,z_2)\), introducing branch points and violating strict Painlevé integrability. Such ramified expansions remain consistent and integrable provided the resonance condition
\[
\exists\, i \neq j \quad \kappa_i - \kappa_j \in \gamma \mathbb{N}
\]
holds, ensuring recursive solvability by allowing nontrivial monomials in the coefficients.

Under the assumption \(\hat{g}_0(A) \equiv 0\), the singularity parameter \(\alpha_0\) is fixed with respect to \(z_2\), so fractional powers occur only through the remaining parameters \(\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_{m-1}\). The case \(\gamma > 1\) satisfying the resonance condition always falls into this scenario.

In contrast, when \(\hat{g}_0 \not\equiv 0\), \(\alpha_0\) varies nontrivially, modifying the resonance structure and integrability conditions.
\end{remark}

We illustrate the theorem in the context of four-dimensional Painlevé equations. By the pairing property of Kovalevskaya exponents in Hamiltonian systems, which we further discuss in Section 7, the Kovalevskaya exponents 
\[
\kappa = (\kappa_0, \kappa_1, \kappa_2, \kappa_3)
\]
satisfy \(\kappa_0 = -1\), \(\kappa_3 = h\), and the middle exponents satisfy
\[
\kappa_1 + \kappa_2 = h - 1.
\]
Our theorem imposes constraints on the choice of \(\kappa_1\) and \(\kappa_2\).

\begin{example}
For \(\gamma = 3\) and \(h = 8\), consider the exponents \((-1, 2, 5, 8)\). The expansion is a Puiseux series in fractional powers of \((z_2 - \beta_0)^{1/3}\). The positive exponent differences are
\[
5 - 2 = 3, \quad 8 - 5 = 3, \quad 8 - 2 = 6,
\]
all of which lie in \(3 \mathbb{N}\). Thus, resonances occur at every step, forming a consistent chain compatible with the quasi-homogeneous structure. This allows recursive construction of higher-order terms and preserves the three expected free parameters, so this configuration is \emph{allowed}.

On the other hand, the exponent sets \((-1, 1, 6, 8)\) and \((-1, 3, 4, 8)\) fail the resonance condition since their positive differences do not lie in \(3 \mathbb{Z}\), and are therefore \emph{not allowed}.

Hence, the only legitimate exponents for \(\gamma=3\) when \(h=8\) is \((-1, 2, 5, 8)\).

We proceed by presenting a selection of representative four-dimensional Painlevé-type systems in Table~\ref{table:weights-kexp-possible}. For each system, the table lists the associated weights, the minimal ramification index \(\gamma\), the quasi-homogeneous degree \(h\), and the Kovalevskaya exponents \(\kappa\). The “Allowed?” column indicates whether the resonance condition
\[
\exists\, i \neq j : \kappa_i - \kappa_j \in \gamma \mathbb{N}
\]
required by Theorem~5.3 is satisfied, thereby identifying exponent sets consistent with the fractional power expansions in the deformation parameter.
\end{example}
