This implies that $\leftTok$ must be a subtree along the right edge of $\token$ and $\rightTok$ must be a subtree along the left edge of $\tokenp$, as those are precisely the only ways that the straddling merge can occur under our circumstances.  Lastly, for $\conflictPair{\leftTok, \rightTok}$ to block \token and \tokenp, it would need to satisfy $\parent(\leftTok) > \conflictPair{\leftTok, \rightTok} \le \parent(\rightTok)$.



\item This characterization is equivalent to $\neg \Phi(\token, \tokenp)$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{proof}





