\section{Introduction}\label{sec-intro}

\subsection{Background and main results}\label{subsec-result}

This paper is devoted to the study of compact K\"ahler manifolds with nef anti-canonical bundle, motivated by the following conjecture, which generalizes classical results for compact K\"ahler manifolds with nef tangent bundle \cite{DPS94, HSW81, Mok88}:


\begin{conj}\label{main-conj}
Let $X$ be a compact K\"ahler manifold with the nef anti-canonical bundle $-K_{X}$. 
Then, there exists a fibration $f \colon X \to Y$ satisfying the following properties$:$
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(1)$] $f \colon X \to Y$ is a locally constant fibration; 
\item[$(2)$] the fiber $F$ is a rationally connected manifold; 
\item[$(3)$] the base $Y$ is a compact K\"ahler manifold with $c_1(Y)=0$.
\end{itemize}
See \cite[Definition 2.3]{MW} for locally constant fibrations. 
\end{conj}


This conjecture was resolved by Cao--H\"oring \cite{CH19} for smooth projective varieties, 
and was generalized to projective klt pairs by the first and second named authors \cite{MW}, 
building on significant contributions by various researchers 
(details are omitted to avoid repetition; see references therein for further information). 
However, these arguments rely crucially on the existence of ample line bundles, 
so the general K\"ahler case remains largely open, apart from a few partial results, 
for example, when $\dim X = 3$~\cite{MW25} or when $-K_{X}$ is semi-positive~\cite{CDP15,DPS96}.



In this paper, we completely resolve Conjecture \ref{main-conj} in a generalized form, 
namely, for a klt pair $(X, \Delta)$ where $X$ is a compact K\"ahler manifold.

\begin{thm}\label{thm-main}
Let $(X, \Delta)$ be a klt pair consisting of a compact K\"ahler manifold $X$ and an effective $\mathbb{Q}$-divisor $\Delta$ such that the anti-log canonical $\bb{Q}$-line bundle $-(K_{X}+\Delta)$ is nef. Then, there exists a fibration $f \colon X \to Y$ with the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(1)$] $f \colon X \to Y$ is locally constant fibration with respect to the pair $(X, \Delta)$; 
\item[$(2)$] the fiber $F$ is a rationally connected manifold; 
\item[$(3)$] the base $Y$ is a compact K\"ahler manifold with $c_1(Y)=0$.
\end{itemize}
\end{thm}


This structure theorem has powerful applications, as in the projective case.  
As a first application, by combining \cite[Proposition 4.4, Theorem 4.7]{Amb05},  
we extend the Beauville--Bogomolov--Yau decomposition \cite{Bea83} to klt pairs:


\begin{cor}
Let $(X, \Delta)$ be a klt pair consisting of a compact K\"ahler manifold $X$ and an effective $\mathbb{Q}$-divisor $\Delta$ such that the anti-canonical $\bb{Q}$-line bundle $-(K_{X}+\Delta)$ is numerically trivial. Then, there exists a finite \'etale cover $\nu \colon X' \to X$ such that the pair $(X', \Delta' := \nu^{*}\Delta)$ decomposes as
\[\textstyle
(X', \Delta') \simeq (F, \Delta'_{F}) \times T \times \prod_{i} Y_{i} \times \prod_{j} Z_{j},
\]
where $F \subset X'$ is a rationally connected subvariety with $\Delta'_F:=\Delta'|_F$, $T$ is a torus, 
each $Y_i$ is a strict Calabi--Yau manifold, and each $Z_j$ is a holomorphic symplectic manifold.
\end{cor}


As a second application, we address a question posed by Hacon--M$^{\mathrm{c}}$Kernan \cite[Question 3.1]{HM07}, 
which asks for a generalization of the classical result that Fano manifolds are rationally connected \cite{KoMM92, Cam92}. 
This question was resolved in the projective case \cite{EG19, CCM19, EIM20}, but remained open in the K\"ahler setting. 
In this paper, we extend the results of \cite{EG19, CCM19} to the K\"ahler setting 
(see \cite{CCM19} for the interpretation of the formulation).



\begin{thm}\label{thm-HM}
Let $(X, \Delta)$ be a klt pair consisting of a compact K\"ahler manifold $X$ and an effective $\mathbb{Q}$-divisor $\Delta$ such that the anti-log canonical $\bb{Q}$-line bundle $-(K_{X}+\Delta)$ is nef. 
Let $F$ be a fiber of an MRC $($maximally rationally connected$)$ fibration $X \dashrightarrow Y$ of $X$. Then, the following holds$:$
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(1)$] $\kappa(-(K_{X}+\Delta)) \leq \kappa(-(K_{F}+\Delta|_{F}))$;
\item[$(2)$] $\nu(-(K_{X}+\Delta)) = \nu(-(K_{F}+\Delta|_{F}))$. 
\end{itemize}
Here $\kappa(\cdot)$ $($resp.\,$\nu(\cdot)$$)$ denotes the Kodaira dimension $($resp.\,the numerical dimension$)$.
\end{thm}



Furthermore, we establish the generic nefness of the tangent bundle $T_{X}$ (see Theorem~\ref{nefness}), 
thereby extending \cite[Theorem~1.4]{zbMATH07720766} to the K\"ahler setting. 
We also characterize the condition under which $X$ admits a $\PP^{1}$-bundle structure over a torus $T$ in terms of the second Chern class $c_{2}(X)$ (see Theorem~\ref{thm-second}), 
generalizing \cite[Corollary~1.7]{IM22}, \cite[Theorem~1.8]{zbMATH07720766}, and \cite[Theorem~1.4]{IMM} to the  K\"ahler setting. 



\subsection{Strategy of the proof and organization of the paper}\label{subsec-strategy}

In this subsection, we briefly outline the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-main} and explain the organization of this paper. 
Our strategy follows the approach in \cite{CH19, CCM19} (referred to as the \textit{previous approach}), 
but it requires significant modifications and a more delicate analysis in the K\"ahler setting. 

For simplicity, we assume that there exists an everywhere-defined MRC 
fibration $\phi \colon X \to Y$ onto a compact K\"ahler manifold $Y$. 
After reviewing the projective case, we explain how to prove that $\phi \colon X \to Y$ satisfies the properties stated in Theorem~\ref{thm-main}. 
The proof is divided into three steps. 

\noindent
\textbf{Step 1.}  
Starting from an ample line bundle $A$ on $X$, 
the previous approach constructs a relatively $\phi$-ample line bundle $B$ such that the direct image sheaf
\[
\mathcal{E} := \phi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}(mG)
\]
is pseudo-effective and has vanishing first Chern class $c_{1}(\mathcal{E}) = 0$.  
The first obstruction to extending this step to the K\"ahler setting is the absence of ample line bundles on $X$.  
Nevertheless, a general fibre $F$ of $\phi \colon X \to Y$ is a rationally connected K\"ahler manifold and is therefore projective. 
Consequently, the fibration $\phi \colon X \to Y$ admits a relatively $\phi$-ample line bundle on $X$ by \cite{CH24}, 
from which we construct a line bundle $G$ with the desired properties.
As in \cite{DPS94}, 
it is natural to consider Albanese maps instead of MRC fibrations (see \cite{Cao13, NW23, Wu23} for this approach), 
but Albanese maps are not projective morphisms, so we do not adopt this strategy. 

The second obstruction is that the previous approach uses an ample line bundle on $Y$ to apply the extension theorem, 
which is unavailable in the K\"ahler context. 
To overcome this difficulty, we \textit{localize} the extension argument 
by employing the positivity theory of direct image sheaves. 

We emphasize that a bimeromorphic analogue of the above argument is needed 
since an everywhere-defined MRC fibration does not exist in general. 
These arguments are carried out in Section~\ref{sec-main}. 



\noindent
\textbf{Step 2.}  
The previous approach proves that the reflexive hull $\mathcal{E}^{**}$ of $\mathcal{E}$ is 
locally free and numerically flat. 
The proof of this flatness relies on a surface $S \subset Y$ obtained as the intersection of ample hypersurfaces. 
The key point is to show the vanishing of the second Chern class $c_{2}(\mathcal{E}^{**})|_{S} = 0$, 
so that we can apply the theory of Hermitian--Einstein metrics for reflexive sheaves. 
However, this argument does not apply in the K\"ahler setting. 
To resolve this, we establish a new flatness criterion for pseudo-effective sheaves 
by generalizing the Segre current construction \cite{Wu22}. 
This technical ingredient is discussed in Section~\ref{sec-pre}.

\noindent
\textbf{Step 3.}  
We then show that the numerical flatness of $\mathcal{E}^{**}$ implies the desired properties of the morphism~$\phi\colon X \to Y$ (cf.\,\cite{CCM19}). Details are given in Section~\ref{sec-main}. 
In recent years, the Minimal Model Program and structure theorems for K\"ahler manifolds have developed rapidly. 
Along the way, in Section~\ref{sec-app}, we establish several technical results for K\"ahler spaces 
that are indispensable for our arguments; these results constitute one of the contributions of this paper.

As applications of Theorem~\ref{thm-main},  
we prove Theorems~\ref{thm-HM}, \ref{nefness}, and~\ref{thm-second} in Section~\ref{sec-application}.





\smallskip
This work constitutes a first step toward solving Conjecture~\ref{main-conj} in full generality for klt pairs.  
Although our main result is formulated for smooth manifolds, we treat the klt case whenever possible and indicate how the arguments extend to singular K\"ahler spaces.  
The remaining technical issues for singular ambient spaces will be addressed in a forthcoming paper.








