\subsection{Notation and conventions}\label{subsec-notation}

Throughout this paper, we work over the field of complex numbers.

We use the terms ``Cartier divisors,'' ``invertible sheaves,'' and ``line bundles'' interchangeably,
and adopt the additive notation for tensor products (e.g., $L + M := L \otimes M$ for line bundles $L$ and $M$).
Similarly, we use the terms ``locally free sheaves'' and ``vector bundles'' synonymously.
A \textit{fibration} is a proper surjective morphism with connected fibers. 
An \textit{analytic variety} is an irreducible and reduced complex analytic space.
A \textit{K\"ahler space} is an analytic variety admitting a K\"ahler form 
(i.e.,\,a smooth positive $(1,1)$-form with local potential.)





Unless stated otherwise, all sheaves are assumed to be coherent.
Let $\mathcal{E}$ be a torsion-free sheaf on a normal analytic variety $X$.
Denote by $X_{\mathcal{E}} \subseteq X$ the largest Zariski-open subset on which $\mathcal{E}$ is locally free. 
A \textit{singular Hermitian metric} on $\mathcal{E}$ means a singular Hermitian metric on the restriction
$
\mathcal{E}|_{X_{\mathcal{E}} \cap X_{\mathrm{reg}}}
$
(see \cite{Mat23} for details).  
We often refer to singular Hermitian metrics simply as ``metrics.''

For any positive integer $m \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$, we define the reflexive hulls:
\[
\mathcal{E}^{[\otimes m]} := (\mathcal{E}^{\otimes m})^{**}, \quad 
\operatorname{Sym}^{[m]} \mathcal{E} := (\operatorname{Sym}^m \mathcal{E})^{**}, \quad 
\wedge^{[m]} \mathcal{E} := (\wedge^m \mathcal{E})^{**}.
\]
The \textit{determinant sheaf} $\det \mathcal{E}$ is defined by
\[
\det \mathcal{E} := \wedge^{[\mathrm{rk}\,\mathcal{E}]} \mathcal{E}.
\]
A  torsion-free sheaf $\mathcal{E}$ of rank one is said to be \textit{$\mathbb{Q}$-Cartier}
if there exists $m \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ such that $\mathcal{E}^{[\otimes m]}$ is an invertible sheaf.
For a torsion-free sheaf $\mathcal{F}$ on $X$ and a morphism $f \colon Z \to X$ between normal varieties, 
we define:
\[
\mathcal{E} [\otimes] \mathcal{F} := (\mathcal{E} \otimes \mathcal{F})^{**}, \quad 
f^{[*]} \mathcal{E} := (f^* \mathcal{E})^{**}.
\]
For a subset $X_{0} \subset X$, 
we say that property~(P) holds \textit{over} $X_{0}$ if it holds on the preimage $f^{-1}(X_{0}) \subset Z$.

For a normal analytic space $X$, the canonical sheaf is defined by
\[
K_X := j_{[*]} \mathcal{O}_{X_{\reg}}(K_{X_{\mathrm{reg}}}),
\]
where $j \colon X_{\mathrm{reg}} \hookrightarrow X$ is the natural inclusion. 
Although $K_X$ is not necessarily represented by a Weil divisor, 
we often refer to it as the \textit{canonical divisor}.
Furthermore, for a log pair $(X, \Delta)$, we refer to $K_X + \Delta$ as the \textit{log canonical divisor},
even though it is a $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundle.






\section{Flatness criterion for pseudo-effective sheaves} \label{sec-pre}


\subsection{Singular Hermitian metrics on pseudo-effective sheaves}\label{subsec-singular}

The purpose of this section is to establish a flatness criterion 
for  pseudo-effective sheaves on compact K\"ahler manifolds (see Theorem~\ref{thm-flatness}). 
Theorem \ref{thm-flatness} was proved in \cite{CCM19} when $X$ is a smooth projective variety,  
and in \cite{Wu22} when $\mathcal{E}$ is locally free on $X$.  
The strategy of our proof follows their approach, but requires substantial improvements.  


\begin{thm}\label{thm-flatness}
Let $\mathcal{E}$ be a torsion-free sheaf on a compact K\"ahler manifold $X$. 
If $\mathcal{E}$ is a pseudo-effective sheaf with $c_{1}(\mathcal{E})=0$, 
then the reflexive hull $\mathcal{E}^{**}$  is locally free and numerically flat on $X$. 
\end{thm}
In this subsection, 
we generalize the basic theory of pseudo-effective sheaves, 
as developed in \cite{Mat23, BKK}, to the complex analytic setting.  
We work with the following setup throughout this section.


\begin{setup}\label{setup}
Let $\cal{E}$ be a torsion-free sheaf on a normal compact K\"ahler space $X$. 
Let $\pi_{\mathcal{E}} \colon  \bb{P}(\cal{E}) \to X$ denote the normalization of the irreducible component dominating $X$ of  
the projectivization ${\bf{Proj}}(\bigoplus_{m=0}^{\infty}\operatorname{Sym}^{m} \cal{E})$ 
of the graded sheaf $\bigoplus_{m=0}^{\infty}\operatorname{Sym}^{m} \cal{E}$. 
(Note that this notation slightly differs from that in \cite{Mat23}, where $\bb{P}(\cal{E})$ was defined before normalization.)
Let $\pi \colon P \to \bb{P}(\cal{E})$ 
be a desingularization of $\bb{P}(\cal{E})$, 
equipped with the commutative diagram:
\[
\xymatrix{
\bb{P}(\cal{E}) \ar[d]_{\pi_{\mathcal{E}}}&  & P\ar[ll]_{\hspace{0cm} \pi}  \ar@/^16pt/[dll]^{p} \\
X. &  & 
}
\]
Set $X_{0} := X_{\reg} \cap X_{\cal{E}}$ and $P_{0} := p^{-1}(X_{0})$. 
Note that $\codim (X \setminus X_{0}) \geq 2$. 
We may assume that $\pi \colon P \to \bb{P}(\cal{E})$ is isomorphic over $X_{0}$, 
so that $p \colon P \to X$ can be identified with the projective bundle 
$\bb{P}(\cal{E}|_{X_{0}}) \to X_{0}$ associated to the locally free sheaf $\mathcal{E}|_{X_{0}}$. 

We use the following notation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item[$\bullet$] $L := \pi^{*} \mathcal{O}_{\bb{P}(\cal{E})}(1)$, 
    where $\mathcal{O}_{\bb{P}(\cal{E})}(1)$ is the pullback of the hyperplane  bundle on 
    ${\bf{Proj}}(\bigoplus_{m=0}^{\infty} \operatorname{Sym}^{m} \cal{E})$;
  \item[$\bullet$] $\omega_{P}$ is a K\"ahler form on $P$;
  \item[$\bullet$] $\omega_{X}$ is a K\"ahler form on $X$;
  \item[$\bullet$] $\Lambda$ is an effective $p$-exceptional divisor such that
    \[
    p_{*}\mathcal{O}_{P}(m(L + \Lambda)) = \operatorname{Sym}^{[m]}\mathcal{E}.
    \]
    See \cite[\S III.5, Lemma 5.10, pp.~107--108]{Nak04} for the existence of such $\Lambda$.
\end{itemize}
\end{setup}

We can easily construct singular Hermitian metrics $\{h_{\varepsilon}\}_{\varepsilon > 0}$  
on the restriction $L|_{P_{0}}$ satisfying properties (1) and (2) in Lemma~\ref{lemm-metric},  
which can then extend to metrics on $L + \Lambda$ by Lemma~\ref{lemm-extension}.


\begin{lemm}\label{lemm-metric}
Under Setting \ref{setup}, we assume that $\mathcal{E}$ is pseudo-effective 
$($see \cite[Definition 2.1]{Mat23} for the definition$)$. 
Then, the line bundle $L|_{P_{0}}$ admits 
singular Hermitian metrics $\{h_{\e}\}_{\e>0}$ such that 
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(1)$] $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{h_{\e}} + \e p^{*}\omega_{X} \geq \delta_{\e} \omega_{P}$ holds over $X_{0}$ 
for some $\delta_{\e}>0$$;$ 
\item[$(2)$] $\{x \in P_{0}\,|\, \nu(h_{\e}, x) >0 \}$ is not dominant over $X_{0}$, 
where $\nu(h_{\e}, x)$ denotes the Lelong number of the local weight of $h_{\e}$. 
\end{itemize}
\end{lemm}
\begin{proof}
We first confirm that there exists a singular Hermitian metric $h$ on $L$
such that $h$ is smooth on $P_0$ and satisfies
\[
\sqrt{-1}\Theta_h + C\, p^{*} \omega_X \geq a\, \omega_P
\]
for some constants $C \gg 1$ and $1\gg a >0$.
The hyperplane bundle associated with ${\bf{Proj}}(\bigoplus_{m=0}^{\infty} \mathrm{S}^m \mathcal{E})$
is relatively ample with respect to ${\bf{Proj}}(\bigoplus_{m=0}^{\infty} \mathrm{S}^m \mathcal{E}) \to X$, 
and the structure sheaf $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})}$
is relatively ample with respect to the normalization $\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E}) \to {\bf{Proj}}(\bigoplus_{m=0}^{\infty} \mathrm{S}^m \mathcal{E})$.
This shows that the line bundle $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})}(1)$ is relatively $\pi_{\mathcal{E}}$-ample.
Hence, there exists a smooth Hermitian metric $g_1$ on $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})}(1)$  such that
$
\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{g_1} + C\, \pi_{\mathcal{E}}^{*} \omega_X > 0 
$
 for some constant $C > 0$.
Let $E$ be an effective $\pi$-exceptional divisor such that $-E$ is relatively $\pi$-ample.
Then $E$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric $g_{2}$ such that  
\begin{align*}
&\sqrt{-1}\Theta(L)_{\pi^{*}g_{1}} + C p^{*} \omega_{X} - \delta \sqrt{-1}\Theta_{g_{2}}(E) \\
= &\pi^{*}\big( \sqrt{-1}\Theta(L)_{g_{1}} + C \pi_{\mathcal{E}}^{*} \omega_{X} \big) - \delta \sqrt{-1}\Theta_{g_{2}}(E) >0
\end{align*}
for $1 \gg \delta > 0$.
We define the singular Hermitian metric on $L = (L - \delta E) + \delta E$ by  
\[
h := (\pi^{*}g_{1}) \cdot g_{2}^{-\delta} \cdot g_{[\delta E]},
\]
where $g_{[\delta E]}$ denotes the singular Hermitian metric associated with  the effective divisor $\delta E$.  
Then $h$ satisfies the required properties.

Next, we construct singular Hermitian metrics $\{g_{\e}\}_{\e > 0}$ on $L|_{P_{0}}$ satisfying the following properties: 
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\bullet$] $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{g_{\e}} + \e p^{*}\omega_{X} \geq 0$ on $X_{0}$;
\item[$\bullet$] $\{x \in P_{0} \mid \nu(g_{\e}, x) > 0\}$ is not dominant over $X_{0}$.
\end{itemize}
By the definition of pseudo-effective sheaves (see \cite[Definition 2.1]{Mat23}),  
there exist singular Hermitian metrics $\{h_{m}\}_{m \in \mathbb{Z}_{+}}$ on ${\rm{S}}^{[m]} \mathcal{E}$  
such that $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{h_{m}} \geq -\omega_{X} \otimes \mathrm{id}$.  
The pullbacks $p^{*} h_{m}$ define singular Hermitian metrics on $p^{*} \Sym^{m} \mathcal{E}|_{P_{0}}$  
such that $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{p^{*} h_{m}} \geq -p^{*} \omega_{X} \otimes \mathrm{id}$ on $P_{0}$.
(See \cite[Section 2]{Mat23} for the meaning of curvature inequalities.)
Let $H_{m}$ be the induced singular Hermitian metric on $L$ via the natural surjective morphism:
\[
p^{*} \Sym^{m} \mathcal{E}|_{P_{0}} \to mL|_{P_{0}}.
\]
By construction, we see that $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{H_{m}} \geq -p^{*} \omega_{X}$  
and $\{x \in P_{0} \mid \nu(H_{m}, x) > 0\}$ is not dominant over $X_{0}$.  
The metrics $H_{m}^{1/m}$ on $L|_{P_{0}}$ yield the desired family $\{g_{\e}\}_{\e > 0}$.

Finally, we define singular Hermitian metrics on $L|_{P_{0}}$ by  
\[
h_{\e} := g_{\e/2}^{1 - \delta} \cdot h^{\delta}.
\]
From the curvature properties of $h$ and $g_{\e}$, we obtain
\[
\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{h_{\e}} \geq -\left( \frac{\e}{2} + \delta C \right) p^{*} \omega_{X} + \delta a \omega_{P}.
\]
Setting $\delta := \e / 2C$, we conclude that $h_{\e}$ satisfies the required properties.
\end{proof}


In general, the metric $h_{\varepsilon}$ on $L|_{P_{0}}$  \textit{cannot} 
extend to a singular Hermitian metric on $L$ while preserving the curvature condition. 
For instance, let $\mathcal{E}$ be the ideal sheaf of a submanifold $Z \subset X$ of codimension $\ge 2$.  
Then $\mathcal{E}$ is pseudo-effective, and we may take $P$ to be the blow-up of $X$ along $Z$ 
with the exceptional divisor $E$, so that $L = -E$.  
Then, the line bundle $L = -E$ never admits metrics $h_{\varepsilon}$ with $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{h_{\e}} \geq - \e \omega_{P}$. 

As noted above, extending a metric $h_{\varepsilon}$ defined only on $P_{0}$ to $P$ is a delicate problem, 
since $P \setminus P_{0}$ can be divisorial.  
Nevertheless, by considering $L + \Lambda$ instead of $L$,  
we can extend $h_{\e}$ to a metric on $L + \Lambda$  
using the identification $L|_{P_{0}} \cong (L + \Lambda)|_{P_{0}}$,  
which is a key step in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm-flatness}.



\begin{lemm}\label{lemm-extension}
Consider the setting of Lemma \ref{lemm-metric}.  
Then, the metric $h_{\e}$ constructed in Lemma \ref{lemm-metric} 
extends to a singular Hermitian metric on $L+\Lambda$ satisfying the same properties~$(1)$ and~$(2)$ over $X$.
\end{lemm}
\begin{proof}
We omit the subscript $\e$ in $h_{\e}$ and regard the metric $h$ on $L|_{P_{0}}$ as a metric on $(L+\Lambda)|_{P_{0}}$ via the identification $L|_{P_{0}} \cong (L+\Lambda)|_{P_{0}}$.  
It suffices to show that the local weight $\varphi$ of $h$ is bounded from above in a neighborhood of $P\setminus P_{0}$.  
The problem is local on $X$, so we may assume that $X$ is Stein.  
Then, replacing $h$ by $h\,e^{-\psi}$, where $\psi$ is a local potential of $\e\,p^{*}\!\omega_{X}$, 
we may assume that $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{h}$ is a K\"ahler current by property~$(1)$.  

For a positive integer $m\in\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$, we consider the $L^{2}$-space  
\[
H^{0}_{L^{2}}(P_{0},m(L+\Lambda))_{h^{m},\omega_{P}}
\]
of (holomorphic) sections $\sigma$ on $P_{0}$ with the finite $L^{2}$-norm $\|\sigma\|_{h^{m},\omega_{P}}< \infty$.  
Let $B_{m}$ denote the Bergman kernel of this Hilbert space,  
which is the singular Hermitian metric on $(L+\Lambda)|_{P_{0}}$ whose local weight $b_{m}$ is defined to be 
\[
b_{m}(p)%=\frac{1}{m}\log\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}|\sigma_{k}(p)|^{2}
          :=\frac{1}{m}\sup\Bigl\{\log|\sigma|(p) \;\Big|\;\|\sigma\|_{h^{m},\omega_{P}}\le 1\Bigr\} \quad \text{ for } p\in P_{0}. 
\]
Then, for every relatively compact set $K\Subset X$, there exists a constant $C=C(K)$ (independent of $m$) such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq-dem}
\varphi(p)\le b_{m}(p)+\frac{C}{m}\qquad\text{for }p\in P_{0}\cap p^{-1}(K).
\end{equation}
This can be verified by modifying Demailly's approximation technique (cf. \cite[(13.21) Theorem, p.138]{Dem10}). 
We briefly explain the proof for the reader's convenience.

Fix a point $p_{0} \in P_{0}\cap p^{-1}(K)$. 
Take an open ball $B \Subset P$ centered at $p_{0}$ and 
a hypersurface $H\subset X$ such that $X\setminus X_{0} \subset H$ and $p_{0} \not \in p^{-1}(H)$.  
Note that $P\setminus p^{-1}(H)$ is a weakly pseudoconvex K\"ahler manifold and $B\setminus p^{-1}(H)$ is Stein. 
This allows us to solve $\dbar$-equations on $P\setminus p^{-1}(H)$ and 
to apply the  $L^{2}$-extension theorem on $B\setminus  p^{-1}(H)$.
If $\varphi(p_{0})=-\infty$, the inequality is trivial, so we may assume $\varphi(p_{0})>-\infty$.  
For a given  $a\in\mathbb{C}$, 
by the $L^{2}$-extension theorem, there exists a holomorphic function $f$ on $B\setminus p^{-1}(H)$ such that
\[
f(p_{0})=a \quad \text{ and } \quad 
\int_{B\setminus p^{-1}(H)}|f|^{2}e^{-m\varphi}\,dV_{\omega_{P}}\le C_{1}|a|^{2}e^{-m\varphi(p_{0})}.
\]
Fix a relatively compact subset $K_{P}\Subset B$ and consider a cut-off function $\theta$ supported in $B$ and equal to~$1$ on $K_{P}$.  
Since $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{h}$ is a K\"ahler current, 
by the standard technique of $\dbar$-equations, 
we can solve $\overline{\partial}g=\overline{\partial}(\theta f)$ on $P\setminus p^{-1}(H)$ with the $L^{2}$-estimate
\[
\int_{P\setminus p^{-1}(H)}|g|_{h^{m}}^{2}\,e^{-2n\theta\log|z-z(p_{0})|}\,dV_{\omega_{P}}
\le C_{2}\int_{B\setminus p^{-1}(H)}|f|^{2}e^{-m\varphi}\,dV_{\omega_{P}}, 
\]
where $z$ is a coordinate of $B$ and $n:=\dim P$. 
Then, the section $\sigma:=\theta f -g$ satisfies that  
\begin{align}\label{eq-norm}
\sigma(p_{0})= f(p_{0})=a \quad \text{ and } \quad  
\|\sigma\|_{h^{m}}\le C_{3}|a|^{2}e^{-m\varphi(p_{0})}, 
\end{align}
where we identify $\sigma$ with a holomorphic function via a fixed local frame.
Since $h$ is defined near every point of $p^{-1}(H)\cap P_{0}$, 
any $L^{2}$-sections on $P\setminus p^{-1}(H)$ extend to $P_{0}$, giving the isomorphism
\[
H^{0}_{L^{2}}(P_{0},m(L+\Lambda))_{h^{m},\omega_{P}}
\cong H^{0}_{L^{2}}(P\setminus p^{-1}(H),m(L+\Lambda))_{h^{m},\omega_{P}}.
\]
This shows that $\sigma$ lies in the left-hand space. 
We can choose $a$ so that the right-hand side in \eqref{eq-norm} equals $1$, and thus, we obtain inequality \eqref{eq-dem}.

\smallskip 
Henceforth, to simplify notation, we assume $m = 1$ and set $L_{\Lambda} := L + \Lambda$.
We will show that the weight $b:=b_{1}$ of the Bergman kernel is locally bounded from above near $P \setminus P_{0}$.  
The crucial point is that every section of $L_{\Lambda}$ on $P_{0}$ extends holomorphically to $P$ 
since $p_{*}(L_{\Lambda})$ is reflexive due to the choice of $\Lambda$. 

Fix a trivialization of $L_{\Lambda}$ on an open ball $B \subset X$.  
Then, for each point $p \in B_{0}:=B \cap P_{0}$, we consider the evaluation map: 
\[
\mathrm{ev}_{p} \colon 
H^{0}_{L^{2}}(P_{0},L_{\Lambda})_{h,\omega_{P}}
\longrightarrow
H^{0}(B_{0},L_{\Lambda}) \cong H^{0}(B_{0},\mathcal{O}_{B})
\longrightarrow
\mathbb{C}. 
\]
Taking duals and the operator norm, 
we can interpret the weight $b$  as the composition 
\[
b=b_{1}\colon\,
B_{0}\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{ev}^{*}\ }
\bigl(H^{0}_{L^{2}}(P_{0},L_{\Lambda})_{h,\omega_{P}}\bigr)^{*}
\xrightarrow{\ \|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{op}}\ }
\mathbb{R}, 
\]
where the dual space is equipped with the strong topology.
By Montel's theorem, the map $\mathrm{ev}^{*}$ is continuous.  
Hence, it is enough to extend $\mathrm{ev}^{*}$ continuously to $B$; 
once this is done, $b=b_{1}$ is bounded on compact subsets of $B$.

The restriction map
\[
H^{0}(P,L_{\Lambda}) \longrightarrow H^{0}(P_{0},L_{\Lambda})
\]
is an isomorphism of Fr\'echet spaces with the topology induced by local sup-norms. 
Indeed, this map is clearly continuous, injective, and linear. 
Moreover, it is surjective 
since we have
\[
H^{0}(P,L_{\Lambda})
\cong H^{0}(X,p_{*}(L_{\Lambda}))
\cong H^{0}(X_{0},p_{*}(L_{\Lambda}))
\cong H^{0}(P_{0},L_{\Lambda})
\]
by reflexivity. 
Hence, the map is a homeomorphism by the open mapping theorem.
This induces a continuous map
\[
H^{0}(P,L_{\Lambda})^{*}
\cong H^{0}(P_{0},L_{\Lambda})^{*}
\to
\bigl(H^{0}_{L^{2}}(P_{0},L_{\Lambda})_{h,\omega_{P}}\bigr)^{*}. 
\]
On the other hand, there exists a continuous map
\[
B \to H^{0}(B, L_{\Lambda})^{*} \to H^{0}(P,L_{\Lambda})^{*}.
\]
The composition of these maps extends $b = b_{1}$ to $B$, proving the desired local boundedness.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Segre currents and second Chern classes}\label{subsec-segre}

To prove Theorem \ref{thm-flatness}, 
we generalize the results of \cite{Wu22}, which were originally formulated for projective space bundles, 
to a broader setting: 


\begin{thm}\label{Segre}
Let $f\colon X \to Y$ be a fibration of relative dimension $r-1$ 
between compact K\"aher manifolds, smooth outside a Zariski closed subset $Z \subset Y$ with  
$\codim Z \geq k+1$.
Let $T$ be a semi-positive $(1,1)$-current on $X$ with analytic singularities such that  
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\bullet$] $T=\alpha+\ddbar\varphi$, where $\alpha$ is a $d$-closed smooth $(1,1)$-form on $X$;
\item[$\bullet$] $T$ is smooth outside a Zariski closed set  
      $A\subset X$ with $A=\pi^{-1}\!\bigl(\pi(A)\bigr)$ and $\operatorname{codim}\pi(A)\ge k$.
\end{itemize}
Let $c\colon Y\setminus Z \to \RR_{>0}$ be a continuous function. 
Assume that for every point $x\in\pi^{-1}(Y\setminus Z)$, 
there exists a quasi-psh function $\psi$ on $X$ $($depending on $x$$)$ with analytic singularities such that 
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\bullet$] $\alpha+\ddbar\psi$ is semi-positive on a neighborhood of the fiber $X_{\pi(x)}$;
\item[$\bullet$] $\psi$ is smooth outside a Zariski closed set $B \subset X$ with $\codim B\geq k+r$;
\item[$\bullet$] $\nu(\psi,x)\ge c (\pi(x))$.
\end{itemize}

Then, there exists a semi-positive $(k,k)$-current $S$ in the class $f_{*}\{\alpha\}^{\,r+k-1}$ such that for every $y\in Y\setminus Z$,
\[
\nu(S,y)\;\ge\;
\min\!\Bigl\{\bigl(\sup_{x\in X_{y}}\nu(\varphi,x)\bigr)^{\,r+k-1},\;c(y)\Bigr\}.
\]
\end{thm}

\begin{proof}
The proposition is proved by suitably adapting the arguments of \cite{Wu22}.  
To avoid repetition, we explain only the essential modifications here.

Following \cite[Section~4]{Wu22}, we define a $(k,k)$-current $S$ on $Y$ by
\[
S := \wlim_{\delta \to 0} f_* \big( ( \alpha + \ddbar \log (e^{\varphi} + \delta) )^{r+k-1} \big)
\in \pi_* \{\alpha\}^{r+k-1}.
\]
Note that $S$ is well-defined since the potential function is bounded  thanks to $\delta$. 
Fix a point $y \in Y \setminus Z$, and take $x \in \pi^{-1}(y)$.
Using the function $\psi$ in the assumptions, we consider
\[
\wlim_{\delta \to 0} f_*  \big( ( \alpha + \ddbar \log (e^{\varphi} + \delta e^{\psi}) )^{r+k-1} \big)
\in \pi_* \{\alpha\}^{r+k-1}, 
\]
which is clearly a semi-positive current near $y$. 
Moreover, this coincides with $S$, showing that $S$ is semi-positive on $Y \setminus Z$.
This follows from the argument in \cite[Lemma~14]{Wu22}. 
Indeed, under the codimension condition on $\pi(A)$, the difference between two weak limits arising from different choices of $\psi$ is a normal current supported on $\pi(A)$.  
By Demailly's support theorem, this difference is a finite linear combination of integration currents over irreducible components of $\pi(A)$, with coefficients given by the generic Lelong numbers along those components.  
Thanks to the codimension assumption on $Z$, we can compute these Lelong numbers at generic points outside $Z$, 
and hence the local argument in \cite[Lemma~14]{Wu22} applies without modification.

Moreover, by a version of the Skoda--El Mir extension theorem (see \cite[pp.~178, Cor.~3.2]{Sib85}), the $(k,k)$-current $S$ extends across the analytic subset $Z \subset Y$ of codimension $\geq k+1$.  
By
\[
H^{2k}(Y \setminus Z, \RR) \cong H^{2k}(Y, \RR),
\]
the extended current represents the same cohomology class.
The Lelong number estimate is obtained by a local computation 
(see \cite[pp.~52--53]{Wu22} for details).
\end{proof}






Applying Theorem \ref{Segre}, 
we prove that  the semi-negativity of the second Chern class $c_{2}(\cal{E}^{**})$, 
which is  a key point in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm-flatness}. 




\begin{prop}\label{prop-c2}
Under Setting \ref{setup}, assume that $\mathcal{E}$ is a pseudo-effective reflexive sheaf 
and satisfies that $c_{1}(\mathcal{E})=0$. 
Then, the second Chern class $c_{2}(\cal{E})$ is represented by a semi-negative $(2,2)$-current on $X$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}




We may assume that the metric $h_{\varepsilon} $ in Lemma \ref{lemm-extension} has analytic singularities by Demailly's approximation theorem (see \cite[(13.21) Theorem, pp.~138]{Dem10}).  
Let $\varphi_{\varepsilon} $ be the quasi-psh function associated with $h_{\varepsilon} $, so that
\[
\alpha_{\varepsilon} + \ddbar \varphi_{\varepsilon}
:= (\beta + \varepsilon p^{*}\omega_{X}) + \ddbar \varphi_{\varepsilon} 
\geq \delta_{\varepsilon} \omega_{P} \geq 0,
\]
where $\beta $ is a smooth representative of $c_{1}(L + \Lambda) $.
The non-smooth locus $Z:=X \setminus X_{0}$ of the fibration $p \colon P \to X $ 
satisfies the assumptions of Theorem \ref{Segre} for $k=2 $
since $\mathcal{E}$ is reflexive. 
The singular locus $A$ of $\varphi_{\e}$ may not satisfy 
the assumption of Theorem \ref{Segre} for $k=2 $, 
but satisfies this assumption for $k=1 $  by property (2). 
Note that there exists $\psi$ with the assumptions in Theorem \ref{Segre} (see \cite[pp.~52]{Wu22}).
Thus, applying Theorem \ref{Segre} to each $\varepsilon $ in the case $k=1 $, 
we obtain a semi-positive $(1,1) $-current $S_{\varepsilon} $ on $X $ such that
\begin{align}\label{eq-lelong}
S_{\varepsilon} \in p_* \{\alpha_{\varepsilon}\}^{r} \quad\text{and}\quad
\nu(S_{\varepsilon}, y) \geq  
\min\!\Bigl\{\bigl(\sup_{x\in P_{y}}\nu(\varphi,x)\bigr)^{\,r},\;c(y)\Bigr\}
\end{align}
for every $y \in X_0 = X \setminus Z $.  
Note that this estimate may not hold for $y \in Z $.

We now compute  the cohomology class 
\[
p_*\left(c_1(L + \Lambda)\right)^{r}  = c_1(\mathcal{E}) = 0
\quad \text{on } X_0,
\]
and then, the same equality holds on $X $ by Proposition \ref{topo} and $H^2(X, \mathbb{R}) \cong H^2(X_0, \mathbb{R}) $. 
Thus, we obtain 
\[
S_{\varepsilon} \in 
p_* \{\alpha_{\varepsilon}\}^{r}
= p_*\left( c_1(L + \Lambda) + \varepsilon p^*\omega_X \right)^r
\to  0
\quad \text{as } \varepsilon \to 0.
\]
This shows that $\sup_{y \in X} \nu(S_{\varepsilon}, y) \to 0 $ (for example, see \cite[Lemma 15]{Wu22}).  
By estimate \eqref{eq-lelong}, 
this implies that $\nu(\varphi_{\delta}, x) \to 0 $ locally uniformly over $p^{-1}(X \setminus Z) $.

Let $\{K_j\}_{j \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}} $ be an open cover of $X \setminus Z$
such that $K_j \Subset K_{j+1} \Subset X \setminus Z$. 
Fix a decreasing sequence $\varepsilon_j \to 0 $.  
By Demailly's regularization theorem with smooth forms (see \cite[Th\'eor\`eme 9.1]{Dem82}, \cite[Theorem 1.1]{Dem94}), 
we find smooth quasi-psh functions $\tilde{\varphi}_j $ on $P $ such that
\[
\tilde{T}_j := \alpha_{\varepsilon_j} + \ddbar \tilde{\varphi}_j \geq -\varepsilon_j \omega_P
\quad \text{on } p^{-1}(K_j).
\]
This estimate follows from the local uniform convergence of $\nu(\varphi_{\delta}, x) $ on $p^{-1}(X \setminus Z) $.


We can define $(2,2)$-currents on $X $ by
\[
S_j := p_* \big( \tilde{T}_j + \varepsilon_j \omega_P \big)^{r+1}
\in p_*\left\{ \alpha_{\varepsilon_j} + \varepsilon_j \omega_P \right\}^{r+1}.
\]
Note that $S_j $ is not semi-positive on $X $, but it is smooth on $X \setminus Z $ and belongs to a bounded family of cohomology classes.  
By compactness, after passing to a subsequence, we may assume  that $S_j $ converges weakly to some current $S $.
For fixed $j_0 $, the family $\{S_j\}_{j \ge j_0} $ is semi-positive on $K_{j_0} $.
Hence, the limit $S $ is semi-positive on each $K_{j_0} $, hence on  $X \setminus Z $.
The current $S $ extends to a semi-positive current on  $X $, as in the argument of Proposition \ref{Segre}. 

The current $S$ represents the class 
$$\lim_{j \to \infty} p_*\left\{ \alpha_{\varepsilon_j} + \varepsilon_j \omega_P \right\}^{r+1} =p_*\left( c_1(L + \Lambda) \right)^{r+1}.$$
Observe that 
\[
p_*\left( c_1(L + \Lambda) \right)^{r+1}
= c_1^2(\mathcal{E}) - c_2(\mathcal{E}) = -c_2(\mathcal{E}).
\]
on $X_0 $. 
The same equality holds globally on $X $ 
by Proposition \ref{topo} and $H^4(X, \mathbb{R}) \cong H^4(X_0, \mathbb{R}) $. 
This completes the proof. 
\end{proof}


With the above preparations, Theorem \ref{thm-flatness} follows easily from the arguments in \cite{CCM19, Wu22}. 

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{thm-flatness}]
It is sufficient to show that $\mathcal{E}$ is locally free. 
Indeed, once this is established, the numerical flatness of $\mathcal{E}$ follows directly from \cite{Wu22}. 

Take a reflexive subsheaf $\mathcal{S} \subset \mathcal{E}$ 
of minimal rank among the coherent subsheaves satisfying 
\begin{align}\label{eq-slope}
\int_X c_{1} (\mathcal{S}) \cdot \omega_X^{n-1} \geq 0.
\end{align}
Consider the exact sequence
\begin{align}\label{exact}
0 \rightarrow \mathcal{S} \rightarrow \mathcal{E} \rightarrow 
\mathcal{Q} := \mathcal{E}/\mathcal{S} \rightarrow 0.
\end{align}
The quotient sheaf $(\mathcal{Q}/Q_{\tor})$ modulo torsion is pseudo-effective, 
since it is also a quotient of the pseudo-effective sheaf $\mathcal{E}$. 
In particular, its first Chern class $c_{1}(\mathcal{Q})$ is also pseudo-effective, 
since the torsion part contributes only a pseudo-effective first Chern class by Proposition \ref{det-tor}.
We can then deduce that $c_{1}(\mathcal{S}) = c_{1}(\mathcal{Q}) = 0$. 
Indeed, we have $c_{1}(\mathcal{S}) = -c_{1}(\mathcal{Q})$ from 
$c_{1}(\mathcal{E}) = 0$, and the equalities follow from \eqref{eq-slope}.

If $\mathcal{S} = \mathcal{E}$, 
then by the stability of $\mathcal{S}$ and the condition $c_1(\mathcal{S}) = 0$, we have
\[
\int_{X} c_{2}(\mathcal{S}) \cdot \omega_{X}^{n-2} \geq 0.
\]
Combined with Proposition~\ref{prop-c2}, we obtain $\int_X c_2(\mathcal{S}) = 0$, 
and hence $\mathcal{S} = \mathcal{E}$ is locally free by \cite[Corollary~3]{BS94}. 

It remains to consider the case $\rank \mathcal{S} < \rank \mathcal{E}$. 
Applying \cite[Lemma 2.9]{IMZ23} (cf.\,\cite[Lemma~1.20]{DPS94}) to the morphism $\mathcal{Q}^{*} \to \mathcal{E}^*$, 
we deduce that both $\mathcal{Q}$ and $\mathcal{S}$ are vector bundles on $X_{\mathcal{E}}$, 
and that the morphism is a bundle morphism  over $X_{\mathcal{E}}$. 
Consider the surjective bundle morphism 
\[
\Lambda^{m+1} \mathcal{E} \otimes \det \mathcal{Q}^* \to \mathcal{S}
\]
on $X_{\mathcal{E}}$, where $m = \rank \mathcal{Q}$. 
This implies that the reflexive sheaf $\mathcal{S}$ is pseudo-effective 
by $c_1(\mathcal{Q}) = 0$. 
By the induction hypothesis on the rank, 
we conclude that $\mathcal{S}$ is a numerically flat locally free sheaf on $X$. 
Similarly, the reflexive hull $\mathcal{Q}^{**}$ is a locally free sheaf on $X$ by the same hypothesis.

By \cite[Lemma~16]{Wu22}, the extension class of \eqref{exact}, defined on $X_{\mathcal{E}}$, 
extends to an extension class on $X$ between $\mathcal{S}$ and $\mathcal{Q}^{**}$. 
This extension determines a vector bundle whose restriction to $X_{\mathcal{E}}$ coincides with $\mathcal{E}$. 
Hence, by reflexivity, we conclude that $\mathcal{E}^{**}$ is locally free. 
\end{proof}





\section{Certain numerical flatness of direct image sheaves} \label{sec-main}

The purpose of this section is to prove Theorem \ref{thm-faked-flat}. 
To this end, we first summarize some preliminary results in Subsection \ref{subsec-pre}, 
and then begin the proof of Theorem \ref{thm-faked-flat} in Subsection \ref{subsec-setup}.

\subsection{Preliminary results on singular Hermitian metrics}\label{subsec-pre}


In this subsection, we summarize several results that will be used in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm-faked-flat}. 
By applying the positivity results for direct image sheaves in \cite{PT18, HPS18}, 
we obtain the following proposition:


\begin{prop}\label{prop-psef}
Let $f \colon X \to Y$ be a fibration between compact  K\"ahler manifolds. 
Consider the following data$:$
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\bullet$] $G$ is a relatively $f$-big  line bundle on $X$; 
\item[$\bullet$] $N$ is a nef line bundle on $X$; 
\item[$\bullet$] $L$ is a $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundle admitting 
a singular Hermitian metric $h_{L}$ with semi-positive curvature 
such that 
$\mathcal{I}(h_{L})|_{X_{y}}=\mathcal{O}_{X_{y}}$ holds 
for a general fiber $X_{y}$. 
\end{itemize}
Assume that $f_*\OX_X(mK_{X/Y} + G +mN + mL )$ is a non-zero sheaf 
for a sufficiently large $m \gg 1$ with $mL$ Cartier. Then, we have$:$

\begin{itemize}
\item[$(1)$] The $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundle $K_{X/Y}+N+L$ is pseudo-effective. 

\item[$(2)$] 
Let $\theta$ be a $d$-closed smooth $(1,1)$-form on $Y$. 
We further assume that $G$ is $f^{*}\theta$-pseudo-effective 
$($i.e.,\,it admits a singular Hermitian metric $h_{\theta}$ 
such that $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{h_{\theta}}\geq f^{*}\theta$
$)$.
Then, the direct image sheaf $f_{*}\OX_X(mK_{X/Y} + G +mN + mL)$ is $\theta$-weakly positively curved 
for $m \gg 1$ with $mL$ Cartier $($see \cite[Definition 2.7]{MW} for the definition.$)$

\end{itemize}
\end{prop}


\begin{proof}
We first remark that $f\colon X \to Y$ is a projective morphism by \cite[Theorem 1.1]{CH24}. 


(1)
By assumption, there exists a singular Hermitian metric $h$ on $G$ 
such that $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{h} + f^{*}\omega_{Y} \geq \omega_{X}$, 
and there exist smooth Hermitian metrics $\{g_{\e}\}_{\e>0}$ on $mN$
such that $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{g_{\e}} \geq -\e \omega_{X}$. 
Here $\omega_{X}$ and $\omega_{Y}$ are K\"ahler forms on $X$ and $Y$, respectively. 
The singular Hermitian metric $H := h \cdot g_{\e} \cdot h_{L}^{m}$ on $G + mN + mL$
satisfies $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{H} \geq -f^{*}\omega_{Y}$. 
Thus, we have $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{H \cdot e^{-f^{*}\psi}} \geq 0$, 
where $\psi$ is a local potential of $\omega_{Y}$. 

Consider the $m$-Bergman kernel metric $B_{m}^{-1}$
on $mK_{X/Y} + G + mN + mL$ induced by $H$ (see \cite[\S 4.2]{PT18} for the definition), 
and the $m$-Bergman kernel metric $B_{m}^{'-1}$ induced by $H \cdot e^{-f^{*}\psi}$, 
which is defined locally on $Y$. 
By the assumption on $h_{L}$, 
the $L^{2/m}$-multiplier ideal sheaf $\mathcal{J}_{m}(H^{1/m})$ satisfies
\begin{align}\label{eq-Lm}
\mathcal{J}_{m}(H^{1/m} |_{X_{y}}) = \mathcal{J}_{m}(h^{1/m} h_{L} |_{X_{y}}) = \mathcal{O}_{X_{y}}
\end{align}
for a general fiber $X_{y}$ and $m \gg 1$. 
Here, we use the solution of the strong openness conjecture and H\"older's inequality. 
Thus, by \cite[Theorem 4.2.2]{PT18} (see also \cite[Theorem 3.5]{Cao17}), we deduce that 
the curvature of $B_{m}^{'-1}$ is semi-positive. 
By construction, we have
\[
B_{m}^{'-1} = B_{m}^{-1} \cdot e^{-f^{*}\psi}.
\]
This shows that 
\[
\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{B_{m}^{-1}}(mK_{X/Y} + G + mN + mL) \geq -\omega_{Y}.
\]
By taking the limit as $m \to \infty$, we obtain the conclusion. 

(2) 
We may assume that $\omega_{Y} \geq \theta$ by replacing $\omega_{Y}$ if necessary. 
The singular metric $G := h^{\delta} \cdot h_{\theta}^{1-\delta} \cdot g_{\e} \cdot h_{L}^{m}$ 
on $G + mN + mL$ satisfies
\[
\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{G} \geq 
-\delta f^{*}\omega_{Y} + \delta \omega_{X} - \e \omega_{X} + (1-\delta) f^{*}\theta
\geq f^{*}(\theta - 2\delta \omega_{Y})
\]
for $\delta > \e > 0$. 
The $L^{2/m}$-multiplier ideal sheaf $\mathcal{J}_{m}(G^{1/m})$ 
satisfies the same property as in \eqref{eq-Lm}.  
Hence, \cite[Theorem 21.1]{HPS18} (see also \cite[Theorem 5.3]{DWZZ18}) leads to the desired conclusion.
\end{proof}


The following lemmas are slight modifications of \cite[Lemmas 2.8 and 2.10]{MW}.



\begin{lemm}\label{lem-push}
Let $\pi \colon  M \to X$ be a bimeromorphic morphism between normal compact K\"ahler spaces 
and let $\mathcal{F}$ be a weakly positively curved sheaf on $M \setminus E_{\pi}$ 
with respect to a K\"ahler form $\omega_{M}$ on $M$ $($not only on $M \setminus E_{\pi}$$)$, 
where $E_{\pi}$ is the exceptional locus of $\pi \colon  M \to X$. 
Then, the push-forward $\pi_{*} \mathcal{F}$ is weakly positively curved  on $X$. 
\end{lemm}
\begin{proof}
The lemma was proved in \cite[Lemma 2.10]{MW} under additional assumptions. 
In what follows, we explain how to adapt the argument in \cite[Lemma 2.10]{MW} to our setting.
The key point in the proof is to show that $\pi^{*} \{\omega_{X}\} - c_{1}(G)$ is a K\"ahler class on $M$ 
for some K\"ahler form $\omega_{X}$ on $X$ and  effective $\pi$-exceptional divisor $G$. 







However, in our setting, this point is not obvious. 
Moreover, the pushforward $\pi_* \omega_M$ of an arbitrary K\"ahler form $\omega_M$ may not  define a Bott-Chern class on $X$ 
(i.e.,\,$\pi_* \omega_M$ may not admit a local potential). 
An idea of the proof is to consider $\pi^{*}\{\omega_X\} - c_1(G)$ instead of $\omega_M$ 
so that the pushforward $\pi_* (\pi^{*}\{\omega_X\} - c_1(G))$ define a Bott-Chern class on $X$. 


We may assume that $M$ is smooth by taking a desingularization. 
By Hironaka's flattening theorem \cite{Hir75}, 
there exists a sequence of blow-ups $p \colon \tilde{X} \to X$ with smooth centers, 
and a bimeromorphic morphism $q \colon \tilde{X} \to M$ with the following diagram:
\begin{align}\label{eq-hironaka}
\xymatrix{
\tilde{X} \ar[d]_q \ar[rd]^p & \\
M \ar[r]_\pi & X
}
\end{align}
Then, there exists an effective $p$-exceptional $\QQ$-divisor $E$ such that
$p^* \{\omega_X\} - c_1(E)$ is a K\"ahler class on $\tilde{X}$. 
Thus, the pushforward 
$$
q_*(p^* \{\omega_X\} - c_1(E))
=\pi^{*}\{\omega_X\} - c_1(G)
$$ 
contains a current $T$ such that 
$T \geq  \omega_{M}$ holds for some K\"aher form $\omega_{M}$ on $M$, 
where $G := q_*(E)$ is the pushforward of the divisor $G$. 
Since we have 
\[
\pi_* (\pi^{*}\{\omega_X\} - c_1(G)) = \{\omega_X\} \in H^{1,1}_{BC}(X, \RR),
\]
there exists a quasi-psh function $\varphi$ on $X$ such that
\begin{align}\label{eq-pot}
T = \pi^* \omega_X - [G] + \ddbar \pi^* \varphi,
\end{align}
where $[G]$ denotes the integration current.

In the proof of \cite[Lemma 2.10]{MW}, 
the current $T$ was taken to be the K\"ahler form $\omega_M$. 
Nevertheless, the same argument applies to the current~$T$, 
and the desired conclusion follows from the fact that any $\varepsilon\omega_M$-psh function is also $\varepsilon T$-psh, since $T \ge \omega_M$.
\end{proof}











\begin{lemm}\label{lem-pull}
Let $\phi \colon M \to Y$ be a fibration between normal compact K\"ahler spaces, 
and let $Y_{0} \subset Y$ be a Zariski open subset 
such that $\phi|_{M_{0}} \colon M_{0}:=\phi^{-1}(Y_{0}) \to Y_{0}$ is a flat fibration. 
Let $\mathcal{E}$ be a torsion-free sheaf on $Y$. 
Assume that $\mathcal{E}$ is weakly positively curved on $Y_{0}$ with respect to a K\"ahler form $\omega_{Y}$ on $Y$. 
Then, we have$:$
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(1)$] 
The reflexive pullback $\phi^{[*]}\mathcal{E}$ is also weakly positively curved on $M_{0}$ with respect to a K\"ahler form $\omega_{M}$ on $M$. 

\item[$(2)$]
Let $\pi \colon M \to X$ be a bimeromorphic morphism onto a normal compact K\"ahler space $X$ 
such that  $M \setminus M_{0}$ is contained in the $\pi$-exceptional locus $E_{\pi}$. 
Then, the reflexive pushforward $\pi_{[*]}\phi^{[*]}(\mathcal{E})$ is weakly positively curved on $X$. 

\end{itemize}

\end{lemm}
\begin{proof}
Conclusion~(1) follows directly from \cite[Lemma 2.8]{MW}, 
and conclusion~(2) follows from Lemma~\ref{lem-push}.
\end{proof}



The following result is straightforward; however, we state it as a lemma since it is used frequently.


\begin{lemm}\label{lem-Qfact}
Let $\pi \colon  M \to X$ be a bimeromorphic morphism 
between strongly $\mathbb{Q}$-factorial compact K\"ahler spaces. 
Let $L$ be a line bundle on $M$. 
Consider the $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundle $B:=(1/m)\pi_{[*]}\mathcal{O}_{M}(mL)$ for a divisible integer $m \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$. 

Then, we have$:$

\begin{itemize}
\item[$(1)$] There exist a $($not necessarily effective$)$ $\pi$-exceptional $\mathbb{Q}$-divisor $F$ 
such that 
$$
L \sim_{\mathbb{Q}} \pi^{*}B + F. 
$$

\item[$(2)$] If  the sheaf $\pi_{[*]}\mathcal{O}_{M}(L)$ is weakly positively curved on $X$, 
then $L+kE_{\pi}$ is pseudo-effective for $k\gg1$, 
where $E_{\pi}$ is the $\pi$-exceptional divisor. 


\item[$(3)$] We further assume that $L$ is weakly positively curved on $M \setminus E_{\pi}$ 
with respect to a K\"ahler form $\omega_{M}$ on $M$. 
Then, the line bundle $B$ is pseudo-effective on $X$. 



\end{itemize}


\end{lemm}
\begin{proof}
The natural sheaf morphisms
\[
\pi^* \pi_* \mathcal{O}_{M}(mL) \to \mathcal{O}_{M}(mL)  \quad \text{and} \quad
\pi^* \pi_* \mathcal{O}_{M}(mL) \to \pi^* \pi_{[*]} \mathcal{O}_{M}(mL) = \mathcal{O}_{M}(m\pi^{*}B) 
\]
are isomorphisms on $M \setminus E_{\pi}$.
Hence, the kernels and cokernels are supported in $E_{\pi}$.
Then, by Proposition \ref{det-tor}, 
the determinant sheaves of them are $\QQ$-linearly equivalent 
to some $\pi$-exceptional effective divisors.
Proposition \ref{det-exact} shows that 
the operation of taking determinants is additive under short exact sequences.
Thus, $L$ is $\QQ$-linearly equivalent to $\pi^{*}B + F$ for some $\pi$-exceptional divisor $F$ 
since $M$ is normal and strongly $\QQ$-factorial, 


We will prove that $B$ is independent of a sufficiently divisible $m$. 
Let $m$ be an arbitrary  number such that $\pi_{[*]} \mathcal{O}_{M}(mL)$ is an invertible sheaf.
By Lemma \ref{lem_codimen}, 
we can find effective divisors $E,F$ such that
\[
\pi^* \pi_* \mathcal{O}_{M}(kmL) \to  \mathcal{O}_{M}(k(mL-E)) \quad \text{and} \quad
\pi^* \pi_* \mathcal{O}_{M}(kmL) \to \mathcal{O}_{M}(k(m \pi^{*}B-F)).
\]
are isomorphic in codimension 1 for any $k \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$.  
The desired independence follows from taking the determinant sheaves of these natural morphisms.

(2)
The $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundle $B$ is weakly positively curved, and in particular, pseudo-effective by assumption.  
Choose $k \gg 1$ so that $kE_{\pi} + F$ is effective.  
Then, by (1), the line bundle $L + kE_{\pi}$ is pseudo-effective.



(3) Lemma~\ref{lem-push} shows that the direct image sheaf $\pi_{*} \mathcal{O}_{M}(mL)$ 
is weakly positively curved on $X$, and hence so is its reflexive hull $B$, 
since $\codim (X \setminus \pi(E_{\pi})) \geq 2$. 
This implies that $B$ is pseudo-effective since it is a line bundle.
\end{proof}


\begin{lemm}
\label{lem_codimen}
Let $X$ be a normal complex analytic variety, and let $\mathcal{I}$ be a coherent ideal sheaf on $X$.  
Then, there exist an effective Weil divisor $D$ on $X$ and morphism 
\[
\mathcal{I} \;\cong\; \mathcal{O}_X(-D).
\]
that is an isomorphism in codimension $1$.  
\end{lemm}
\begin{proof}
Let $\pi \colon \tilde{X} \to X$ be a bimeromorphic morphism such that
$\pi^{-1}\calI\cdot\OX_{\tilde X}=\mathcal{O}_{\tilde{X}}(-E)$
where $E$ is an SNC (simple normal crossing) divisor on $\tilde{X}$.
Without loss of generality,
we may assume that
$\pi$ is a biholomorphism outside a closed analytic subset of codimension $\geq 2$ in $X$.
Thus the composition of the morphism
$$\pi_* (\pi^{-1}\calI\cdot\OX_{\tilde X}) \to \mathcal{O}_X(-\pi_* E)  \subset \mathcal{O}_X$$
and the natural map $ \mathcal{I} \to \pi_* (\pi^{-1}\calI\cdot\OX_{\tilde X})$ is isomorphic in codimension $1$.
\end{proof}



\subsection{MRC fibrations and relatively big line bundles}\label{subsec-setup}

To prove Theorem~\ref{thm-faked-flat}, we begin by introducing the notation used throughout this section.


\begin{setup}\label{setting}
Let $(X,\Delta)$ be a klt pair of  a normal compact  K\"ahler space $X$  and 
an effective $\bb{Q}$-divisor $\Delta$  such that 
$X$ is strongly $\QQ$-factorial and the anti-log canonical $\bb{Q}$-line bundle $-(K_X+\Delta)$ is nef.
Fix an MRC fibration $\psi \colon X \dashrightarrow Y$ of $X$ onto a compact K\"ahler manifold $Y$, 
and a log resolution $\pi \colon M \to X$ of $(X, \Delta)$ 
with a fibration $\phi \colon M \to Y$ in the following diagram:
\begin{align}\label{comm-start}
\xymatrix{
M  \ar[rr]^{\pi} \ar[rd]_{\phi}&   & X \ar@{.>}[ld]^{\psi} \\
  & Y & 
}
\end{align}
Let $F_{+}$ and $F_{-}$ be the effective $\pi$-exceptional divisors having no common components 
such that
\begin{equation}
\label{eq_can}
K_M + \Delta_M \sim_{\QQ} \pi^*(K_X + \Delta) + F_{+}, 
\text{ where }\Delta_M := \pi^{-1}_* \Delta + F_{-}. 
\end{equation}
Note that $(M, \Delta_M)$ is a klt pair.  

Let $E := \Exc(\pi)$ be the (reduced effective) $\pi$-exceptional divisor on $M$.  
We fix $m \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ to be a sufficiently large and divisible integer.  
For later use, we carefully choose a relatively $\phi$-big line bundle $G$ on $M$ by the following lemma. Note that if $X$ is projective, we can simply take $G$ to be the pullback of a sufficiently ample line bundle on $X$; in the K\"ahler case, the construction of $G$ is much more subtle, especially the properties (1) and (2) below, which are crucial in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm_splitting}.








\begin{lemm}\label{lemm-good}
There exists a relatively $\phi$-big line bundle $G$ on $M$ satisfying the following properties$:$
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(1)$] There exist a line bundle $A$ on $X$ and  an effective $\pi$-exceptional divisor $F$ on $M$
such that $G:=\pi^{*} A + F$ and the restriction of $A$ to a general fibre of $\psi$ is ample (note that $\psi$ is almost holomorphic).  
\item[$(2)$] Let $S$ be an irreducible component of a fibre of $\phi$ $($with reduced structure$)$ such that 
$S$ is not contained in $E$. Then $G|_{S}$ is big.
\item[$(3)$] For a positive integer $c \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ and for a general point $y \in Y$, the natural map 
$$
\Sym^{m}H^{0}(M_{y}, \OX_{M_y}(G+c E)) \to H^{0}(M_{y}, \OX_{M_y}(m(G+c E)))
$$
is surjective, where $M_{y}:=\phi^{-1}(y)$ is the fiber of $\phi \colon M \to Y$ at $y$. 
\end{itemize}
\end{lemm}
\begin{proof}
Since $\psi \colon X \dashrightarrow Y$ is an almost holomorphic map, after possibly blowing up $M$ further, 
we may assume 
that $\pi \colon M \to X$ factors through a variety $\Gamma$, as in the following diagram:
\[
\xymatrix{
M  \ar[r]^{\alpha} \ar[rd]_{\phi}&  \Gamma \ar[d]^{\varphi} \ar[r]^{\beta} & X \ar@{.>}[ld]^{\psi} \\
  & Y. & 
}
\]
Here $\Gamma$ is a normal variety with strongly $\mathbb{Q}$-factorial klt singularities, and $\beta$ is an isomorphism over a general fiber of $\varphi$.  
The construction of such a $\Gamma$ proceeds as follows (see also the strategy of \cite[Lemma~2.12]{CH24}):  
first, let $\Gamma'$ be the normalization of the graph of $\psi$, with natural morphisms $\varphi' \colon \Gamma' \to Y$ and $\beta' \colon \Gamma' \to X$.  
Take a desingularization  $\alpha' \colon M' \to \Gamma'$ with simple normal crossing exceptional divisor $\Phi = \sum_i \Phi_i$.  
Then, for any sufficiently small $\varepsilon > 0$, we may apply the MMP for $K_{M'} + (1 - \varepsilon)\Phi$ over $\Gamma'$, as in \cite[Theorem~1.4]{DHP24} (cf.\ \cite[Theorem~1.7]{Fuj22}), to obtain a morphism $h \colon M' \dashrightarrow \Gamma$, where $(\Gamma, (1 - \varepsilon)h_*\Phi)$ is a strongly $\mathbb{Q}$-factorial klt pair, equipped with a (bimeromorphic) morphism $\mu \colon \Gamma \to \Gamma'$ such that $K_\Gamma + (1 - \varepsilon)h_*\Phi$ is $\mu$-nef.

Since $\psi$ is almost holomorphic, there exists a Zariski open subset $U_Y \subset Y$ such that $\beta'|_{(\varphi')^{-1}(U_Y)}$ is an isomorphism.  
In particular, $(\varphi')^{-1}(U_Y)$ has strongly $\mathbb{Q}$-factorial klt singularities.  
Reordering the components if necessary, we may assume that $\Phi_1,\ldots,\Phi_k$ are those components of $\Phi$ intersecting $(\varphi'\circ\alpha')^{-1}(U_Y)$.  
Then we can write
\[
K_{M'}|_{(\varphi'\circ\alpha')^{-1}(U_Y)} \sim_{\mathbb{Q}} (\alpha')^*K_{(\varphi')^{-1}(U_Y)} + \sum_{i=1}^k a_i \Phi_i
\]
with $a_i > -1$ for all $i$.  
It follows that 
\[
\Phi' := \sum_{i=1}^k (1 + a_i - \varepsilon) h_*\Phi_i \geq 0
\]
is $\mu$-exceptional and $\mu$-nef.  
By the Negativity Lemma (see \cite[Lemma~1.3]{JWang19} for the analytic case), 
we then conclude that $\Phi' = 0$.  
If $\varepsilon$ is chosen sufficiently small so that $\varepsilon < \min_i \{1 + a_i\}$, then every component of $\Phi = \mathrm{Exc}(\alpha')$ intersecting $(\varphi'\circ\alpha')^{-1}(U_Y)$ is contracted by $h$.  
Therefore, the restriction $\mu|_{(\varphi'\circ\mu)^{-1}(U_Y)}$ is a small contraction.  
Since $(\varphi')^{-1}(U_Y)$ is strongly $\mathbb{Q}$-factorial, we conclude by \cite[Lemma~2.4]{DH25} that $\mu|_{(\varphi'\circ\mu)^{-1}(U_Y)}$ is an isomorphism.

Set $\beta := \beta' \circ \mu$ and $\varphi := \varphi' \circ \mu$.  
Then $\beta|_{\varphi^{-1}(U_Y)}$ is an isomorphism.  
In particular, the exceptional locus $E_\beta := \mathrm{Exc}(\beta)$ does not dominate $Y$.



Since a general fiber of $\varphi \colon \Gamma \to Y$ is rationally connected, 
we can take a relatively $\varphi$-ample line bundle $G'$ by \cite[Corollary 4.2]{CH24}. 
By strong $\mathbb{Q}$-factoriality, after replacing $G'$ with $mG'$ for some $m \gg 1$ sufficiently divisible,  we may assume that 
\[
G' = \beta^{*} A + F'
\]
for some line bundle $A$ on $\Gamma$ and $\beta$-exceptional divisor $F'$ (see Lemma~\ref{lem-Qfact}). 
By construction of $\Gamma$, 
we see that $\beta|_{\varphi^{-1}(U_Y)}$ is an isomorphism for some Zariski open $U_Y$ of $Y$. 
Hence, the restriction of $A$ to a general fibre of $\psi$ is ample. 
Fix $d \gg 1$ so that $dE_{\beta} + F'$ is effective. 
We define $G$ and $F$ by
\[
G := \pi^{*} A + F := \pi^{*} A + \alpha^{*}(dE_{\beta} + F') 
= \alpha^{*}(G' + dE_{\beta}).
\]
The first property is clear by construction. Moreover, if $S$ is a component of $(M_y)_{\red}$ such that $S$ is not contained in $E$, then $\alpha|_S\colon S\to \alpha(S)\subseteq(\Gamma_y)_{\red}$ is bimeromorphic and $\alpha(S)$ is not contained in $E_\beta$; since $G'|_{\alpha(S)}$ is ample, then $G|_S=\alpha^\ast(G'|_{\alpha(S)}+dE_\beta|_{\alpha(S)})$ is big, 
and thus the second property is verified.    

Note that
\[
\Exc(\alpha) =: E_{\alpha} \leq 
E = E_{\alpha} + \alpha^{-1}_{*} E_{\beta}
\leq E_{\alpha} + \alpha^{*} E_{\beta}.
\]
The induced morphism $\alpha|_{M_y} \colon M_y \to \Gamma_y$ 
is still bimeromorphic, and $E_{\alpha}|_{M_y}$ is $\alpha|_{M_y}$-exceptional. 
Hence, using the inequality $E \leq E_{\alpha} + \alpha^{*} E_{\beta}$ and the projection formula, we obtain
\begin{align*}
H^{0}(M_y,\OX_{M_y}(m(G + cE))) 
&\subset H^{0}(M_y, \OX_{M_y}(mG + mc(E_{\alpha} + \alpha^{*} E_{\beta}))) \\
&\cong H^{0}\left(\Gamma_y, \alpha|_{M_y*} \left( \mathcal{O}_{M_y}(mG + mc(E_{\alpha} + \alpha^{*} E_{\beta})) \right) \right) \\
&\cong H^{0}\left(\Gamma_y, \mathcal{O}_{\Gamma_y}(m(G' + dE_{\beta})) \otimes 
(\alpha|_{M_y})_\ast \left( \mathcal{O}_{M_y}(mc(E_{\alpha} + \alpha^{*} E_{\beta})) \right) \right) \\
&\cong H^{0}(\Gamma_y, \OX_{\Gamma_y}(mG')).
\end{align*}
Here, to obtain the last isomorphism, we used the fact that 
$\alpha|_{M_y*}(\mathcal{O}_{M_y}(mcE_{\alpha})) = \mathcal{O}_{\Gamma_y}$ 
and $\Gamma_y \cap E_{\beta} = \emptyset$. 
By the same argument applied to the inequality $E_{\alpha} \leq E$, 
we conclude that
\begin{align}\label{eq-rank}
H^{0}(M_y, \OX_{M_y}(m(G + cE))) \cong H^{0}(\Gamma_y, \OX_{\Gamma_y}(mG'))
\end{align}
for a general point  $y \in Y$.

Since $G'|_{\Gamma_y}$ is ample, the section ring 
$\bigoplus_{k \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}} H^{0}(\Gamma_y, kG')$ is finitely generated. 
Thus, by replacing $G$ with $mG$ for sufficiently large $m$, we obtain the third property.
\end{proof}



For a fixed  integer $c \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ (which will be chosen large enough later), 
we consider the determinant sheaf 
$$\det \phi_* \mathcal{O}_{M}(G+ cE):= 
\Big (\bigwedge^{[r]} \big( \phi_* \mathcal{O}_{M}(G + cE) \big) \Big ), 
$$  
where $r:=\rank \phi_* \mathcal{O}_{M}(G + cE)$. 
Note that $r$ does not depend on $c$ by \eqref{eq-rank} and 
this determinant sheaf is an invertible sheaf on $Y$ since $Y$ is smooth. 
We define the $\phi$-big line bundle $L_{m}$ on $M$  by   
\begin{align}\label{eq-line}
L_{m}:=L_{c, m}:=m(G + cE) 
-\frac{m}{r}\phi^* \det \phi_* \mathcal{O}_{M}(G + cE) 
\end{align}
where $m$ is a positive integer with $m/r \in \mathbb{Z}$. 
Furthermore, we define the direct image sheaf  $\scrV_{m}$ on $Y$  by 
$$
\scrV_{m} :=\scrV_{c, m}:=\phi_* \mathcal{O}_{M}( L_{m} ).
$$
Let $Y_0 \subset Y$ be the maximal Zariski open set 
satisfying the following properties: 
\begin{itemize}
\item $\phi \colon M\to Y$ is a flat  morphism over $Y_0$. 
\item $\phi^{*}P$ is not $\pi$-exceptional for any prime divisor $P$ on $Y_0$. 
\end{itemize}
\end{setup}

This section is devoted to the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-faked-flat}.  
Throughout, we work under the assumptions and notation introduced in Setting~\ref{setting}.


\begin{thm}\label{thm-faked-flat}
In the situation of Setting~\ref{setting}, 
for a fixed large integer $c \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ and 
a sufficiently large and divisible integer $m \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$, 
the reflexive hull 
\[
\mathcal{E}_{m} := \pi_{[*]}(\phi^{*} \scrV_{m})
\]
is a weakly positively curved sheaf with $c_{1}(\mathcal{E}_{m}) = 0$. 
\end{thm}

\begin{cor}\label{thm-flat}
In the setting of Theorem~\ref{thm-faked-flat}, 
assume further that $\mathcal{E}_{m}$ is locally free on $X$ 
$($which is satisfied when $X$ is smooth by Theorem~\ref{thm-flatness}$)$.  
Then, the sheaf $\scrV_{m}$ is locally free on $Y_{0}$ and admits a flat connection on $Y_{0}$. 
\end{cor}


\begin{rem}\label{rem-proj}
(1)  
For the purpose of proving Theorem~\ref{thm-main}, it suffices to consider the case where $X$ is smooth.  
However, we formulate the results for strongly $\mathbb{Q}$-factorial klt pairs to accommodate future applications.

(2)  
Corollary~\ref{thm-flat} follows from exactly the same argument as in \cite[Proof of Theorem 3.2]{MW}.  
Hence, we will concentrate on the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-faked-flat} in this section.

(3)  
Theorem~\ref{thm-faked-flat} has already been proved in the projective case 
(see \cite{CH19, MW, Wang20}). The proof in the projective setting often relies on the existence of an ample line bundle 
$A_Y$ on $Y$ in order to apply extension theorems.  
Therefore, a generalization to the K\"ahler setting is not straightforward and involves several subtle issues.  
Our strategy is to \textit{localize} the projective argument using the theory of relative Bergman kernels.  
To facilitate comparison with existing proofs, we adopt the same notation as in \cite{Wang20}.
\end{rem}







\subsection{Bimeromorphic properties of MRC fibrations}\label{subsec-mrc}


In this subsection, we study bimeromorphic properties of the MRC fibration $\psi \colon X \dashrightarrow Y$, 
extending \cite[Proposition 3.3]{MW} to the setting of K\"ahler spaces.





\begin{prop}
\label{prop_bir-geometry-psi}
Let everything be as in Setting \ref{setting}. 
Then, we have:   
\begin{itemize}
\item[\rm(a)] $K_Y$ is $\QQ$-linearly equivalent to an effective divisor $N_{Y}$ whose pullback $\phi^{*}N_{Y}$ is $\pi$-exceptional; in particular, $\kappa(Y)=0$.
\item[\rm(b)]  $\Delta$ is horizontal with respect to $\psi$ 
$($i.e.,\,$Y=\phi( \pi^{-1}_{*} \Delta_{i})$ for any component $\Delta_{i}$ of $\Delta$$)$. 
\item[\rm(c)] $\pi(\phi\inv(Y\backslash Y_0))$ is of codimension $\geqslant 2$ in $X$. In particular, every $\phi$-exceptional divisor on $M$ is also  $\pi$-exceptional.
\item[\rm(d)]  $Y_0$ has the generalized Liouville property in the following sense$:$ 
For a flat vector bundle  $(\calH_0,\nabla_0)$ on $Y_0$  with the following condition $\rm(\bullet)$, 
every global section of $\calH_0$ is parallel with respect to $\nabla_0$. 
\begin{itemize}
\item[\rm($\bullet$)] 
There exists a numerically flat vector bundle $\calH$ on $X$ such that 
$$
\text{$(\phi^\ast\calH_0, \phi^\ast \nabla_0) \simeq (\pi^\ast\calH, \nabla)$ on $M_{0}:=\phi\inv(Y_0)$, 
}
$$
where $\phi^\ast \nabla_0$  is the connection on $\phi^\ast\calH_0$ 
defined by the pullback and $\nabla$ is the $($unique$)$ flat connection 
on $\pi^\ast\calH$ defined by \cite[Corollary 3.10 and the discussion thereafter]{Sim92} 
$($which is compatible with the filtration given by \cite[Theorem 1.18]{DPS94}$)$. 
\end{itemize}
\item[\rm(e)] $\psi$ is semistable in codimension $1$ 
$($i.e.,\,for a prime divisor $P$ on $Y_0$ and the irreducible decomposition $\phi^\ast P=\sum_{i}c_i P_i$ on $\phi\inv(Y_0)$, 
any component $P_i$ with  $c_i>1$ is $\pi$-exceptional$)$. 
\end{itemize}
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
We prove only Conclusion~(a), which is the core of the proposition. 
Conclusions~(b) and (e) can be easily verified 
by a slight modification of the following argument, together with the proof of \cite[Proposition 3.1]{Wang20}.
Conclusions~(c) and (d) are direct consequences of the other conclusions 
(see \cite[Proposition 3.1]{Wang20} for Conclusion~(c) and \cite[Proposition 3.3]{MW} for Conclusion~(d)). 

The proof of (a) proceeds in four steps:
\paragraph{\bf Step 1: we show that $F_{+} - \phi^{*}K_{Y}$ is pseudo-effective}  
To this end, we verify that the direct image sheaf
\[
\phi_{*}\left(\mathcal{O}_{M}\left(m(K_{M/Y}+\Delta_{M}) + G - m \pi^{*}(K_{X}+\Delta) \right)\right)
\]
satisfies the assumptions of Proposition~\ref{prop-psef}(1). 
By assumption and Lemma~\ref{lemm-good}, $-\pi^{*}(K_X+\Delta)$ is nef and $G$ is relatively $\phi$-big. 
The $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundle $\Delta_{M} (=: L)$, equipped with the natural metric $h_{\Delta_M} (=: h_L)$, 
satisfies $\mathcal{I}(h_{\Delta_M})|_{M_y} = \mathcal{O}_{M_y}$ by the klt condition. 
Moreover, by the projection formula, the above sheaf  is isomorphic to
\[
\phi_{*} \left( \mathcal{O}_{M}(m(F_{+} - \phi^{*}K_{Y}) + G) \right) 
= \phi_{*} \left( \mathcal{O}_{M}(mF_{+} + G) \right) \otimes \mathcal{O}_{Y}(-mK_{Y}),
\]
which is non-zero since $F_{+}$ is effective and $G$ is relatively $\phi$-big. 
Hence, Proposition~\ref{prop-psef} shows that $F_{+} - \phi^{*}K_{Y}$ is pseudo-effective. 


\paragraph{\bf Step 2: we prove that $\phi^{*}K_{Y}$ is numerically equivalent to a $\pi$-exceptional divisor $N$.} 
By Lemma~\ref{lem-Qfact}, we have
\[
\phi^{*}K_Y \sim_{\mathbb{Q}} \pi^{*}L + N
\]
for some $\bb{Q}$-line bundle $L$ on $X$ and $\pi$-exceptional $\mathbb{Q}$-divisor $N$. 
The class 
\[
\pi_{*} c_1(F_{+} - \phi^{*}K_Y) = - c_1(\pi_{*} \phi^{*}K_Y) = -c_1(L) \in H^{1,1}_{BC}(X, \mathbb{R})
\]
is represented by a semi-positive $(1,1)$-current, 
since it contains the pushforward of a semi-positive current in the class $c_{1}(F_{+} - \phi^{*}K_Y)$ 
(see Proposition \ref{BEG}). 
On the other hand, the base $Y$ of an MRC fibration is non-uniruled by \cite{GHS03}.  
Indeed, if there exists a covering rational curve $ R \subseteq Y $, then although $ M $ is not projective, 
the fiber product $ M \times_Y R $ is projective.  
Therefore, we can apply \cite[Theorem~1.1]{GHS03} to the induced fibration 
\[
\phi \colon M \times_{Y} R \to R
\]
to get a contradiction.  
It then follows from \cite{Ou25} that $K_Y$ is pseudo-effective. 
Consequently, we obtain
$
c_1(L) = c_1(\pi_{*} \phi^{*}K_Y) = 0.
$

\paragraph{\bf Step 3: we show that $N = \phi^{*}N_Y$ for some effective divisor $N_Y$ on $Y$.} 
Let $T$ be a semi-positive current representing $K_Y$. 
Then $\phi^{*}T$ represents $c_1(\phi^{*}K_{Y})=c_1(N)$, and thus, 
the pushforward $\pi_{*}\phi^{*}T$ represents the zero class. 
This implies that the support of $\phi^{*}T$ is contained in $E$. 
By the support theorem for currents \cite[III.2.14, pp.~143]{agbook}, there exists an  effective $\pi$-exceptional  divisor $F$ such that
\[
[F] = \phi^{*}T \in c_1(\phi^{*}K_Y) = c_1(N).
\]
Since $N$ is $\pi$-exceptional, we see $F = N$, and in particular, $N$ is effective.
Let $y \in Y$ be a general point not contained in the polar set of the weight of $T$. 
Then, we have
\[
[N \cap M_y] = \phi^{*}T|_{M_y} = 0,
\]
which shows that $N$ is a $\phi$-vertical divisor. 
Applying the support theorem \cite[III.2.14, pp.~143]{agbook} to $T$ again, 
we obtain an effective divisor $N_Y$ on $Y$ such that $T = [N_Y]$. 
Hence, we conclude that $N = \phi^{*}N_Y$ by $[N] = \phi^{*}T$.

\paragraph{\bf Step 4: we conclude by show that $K_Y\sim_{\QQ} N_Y$}
By {\bf Step 2} and {\bf Step 3} we see that $\phi^\ast K_Y\sim_{\QQ}\phi^\ast N_Y+\pi^\ast L$, with $N_Y\ge 0$ and $c_1(L)=0$. Hence $L_0:=N_Y-K_Y\in\Pic^0(Y)$ (note that $\Pic^0(Y)$ is divisible). Since $N=\phi^\ast N_Y$ is $\pi$-exceptional, for any $m\in\ZZ_{>0}$ sufficiently divisible, we have
\[
h^0(Y,mN_Y)=h^0(M,m\phi^\ast N_Y)=1,
\]
which implies that $\kappa(K_Y+L_0)=\kappa(N_Y)=0$. 
Then by the Campana-Koziarz-P{\u a}un type result \cite[Corollary 5.9(b)]{JWang19}, 
we see that $L_0\sim_\QQ 0$ and hence $K_Y\sim_\QQ N_Y$. In particular, we have $\kappa(Y)=0$.  
\end{proof}

As in \cite{Zhang05}, Proposition \ref{prop_bir-geometry-psi} immediately implies that the Albanese map is a fibration, partially generalizing \cite[Corollary 2]{Zhang05} to the K\"ahler setting.

\begin{cor}
Let $(X,\Delta)$ be a klt pair such that $-(K_{X}+\Delta)$ is nef, 
where $X$ is a normal K\"ahler space and $\Delta$ is an effective $\mathbb{Q}$-divisor on $X$.  
Then, the Albanese map
\[
\alb_X : X \dashrightarrow \Alb_X
\]
is an everywhere defined proper surjective morphism with connected fibres $($i.e.\,a fibration$)$.
\end{cor}

\begin{proof}
Since $X$ has rational singularities by \cite[Theorem 5.22, pp.\,161--162]{KM98}, its Albanese map $\alb_X$ is a morphism (\cite[Lemma 8.1]{Kaw85}).  
Replacing $X$ with a $\QQ$-factorialization (\cite[Lemma 2.11]{CH24}), we may assume that $X$ is strongly $\QQ$-factorial, 
so the hypotheses of Proposition \ref{prop_bir-geometry-psi} are satisfied.  
The proposition then yields a desingularisation $\pi\colon M\to X$ and a fibration $\phi\colon M\to Y$ 
onto a compact K\"ahler manifold $Y$ with $\kappa(Y)=0$ such that $\phi$ is a model of the MRC fibration of $X$.

By \cite[Theorem C]{JWang19}, the Albanese map $\alb_Y\colon Y\to\Alb_Y$ is a fibration, then so is $\alb_Y\circ\,\phi\colon M\to\Alb_Y$\,.  By definition, the composition $\alb_X\circ\pi\colon M\to\Alb_X$ is the Albanese map of $M$ ($\Alb_X=\Alb_M$), thus yields a morphism  
$f \colon \Alb_X\to\Alb_Y$ of complex tori by the universal property of Albanese maps, such that $\alb_Y\circ\,\phi=f\circ\alb_X\circ\,\pi$; in particular this implies that $f$ is surjective. From the property of MRC fibrations, we see that $H^1(M,\OX_M)\simeq H^1(Y,\OX_Y)$, hence $\Alb_X\simeq\Alb_Y$, and thus $f$ is an isogeny (see \cite[\S 2.3-2.4, pp.7-8]{Deb99}); but $f$ is a fibration, it is a fortiori an isomorphism. 
Therefore $\alb_X$ is a fibration.
\end{proof}









\subsection{Positivity and numerical flatness of direct image sheaves}\label{subsec-direct}

Define the $\pi$-exceptional divisor 
\begin{align}\label{eq-e'}
E' := F_{+} - \phi^{*} N_Y
\end{align}
on $M$ so that 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq_rel-canonical-pi-exc}
-(K_{M/Y} + \Delta_M) + E' \sim_{\bb{Q}} -\pi^{*}(K_X + \Delta). 
\end{equation}
Define the $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundle $D_{c,m}$ on $Y$ by
\begin{align*}
D_{c,m} := \frac{1}{r_m} \cdot \det \phi_{*} \mathcal{O}_M(m(G + cE)),
\end{align*}
where $r_m := \rank \phi_{*} \mathcal{O}_M(m(G + cE))$.  
Then we have:

\begin{prop}% Add proper citation when confirmed
\label{prop_more-psef-det}
Let everything be as above.  
Then, the reflexive pushforward
\[
\pi_{[*]} \left( \mathcal{O}_M\left( r_m (mG - \phi^{*} D_{c,m}) \right) \right)
\]
is weakly positively curved.  
In particular, the line bundle 
\[
r_m (mG - \phi^{*} D_{c,m}) + k_{m}E
\]
is pseudo-effective on $M$ for a sufficiently large $k_{m} \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ 
$($see Lemma \ref{lem-Qfact}$)$.
\end{prop}




\begin{proof}

For simplicity, we consider only the case $ m = 1 $ and set $ r := r_1 $.  
This proposition corresponds to \cite[Proposition~3.3]{Wang20}, whose proof is divided into Steps~(A)--(C).  
Step~(C) relies on the existence of an ample line bundle on $ Y $.  
By localizing the argument, we give an alternative proof that uses only the positivity of direct image sheaves.  
Steps~(A) and (B) remain unchanged, but we recall them here to fix notation.




\paragraph{\bf (A) Construction of the fiber product and the canonical section.}

Consider the fiber product
\[
M^r := \underbrace{M \times_Y M \times_Y \cdots \times_Y M}_{r\ \text{times}},
\]
equipped with the natural projections $\pr_i \colon M^r \to M$, 
and let $\mu : M^{(r)} \to M^r$ be a strong desingularization 
such that $\mu$ is an isomorphism over $M^r_{\reg}$.  
We define $p_i := \pr_i \circ \mu$ and $\phi^{(r)} := \phi^r \circ \mu$:  
\[
\xymatrix@C=5em@R=4em{
M^{(r)} \ar[r]^{\mu} \ar[dr]_{p_i} \ar@/_4pc/[rrd]_{\phi^{(r)}} & 
M^r \ar[r]^{\pr_i} \ar[d]_{\pr_j} \ar[dr]^{\phi^r} & 
M \ar[d]^{\phi} \\
& M \ar[r]_{\phi} & Y. 
}
\]
Further, we define:
\begin{align*}
G^r &:= \sum_{i=1}^r \pr_i^* G, &\quad G^{(r)} &:= \mu^* G^r, \\
E^r &:= \sum_{i=1}^r \pr_i^* E, &\quad E^{(r)} &:= \mu^* E^r, \\
\Delta_{M^r} &:= \sum_{i=1}^r \pr_i^* \Delta_M, &\quad \Delta_{M^{(r)}} &:= \mu^* \Delta_{M^r}.
\end{align*}
Let $Y_{\mathrm{ff}} \subset Y$ be the maximal Zariski open subset 
over which $\phi$ is flat and $\phi_*\mathcal{O}_M(G + cE)$ is locally free.  
Then $\codim_Y(Y \setminus Y_{\mathrm{ff}}) \geq 2$, and the fiber $M_y$ is Gorenstein for every $y \in Y_{\mathrm{ff}}$  
(cf.\ \cite[\S23, Theorem 23.4, pp.~181]{Mat89}).  
Over $Y_{\mathrm{ff}}$, we have the natural inclusion:
\begin{equation}\label{eq_incl-det-tensor}
\det \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE)|_{Y_{\mathrm{ff}}} 
\simeq \mathcal{O}_{Y_{\mathrm{ff}}}(r D_{c,1}|_{Y_{\mathrm{ff}}}) 
\hookrightarrow \bigotimes^r \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE)|_{Y_{\mathrm{ff}}}.
\end{equation}
By the projection formula and induction, we obtain:
\[
\phi^{(r)}_* \mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}}(G^{(r)} + cE^{(r)})|_{Y_{\mathrm{ff}}}
\simeq \phi^r_* \mathcal{O}_{M^r}(G^r + cE^r)|_{Y_{\mathrm{ff}}}
\simeq \bigotimes^r \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE)|_{Y_{\mathrm{ff}}}.
\]
The inclusion \eqref{eq_incl-det-tensor} thus induces a non-zero section
\[
s_0 \in H^0\left(Y_{\mathrm{ff}},\, \phi^{(r)}_* \mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}}(G^{(r)} + cE^{(r)}) \otimes \left(\det \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE)\right)^{-1} \right).
\]
By \cite[\S III.5, Lemma 5.10, pp.~107--108]{Nak04} 
(see also \cite[Theorem 1.14]{JWang19}),  
there exists an effective divisor $B_1$ supported on $M^{(r)} \setminus (\phi^{(r)})^{-1}(Y_{\mathrm{ff}})$ such that $s_0$ extends to a non-zero section in $ H^{0}(M^{(r)}, L_0)$, where
\[
L_0 := G^{(r)} + cE^{(r)}  - r(\phi^{(r)})^* D_{c,1}+B_1\,.
\]

\paragraph{\bf (B) Comparison of the relative canonical divisors.}
By induction and the base change formula for relative canonical bundles \cite[Proposition~(9)]{Kle80}, 
$M^r_{\mathrm{ff}}$ is Gorenstein and its relative dualizing sheaf satisfies
\[
\omega_{M^r_{\mathrm{ff}} / Y} \simeq \mathcal{O}_{M^r_{\mathrm{ff}}}\left( \sum_{i=1}^r \pr_i^* K_{M/Y} \right).
\]
The natural morphism 
\[
\omega_{M^r_{\mathrm{ff}} / Y} \to \mu_* \mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}}(K_{M^{(r)}/Y})|_{M^r_{\mathrm{ff}}}
\]
(see \cite[\S II.8, Proposition 8.3, pp.~176]{Har77}) is an isomorphism over the locus where $M$ has rational singularities.  
Assume that the branch locus $\Sigma$ of $\phi \colon M \to Y$ is an SNC divisor on $Y$, 
and that $f^* \Sigma$ has SNC support. Write
\[
f^*\Sigma := \sum_\lambda W_\lambda + \sum_\mu a_\mu V_\mu,
\]
with $a_\mu > 1$ for all $\mu$, and define
\[
W := \sum_\lambda W_\lambda, \qquad V := \sum_\mu V_\mu.
\]
Then, by \cite[Lemma 3.13]{Hor10}, $M^r_{\mathrm{ff}}$ has rational singularities along
\[
\underbrace{(M_{\mathrm{ff}} \setminus (V \cup \phi^{-1} \Sigma_{\sing})) \times_{Y_{\mathrm{ff}} \setminus\Sigma_{\sing}} \cdots \times_{Y_{\mathrm{ff}} \setminus \Sigma_{\sing}} (M_{\mathrm{ff}} \setminus (V \cup \phi^{-1}\Sigma_{\sing}))}_{r\text{ times}}.
\]
Hence, there exists a divisor $B_2$ on $M^{(r)}$ supported on
\[
E^{(r)} \cup \left( M^{(r)} \setminus \mu^{-1}(M^r_{\mathrm{ff}}) \right) \cup \mathrm{Supp}\left( \sum_{i=1}^r \pr_i^* V \right)
\]
such that
\[
- (K_{M^{(r)}/Y} + \Delta_{M^{(r)}}) + B_2 
\sim \sum_{i=1}^r \pr_i^*\left( - (K_{M/Y} + \Delta_M) + E' \right).
\]
Note that the right-hand side is nef by \eqref{eq_rel-canonical-pi-exc}.

\paragraph{\bf (C) Positivity of direct images.}

The line bundle $L_0$ is relatively $\phi^{(r)}$-big and (pseudo-)effective, 
and the natural metric $h_{\Delta_{M^{(r)}}}$  on $\Delta_{M^{(r)}}$
satisfies $\mathcal{I}(h_{\Delta_{M^{(r)}}})|_{M^{(r)}_y} = \mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}_y}$ 
for a general fiber $M^{(r)}_y$. 
Hence, by applying Proposition~\ref{prop-psef}~(2), we deduce that the direct image sheaf
\begin{align*}
&\phi^{(r)}_{*} \mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}} \Big(  m K_{M^{(r)}/Y} + L_0 
+ \big( -m(K_{M^{(r)}/Y} + \Delta_{M^{(r)}}) + m B_2 \big) 
+ m \Delta_{M^{(r)}} \Big)\\ 
=& \phi^{(r)}_{*} \mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}}(L_0 + m B_2)
\end{align*}
is weakly positively curved on $Y$.


Let $C \subset Y_0$ be a Zariski closed subset of codimension $\geq 2$ such that $\phi^{(r)} \colon M^{(r)} \to Y$ is flat over $Y_0 \setminus C$. 
Consider the generically surjective morphism over $Y_0\setminus C$\,:
\[
\phi^{(r)*} \phi^{(r)}_{*} \mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}}(L_0 + m B_2) 
\to \mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}}(L_0 + m B_2).
\]
By Lemma~\ref{lem-pull}, the left-hand side is weakly positively curved over $Y_0 \setminus C$ (and hence over $Y_0$) 
with respect to a K\"ahler form on $M^{(r)}$.
Let $g_\varepsilon$ be the singular Hermitian metrics on $\mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}}(L_0 + m B_2)$ induced 
by the metrics on the left-hand side via this generic surjection. 
By construction, the polar set of $g_\varepsilon$ on $\mathcal{O}_{M^{(r)}}(L_0 + m B_2)$ is not dominant over $Y_0$. 
In particular, the restriction of $g_\varepsilon$ to the strict transform $\bar{D}$ 
of the diagonal $D \subset M^r$ is well-defined. 
This implies that $\mathcal{O}_{\bar{D}}(L_0 + m B_2)$ is weakly positively curved over $Y_0$.

The diagonal $D \subset M^r$ is isomorphic to $M$. 
Under this identification, we have
\[
(L_0 + m B_2)|_{\bar{D}} \cong r(G + cE - D_{c,1}) + B_1|_{\bar{D}} + m B_2|_{\bar{D}}.
\]
Note that the induced morphism $\gamma \colon \bar{D} \to D \cong M \to X$ is bimeromorphic. 
By Lemma~\ref{lem-push}, we can deduce that 
\[
\gamma_{[*]} \mathcal{O}_{\bar{D}}(L_0 + m B_2)
\cong \pi_{[*]} \mathcal{O}_M\big( r(G + cE - D_{c,1}) + B_1 + m B_2 \big)
\cong \mathcal{O}_X(r (G - D_{c,1}))
\]
is weakly positively curved on $X$. 
The isomorphism on the right-hand side follows from the fact that $B_1$, $B_2$, and $E$ are $\pi$-exceptional.
\end{proof}


The proof of the following proposition also relies on the projectivity of $Y$ 
in the corresponding proofs of \cite[Lemma~3.5]{CH19} and \cite[Proposition~3.6]{CCM19}.  
We therefore carefully generalize the argument to the K\"aher setting.


\cite[Lemma 3.5]{CH19}, \cite[Proposition 3.6]{CCM19}
\begin{prop}[cf.~{\cite[Lemma 3.5]{CH19}, \cite[Proposition 3.6]{CCM19}}]
\label{prop_det-stablize}
Let everything be as above. 
Then, there exists an integer $c_0 \in \ZZ_{>0}$ such that, for every $c \geqslant c_0$, 
the natural inclusion
\[
\det \phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE) \to \det \phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + (c+1)E)
\]
is an isomorphism over $Y_0$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Recall that 
\[
r := \rank \phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE) = \dimcoh^0(M_y, \mathcal{O}_{M_y}(G + cE))
\]
is independent of $c$ by \eqref{eq-rank}. 
Assume, for contradiction, that the inclusion
\[
\det \phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE) \to \det \phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + (c+1)E)
\]
is not an isomorphism over $Y_0$ for infinitely many  $c$. 
Then, there exist an increasing sequence $(c_k)_{k \in \ZZ_{>0}}$ and  
effective divisors $B_k$ on $Y$ such that $B_k \cap Y_0 \neq \emptyset$, and the line bundle
\[
r D_{c_{k+1}, 1} - (r D_{c_k, 1} + B_k)
\]
admits a non-zero section for every $k$.
By Proposition~\ref{prop_more-psef-det}, we obtain the inequality
\[
c_1(rG + k_1 E) 
\geq c_1(\phi^{*} r D_{c_N, 1}) 
\geq c_1(\phi^{*} r D_{c_0, 1}) + \sum_{k=1}^N c_1(\phi^{*} B_k).
\]
Here we write $\alpha\ge\beta$ for cohomology classes $\alpha$ and $\beta$ 
if the class $\alpha-\beta$ is pseudo-effective. On the other hand, by Lemma~\ref{lem-Qfact}, we can write
\begin{align}\label{eq-dec}
\phi^{*} D_{c,m} \sim_{\mathbb{Q}} \pi^{*} \Lambda_{c,m} + F_{c,m}, \quad
\phi^{*} B_k \sim_{\QQ} \pi^{*} \Lambda_k + F_k. 
\end{align}
Note that $\Lambda_k$ is an effective Weil $\ZZ$-divisor on $X$ since $\Lambda_k=\pi_\ast\phi^{*} B_k$. 
Since $G = \pi^{*} A + F$ by Lemma~\ref{lemm-good}(1), by pushing forward $\pi_\ast$, then, we obtain 
\[
c_1(rA) \geq c_1(r\Lambda_{c,m}) + \sum_{k=1}^N c_1(\Lambda_k) \geq c_1(r\Lambda_{c,m})
\]
for every $N \in \ZZ_{>0}$.
This implies that the integration currents $[\Lambda_k]$ converge weakly to zero. 
Since each $\Lambda_k$ is an effective Weil $\ZZ$-divisor on $X$, this forces $\Lambda_k = 0$ for $k \gg 1$. 
Therefore, we conclude that $\phi^{*} B_k \sim_{\mathbb{Q}} F_k$ is $\pi$-exceptional for large $k$, 
which contradicts the definition of $Y_0$ since $B_k \cap Y_0 \neq \emptyset$. 
\end{proof}







As an immediate corollary of {\hyperref[prop_det-stablize]{Proposition \ref*{prop_det-stablize}}}, we obtain the following:

\begin{cor}
\label{cor_iso-direct-image-Y0}
Let everything be as above. 
Let $c_0$ be the integer given by {\hyperref[prop_det-stablize]{Proposition \ref*{prop_det-stablize}}}, and let $c \geqslant c_0$. 
Then, we have$:$

\begin{itemize}
\item[\rm(a)] 
The sheaf $\phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE)$ is isomorphic to $\phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + kE + pE')$ over $Y_0$ for any $k \geqslant c$ and any $p \in \ZZ_{\geqslant 0}$ such that $pE'$ is integral$;$

\item[\rm(b)] 
The sheaf $\phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE)$ is $\frac{1}{m} D_{c,m}$-weakly positively curved over $Y_0$ for every $m\in\ZZ_{>0}$\,.
\end{itemize}
\end{cor}

\begin{proof}
The proof is completely the same as in that of \cite[Corollary 3.1]{Wang20}, and is therefore omitted.
\end{proof}




We complete the proof of Theorem \ref{thm-faked-flat} 
by the following two propositions: 

\begin{prop}
\label{prop_positivity-Um-Vm}
 For every $m$ divisible by $r$, we consider
\begin{align*}
\scrU_{c,m} &:=\Sym^m\!\phi_\ast\mathcal{O}_M(G+cE)\otimes\mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1}), \\
\scrV_{c,m} &:=\phi_\ast\mathcal{O}_M(m(G+cE))\otimes\mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1})=\phi_\ast\mathcal{O}_M(L_{m}). 
\end{align*} 
Then, both $\scrU_{c,m}$ and $\scrV_{c,m}$ are weakly positively curved on $Y_0$.
In particular, 
the sheaf $\mathcal{E}_{m} = \pi_{[*]}(\phi^{*} \scrV_{m})$ is weakly positively curved on $X$ 
$($see Lemma \ref{lem-pull}$)$. 
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
By {\hyperref[cor_iso-direct-image-Y0]{Corollary \ref*{cor_iso-direct-image-Y0}(b)}}, 
we see that $\phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE)$ is $D_{c,1}$-weakly positively curved on $Y_0$, 
which implies that  $\scrU_{c,m}$ is also weakly positively curved on $Y_0$.
By Lemma~\ref{lemm-good}~(3), 
the natural morphism $\scrU_{c,m} \to \scrV_{c,m}$ is generically surjective. 
Hence, $\scrV_{c,m}$ is also weakly positively curved on $Y_0$.
\end{proof}



Let $C \subset Y$ be a Zariski closed subset of codimension $\geq 2$ such that 
$\scrV_{c,m}$ is a locally free sheaf on $Y \setminus C$. 
Then, we have 
\[
\det \phi^{*}\scrV_{c,m} = \phi^{*} \det\scrV_{c,m} = \phi^{*} \mathcal{O}_{Y}( r_{m} (D_{c,m} - m D_{c,1}) )
\]
on $M \setminus \phi^{-1}(C)$. 
Note that $\codim \phi^{-1}(C) \geq 2$ over $Y_0$ 
since $\phi \colon M \to Y$ is flat over $Y_0$ and 
$\codim \pi(\phi^{-1}(Y \setminus Y_0)) \geq 2$ by Proposition~\ref{prop_bir-geometry-psi}~(c). 
Therefore, we obtain 
\[
\det \pi_{[*]} \phi^{*}\scrV_{c,m} 
= \pi_{[*]} \phi^{*} \mathcal{O}_{Y}( r_{m} (D_{c,m} - m D_{c,1}) )
\sim_{\bb{Q}}\mathcal{O}_{X}( r_{m} (\Lambda_{c,m} - m\Lambda_{c,1})),
\]
where $\Lambda_{c,m}$ is defined as in \eqref{eq-dec}. 
Hence, to complete the proof of Theorem \ref{thm-faked-flat}, it suffices to prove the following proposition.
\begin{prop}[{\cite[Proposition 3.6]{CH19}}] 
\label{prop_det-direct-image-power}
For any $m \in \ZZ_{>0}$ divisible by $r := r_1$, 
we have 
\[
\Lambda_{c,m} \equiv m\Lambda_{c,1}.
\]
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
Since $\mathcal{E}_{m} = \pi_{[*]}(\phi^{*} \scrV_{m})$ is weakly positively curved by Lemma~\ref{lem-pull}, 
so is 
\[
\det \pi_{[*]}(\phi^{*} \scrV_{m})=
\pi_{[*]} \phi^{*} \mathcal{O}_Y(r_m (D_{c,m} - m  D_{c,1}))
\sim_{\bb{Q}}r_{m}(\Lambda_{c,m} - m\Lambda_{c,1}). 
\]
On the other hand, since $\phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE)$ is $\frac{1}{m} D_{c,m}$-weakly positively curved over $Y_0$ 
(see Corollary~\ref{cor_iso-direct-image-Y0}~(b)), 
the sheaf 
\[
\det \phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G + cE) - \frac{r}{m} D_{c,m}
\]
is weakly positively curved on $Y_0$. 
Thus,  by Lemma~\ref{lem-pull} again, we conclude that 
\[
\pi_{[*]} \phi^{*} \mathcal{O}_Y( (m  D_{c,1}-  D_{c,m})) 
\sim_{\bb{Q}}( m\Lambda_{c,1}- \Lambda_{c,m})
\]
is weakly positively curved on $X$. 
\end{proof}



\section{Proof of the main structure theorem}
\label{sec-mainthm}

Let everything be as in Setting \ref{setting}. 
We give the proof of Theorem \ref{thm-main} under the assumption that $X$ is smooth. 
As in \cite{CH19, Wang20, MW}, the main idea is to show that an MRC fibration induces a splitting of the tangent bundle, and then apply Ehresmann's theorem to conclude.


\subsection{Splitting of tangent sheaves}\label{subsec-splitting}

This subsection is devoted to the proof of the following splitting theorem:


\begin{thm}
\label{thm_splitting}
Let $(X,\Delta)$ be a klt pair of  a normal compact  K\"ahler space $X$  and 
an effective $\bb{Q}$-divisor $\Delta$  such that 
the anti-log canonical $\bb{Q}$-line bundle $-(K_X+\Delta)$ is nef.
Assume that the reflexive sheaf $\mathcal{E}_m$ defined in Theorem \ref{thm-faked-flat} 
is locally free on $X$ $($this condition is satisfied if $X$ is smooth$)$. 
Then, the MRC fibration of $X$ induces a splitting of the tangent sheaf $T_X$ of $X$ into foliations: 
\[
T_X\simeq\calF\oplus\calG 
\]
such that 
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\bullet$] $\calF$ is algebraically integrable and its general leaf is a fibre of the MRC fibrations of $X$$;$ 
\item[$\bullet$] The canonical sheaf of $\calG$ satisfies $K_{\calG}\sim_{\QQ}0$. 
\end{itemize}
\end{thm}


\begin{proof}
This proof essentially follows the arguments in \cite[\S 3.C, Proof of Theorem~1.2]{CH19}, 
\cite[Step~3 and Step~4 of the proof of Theorem~54.1]{Wang20}, and 
\cite[Proof of Lemma~4.8]{MW}. 
For the reader's convenience, we recall the proof here with some simplifications and clarifications. 
The main idea is to find a bimeromorphic model $X_G$ of $X$ which admits a locally constant fibration over $Y_0$. 
This induces a splitting of the tangent bundle of $X_G$ over $Y_0$. 
If the bimeromorphic model $X_G$ is carefully chosen, this splitting descends to a splitting of $T_X$. 
We divide the proof into four steps:


\smallskip

\paragraph{\bf Step 1: Construction of the bimeromorphic model $X_G$.}

Let everything be as in Setting \ref{setting}. 
By \cite[Theorem~1.2]{CH24}, the morphism $\phi \colon M \to Y$ is projective. 
Hence, we can take a $\phi$-big line bundle $G$ as in Lemma~\ref{lemm-good}, and let $c \geqslant c_0$ be a positive integer as in Proposition~\ref{prop_det-stablize}. 
After possibly blowing up $M$, we may assume that the $\phi$-relative base locus of $G + cE$ is a divisor. 
Then $G + cE$ can be written as:
\[
G + cE = G_{c,\free} + G_{c,\base},
\]
where $G_{c,\base}$ is the $\phi$-relative fixed part of the $\phi$-relative linear system defined by $G$, and 
$G_{c,\free} := (G + cE) - G_{c,\base}$ is the $\phi$-relatively movable part, which is $\phi$-relatively generated by our assumption. The relative linear system $G_{c,\free}$ induces a morphism over $Y$:
\[
\pi_G \colon M \to \PP(\phi_\ast \OX_M(G_{c,\free})) \quad \text{such that} \quad \OX_M(G_{c,\free}) = \pi_G^\ast \OX_{\PP(\phi_\ast \OX_M(G_{c,\free}))}(1).
\]
After replacing $G$ and $c$ with sufficiently large divisible multiples, we may assume that $\pi_G$ coincides with the $\phi$-relative Iitaka fibration associated to $G + cE$. 
In particular, $\pi_G$ is a bimeromorphic morphism, and its image, denoted by $X_G$, is normal. 
Let $\psi_G \colon X_G \to Y$ be the induced morphism. Then, we obtain the following commutative diagram:


\[
\xymatrix@C=3.2em@R=2.5em{
X \ar@{-->}[dr]_{\psi}
& 
M \ar[l]^{\pi}
  \ar[d]_{\phi}
  \ar[r]^{\pi_G}
& 
X_G \ar@{^{(}->}[r]
    \ar[dl]_{\psi_G}
& 
{\PP\bigl(\phi_\ast\OX_M(G_{c,\free})\bigr)} \ar[dll]^{p}
\\
& Y. & & \\
& & &
}
\]

\bigskip


\paragraph{\bf Step 2: Proof that $X_G$ is a locally constant fibration over $Y_0$.}

By our assumption, the sheaf $\mathcal{E}_m$ is locally free, thus a numerically flat vector bundle by Theorems~\ref{thm-faked-flat} and~\ref{thm-flatness}. 
Hence, by Theorem~\ref{thm-flat}, the sheaf $\mathscr{V}_m$ is a flat vector bundle over $Y_0$ for every $m$.
Moreover, by our construction (see Remark \ref{rem-proj}(2) and \cite[Proof of Theorem 3.2]{MW}), the flat connection on $\mathcal{E}_m$ descends to one on $\mathscr{V}_m$. 
Therefore, the sheaf $\mathscr{V}_m$ satisfies condition~($\bullet$) in Proposition~\ref{prop_bir-geometry-psi}(d). 
We set
\[
\mathscr{W}_m := \psi_{G\ast} \mathcal{O}_{X_G}(m) \otimes \mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1}),
\]
where $\mathcal{O}_{X_G}(1) := \mathcal{O}_{\PP(\phi_\ast \mathcal{O}_M(G_{c,\free}))}(1)\big|_{X_G}$. 
We claim that $\mathscr{W}_m \simeq \mathscr{V}_m$ over $Y_0$.
Indeed, this isomorphism follows from the argument in \cite[\S3.C, Proof of Theorem~1.2, Step~1]{CH19} 
(see also \cite[Lemma~5.3]{Wang20}), as we now explain in more detail:



\begin{proof}[Proof of the claim]
By Lemma~\ref{lemm-good}(3), we have a generically surjective morphism 
$\mathscr{U}_m \to \mathscr{V}_m$, which corresponds to a global section 
$s \in H^0(Y, \mathscr{U}_m^* \otimes \mathscr{V}_m)$. 
Since both $\mathscr{U}_m|_{Y_0}$ and $\mathscr{V}_m|_{Y_0}$ are flat vector bundles satisfying 
condition~($\bullet$) in Proposition~\ref{prop_bir-geometry-psi}(d), 
the same holds for $(\mathscr{U}_m^* \otimes \mathscr{V}_m)|_{Y_0}$. 
Therefore, by Proposition~\ref{prop_bir-geometry-psi}(d), the section $s|_{Y_0}$ is parallel. 
In particular, the morphism $\mathscr{U}_m \to \mathscr{V}_m$ has constant rank over $Y_0$, 
and hence is surjective over $Y_0$.

Now consider the inclusion 
\[
\phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(mG_{c,\free}) \hookrightarrow \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(mG).
\]
By tensoring with $\mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1})$, we obtain the following commutative diagram:
\[
\xymatrix@C=2.5cm@R=1.2cm{
  \Sym^{m}\!\phi_*\mathcal{O}_M(G_{c,\free}) \otimes \mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1})
      \ar[r]^-{\simeq} \ar[d] &
  \mathscr{U}_m \ar[d] \\
  \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(mG_{c,\free}) \otimes \mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1})
      \ar@{^{(}->}[r] &
  \mathscr{V}_m. 
}
\]
As shown above, the right vertical arrow is surjective over $Y_0$. 
Then, by the Snake Lemma, the left vertical arrow is also surjective over $Y_0$. 
Applying the Snake Lemma once more, we deduce that the bottom horizontal arrow is an isomorphism over $Y_0$.

Now consider the short exact sequence
\[
0 \to \mathcal{I}_{X_G} \to \mathcal{O}_{\PP(\phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(G_{c,\free}))} \to \mathcal{O}_{X_G} \to 0,
\]
where $\mathcal{I}_{X_G}$ is the ideal sheaf of $X_G$ in $\PP(\phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(G_{c,\free}))$. 
By relative Serre vanishing, for $m \gg 0$ the natural morphism
\[
p_* \mathcal{O}_{\PP(\phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(G_{c,\free}))}(m) 
\simeq \Sym^m\! \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(G_{c,\free}) 
\to \psi_{G*} \mathcal{O}_{X_G}(m)
\]
is surjective.
Since $\pi_G \colon M \to X_G$ is meromorphic, we have an isomorphism 
$\mathcal{O}_{X_G} \simeq \pi_{G*} \mathcal{O}_M$. 
Then, by the projection formula, we obtain
\[
\psi_{G*} \mathcal{O}_{X_G}(m) 
\simeq \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(mG_{c,\free}).
\]
Tensoring with $\mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1})$, we obtain the injection
\[
\mathscr{W}_m 
= \psi_{G*} \mathcal{O}_{X_G}(m) \otimes \mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1}) 
\simeq \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(mG_{c,\free}) \otimes \mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1}) 
\hookrightarrow \mathscr{V}_m.
\]
Now consider the composition
\[
\mathscr{U}_m 
\simeq \Sym^m\! \phi_* \mathcal{O}_M(G_{c,\free}) \otimes \mathcal{O}_Y(-mD_{c,1}) 
\twoheadrightarrow \mathscr{W}_m \hookrightarrow \mathscr{V}_m,
\]
which is surjective over $Y_0$. 
It follows that the inclusion $\mathscr{W}_m \hookrightarrow \mathscr{V}_m$ is an isomorphism over $Y_0$, 
which completes the proof of the claim.
\end{proof}


As a consequence of the claim above, for every $m$, the sheaf $\mathscr{W}_m$ is a flat vector bundle on $Y_0$ and satisfies condition~($\bullet$) in Proposition~\ref{prop_bir-geometry-psi}(d). 
Therefore, the sheaf $\SheafHom_{\mathcal{O}_Y}(\Sym^m \mathscr{W}_1, \mathscr{W}_m)$ is also a flat vector bundle on $Y_0$ satisfying the same condition. Hence, each of its global sections is parallel. In particular, the compatibility condition~(2) in \cite[Proposition~2.5]{MW} is satisfied. Therefore, by \cite[Proposition~2.5]{MW}, we conclude that the morphism $\psi_G \colon X_G \to Y$ is a locally constant fibration over $Y_0$.

Finally, we claim that $(X_G)_y \simeq F$ for all $y \in Y_0$, where $F$ denotes the general fiber of $\psi$ (note that $\psi$ is almost holomorphic). 
Indeed, since $\psi_G$ coincides with the $\phi$-relative Iitaka fibration of $G + cE$, the restriction $\psi_G|_{M_y}$ is a bimeromorphic morphism for general $y \in Y$. 
On the other hand, by the construction of $G$ in Lemma~\ref{lemm-good}, we have
\[
G + cE = \pi^* A + \text{an effective }\pi\text{-exceptional divisor},
\]
with $A|_F$ being very ample. 
By \eqref{eq-rank}, the linear system $\left|\pi^* A|_{M_y}\right|$ is the free part of $\left|(G + cE)|_{M_y}\right|$. 
Hence, the morphism $\pi_G|_{M_y}$ factorizes through $\pi|_{M_y} \colon M_y \to X_y \simeq F$. 
Since $\pi_G|_{M_y}$ is meromorphic, we conclude by Zariski's Main Theorem that $(X_G)_y \simeq F$.



\smallskip

\paragraph{\bf Step 3: We show that every codimension-one component of the exceptional locus of 
$\pi_G|_{\phi^{-1}(Y_0)} \colon \phi^{-1}(Y_0) \to \psi_G^{-1}(Y_0)$ is contained in $E$.}

The argument follows that of \cite[\S3.C, Proof of Theorem~1.2, Step~2]{CH19} 
(see also \cite[Lemma~4.5]{Wang20}). 
Let $D$ be a divisorial component of the exceptional locus of 
$\pi_G|_{\phi^{-1}(Y_0)}$. We distinguish two cases:

\begin{itemize}
\item Case 1: $D$ is $\phi$-horizontal. In this case, for general $y \in Y_0$, 
the restriction $D|_{M_y}$ is $\pi_G|_{M_y}$-exceptional. 
As shown in {\bf Step 2}, the morphism $\psi_G|_{M_y}$ coincides with $\pi|_{M_y}$. 
Therefore, $D|_{M_y}$ is contained in $E|_{M_y}$, and hence $D \subset E$.  

\item Case 2: $D$ is $\phi$-vertical. Since $\phi$ is flat over $Y_0$, 
$\phi(D)$ is also a divisor. For general $y \in \phi(D)$, 
the fiber $D_y$ of the morphism $\phi|_D \colon D \to \phi(D)$ is a component of the fiber $M_y$ of $\phi$. 
As $\psi_G$ is a morphism over $Y$, this implies that $D_y$ is contracted by $\psi_G$ 
to a variety of dimension strictly less than $\dim D_y$. 
However, by Lemma~\ref{lemm-good}(2), if $D_y$ is not contained in $E$, then 
$(G + cE)|_{D_y}$ is big, and hence $D_y$ cannot be contracted by $\psi_G$ to a variety of smaller dimension. 
Thus, we deduce that $D_y \subset E$, and consequently $D \subset E$.
\end{itemize}



\smallskip

\paragraph{\bf Step 4: Proof of the splitting theorem.}

By {\bf Step 2}, $X_G$ is a locally constant fibration over $Y_0$. 
In particular,  we have a splitting
\[
T_{\psi_G^{-1}(Y_0)} \simeq T_{X_G/Y}|_{\psi_G^{-1}(Y_0)} \oplus \psi_G^* T_{Y_0}
\]
by \cite[Remark~2.2]{Wang20}.
By {\bf Step 3}, any divisorial component of the exceptional locus of 
\[
\psi_G|_{\phi^{-1}(Y_0)} \colon \phi^{-1}(Y_0) \to \psi_G^{-1}(Y_0)
\]
is contained in the $\pi$-exceptional locus $E$. 
Hence, the above splitting induces a splitting of $T_{\phi^{-1}(Y_0) \setminus E}$, 
and consequently yields a splitting 
\[
T_X \simeq \mathcal{F} \oplus \mathcal{G}
\]
on $X$, since $\pi(E)$ has codimension $\geq 2$.


By {\bf Step 2}, the general fiber of $\psi_G$ is $F$.  
Therefore, $\mathcal{F}$ agrees with the foliation induced by the rational map $\psi \colon X \dashrightarrow Y$ over a Zariski open subset of $X$, and hence they are equal.  
In particular, the foliation $\mathcal{F}$ is algebraically integrable, and a general leaf of $\mathcal{F}$ is a fiber of the MRC fibration $\psi \colon X \dashrightarrow Y$, which is rationally connected.
By Proposition~\ref{prop_bir-geometry-psi}(a), we have
\[
K_{\mathcal{G}}|_{\pi(\phi^{-1}(Y_0) \setminus E)} \sim_{\QQ} 0,
\]
which implies $K_{\mathcal{G}} \sim_{\QQ} 0$ since both $\pi(E)$ and $\pi(\phi^{-1}(Y \setminus Y_0))$ have codimension  $\geq 2$ in $X$. 
This completes the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm_splitting}.
\end{proof}







\subsection{Structure of MRC fibrations}\label{subsec-str}

In this subsection, we prove our main theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm-main}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-main}]
Since $X$ is smooth, Theorem~\ref{thm-flatness} implies that the reflexive sheaf $\mathcal{E}_m$ is locally free on $X$ for every $m \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$. 
Thus, the condition in Theorem~\ref{thm_splitting} is satisfied, and we obtain a splitting 
\[
T_X \simeq \mathcal{F} \oplus \mathcal{G},
\]
where $\mathcal{F}$ is the foliation induced by the MRC fibration of $X$, and $K_{\mathcal{G}} \sim_{\mathbb{Q}} 0$.

Since $X$ is smooth, both $\mathcal{F}$ and $\mathcal{G}$ are regular foliations. 
In particular, a general leaf of $\mathcal{F}$ is compact and rationally connected. 
Hence, we can apply \cite[Corollary~2.11]{Hor07} to the pair $(X, \mathcal{F})$ 
to obtain a smooth projective fibration $f \colon X \to Z$ onto a compact K\"ahler manifold $Z$ 
such that $f$ coincides with the MRC fibration of $X$ and $\mathcal{F} = T_{X/Z}$.
Moreover, since $T_X \simeq T_{X/Z} \oplus \mathcal{G}$, the analytic version of Ehresmann's theorem 
(see \cite[Theorem~3.17]{Hor07} and also \cite[\S V.3, Theorem~1 and Theorem~3, pp.~91--95]{CLN85}) 
implies that $f$ is a locally constant fibration.

We finally show that $f$ is locally constant with respect to the pair $(X, \Delta)$, 
By Proposition \ref{prop_bir-geometry-psi} (a), 
we see that $K_Z \sim_{\mathbb{Q}} 0$, 
which implies that $-(K_{X/Z} + \Delta)$ is nef. Then, we can apply \cite[Theorem~4.1]{MW} (whose proof works as long as the fibration is projective, regardless of whether the total space is projective) to conclude.
\end{proof}






\section{Applications of Theorem \ref{thm-main}}\label{sec-application}

\subsection{On the inequalities of Hacon--\texorpdfstring{M\textsuperscript c}{text}Kernan  type} 
\label{subsec-HM}

This subsection is devoted to the proof of Theorem \ref{thm-HM}. 


\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{thm-HM}]
Consider a locally constant fibration $f \colon X \to Y$ with the properties in Theorem \ref{thm-main}. 
Both conclusions (1), (2) essentially follow from the properties of the local constancy of MRC fibrations. 

(1) 
Let $D$ be an effective divisor with $D \sim_{\bb{Q}} -(K_{X}+\Delta)$. 
Then, for $0< \delta \ll 1$, 
the pair $(X, \Delta + \delta D)$ is still a klt pair with the nef anti-canonical $\bb{Q}$-line bundle, 
and thus $f \colon X \to Y$ is a locally constant fibration also 
with respect to the new boundary divisor $\Delta + \delta D$, 
which shows that each component of $D$ is horizontal. 
This implies that the restriction map 
$$
H^{0}(X, -m(K_{X}+\Delta)) \to H^{0}(F, -m(K_{F}+\Delta|_{F}))
$$
is injective for any $m \in \mathbb{Z}_{+}$ with $mD$ Cartier. 
The desired inequality is a direct consequence of this injectivity. 

(2) 
The inequality $\nu (-(K_{X}+\Delta)) \geq \nu (-(K_{F}+\Delta)|_{F})$ is 
obvious by the definition of numerical dimensions. 
To prove the converse inequality, we consider the diagram in the definition of locally constant fibrations: 
\begin{equation}\label{locally-constant}
\begin{gathered}\xymatrix@C=40pt@R=30pt{
X  \ar[d]_{f} &  X\times_Y\Unv Y \cong \Unv{Y} \times F \ar[d]_{\pr_{1}} \ar[l]^{p_{X} \qquad  \qquad}  
\ar[r]^{\qquad \qquad \pr_{2}} & F  \\ 
Y   & \ar[l]^{p_{Y}} \Unv{Y}.  &  \\   
}\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
From now on, let $m$ denote a sufficiently divisible and large integer. 
By \cite{CH24}, there exists a relatively $f$-ample line bundle $A$. 
Following \cite[Lemma 2.4]{MW}, 
we consider the decomposition of $A$ into $A \sim_{\mathbb{Q}} A_{f}+f^{*}{A_{b}}$ 
such that 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] $p_{X}^{*}A_{f}=\pr_{1}^{*} mA_{F}$  for some $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundle $A_{F}$; 
\item[(2)] $f_{*}(mA_{f})$ is a flat vector bundle.  
\end{itemize}
Let $\rho \colon \pi_{1}(Y) \to \Aut(F)$ be 
a representation such that $X \cong  (\Unv{Y} \times F)/\pi_{1}(Y)$. 
The representation $\rho \colon \pi_{1}(Y) \to \Aut(F)$ preserves the line bundle $A_{F}$, 
and thus, it can be extended to a $\GL$ representation:
$$
\bar \rho \colon \pi_{1}(Y) \to \Aut(F, A|_{F}) \to\Aut(\mathbb{P}^{N-1})\cong \GL_{N}(\bb{C})  
$$
by considering the embeding $F \hookrightarrow \mathbb{P}^{N-1}$ associated with the line bundle $mA|_{F}$. 
The Fubini-Study metric $g_{FS}$ on $\mathcal{O}_{\bb{P}^{N-1}}(1)$ is clearly $\bar \rho$-invariant, 
and its restriction to the fiber $F$ is  the $\rho$-invariant metric $g_{F}:=g_{FS}|_{F}$ on $A|_{F}$. 
Consequently, the pullback $\pr_{1}^{*}g_{FS}|_{F}$ determines the metric  on $\pr_{1}^{*} A_{F}=p_{X}^{*}A_{f}$ 
that is invariant under the action $\pi_{1}(Y) \to \Unv{Y} \times F$, 
and thus it descends to the metric $g$ on  $A_{f}$. 
By construction, the metric on $A_{f}$ satisfies that  
\begin{align}\label{eq-cur}
\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{g} =  \pr_{2}^{*} \sqrt{-1}\Theta_{g_{F}}
\end{align}
for a fixed local trivialization $f^{-1}(B) \cong B \times F$ 
with  the projection $\pr_{2} \colon B \times F \to F$. 
Furthermore, we see that $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{g}|_{F}$ is strictly positive on the fiber $F$. 

Consider the nef $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundle $L:=-(K_{X}+\Delta)$. 
In the same way as above, 
by noting that $mL + A$ is relatively $f$-ample, 
we consider the decomposition 
$$
mL + A=mL+A=(mL+A)_{f} + f^{*}(mL+A)_{b}. 
$$ 
On the other hand, we see that  
\begin{align}
p_{X}^*(K_X+\Delta)\sim_\bb{Q}\pr_{1}^*K_{\Unv{Y}} +  \pr_{2}^*(K_F+\Delta|_F) 
\sim_\bb{Q}  \pr_{2}^*(K_F+\Delta|_F)  
\end{align}
by $K_{Y} \sim_{\bb{Q}} 0$ and the local constancy (with respect to the boundary divisor $\Delta$). 
This shows that  $(mL+A)_{f}=mL+A_{f}$ and $(mL+A)_{b}=A_{b}$. 
Consequently, we can find a metric $g_{m}$ on $L$ with the same property \eqref{eq-cur}. 
Hence, we can deduce that 
$$
c_{1}(L)^{a}
\cdot 
c_{1}(A_{f})^{b} =0 
$$
for integers $a, b$ with $a+b> n-m$
by noting that $n-m$ is the fiber dimension, 
where $n:=\dim X$ and $m:=\dim Y$. 


For simplicity, we put $\nu:=\nu(L)$. 
For a K\"ahler form $\omega_{Y}$ on $Y$,  
we consider the intersection number 
$$
c_{1}(L)^{\nu} \cdot c_{1}(A_{f})^{n-\nu-k} \cdot \{ f^{*}\omega_{Y} \}^{k}
$$ 
for an integer $0 \leq k \leq n-\nu $. 
We have $f^{*}\omega_{Y}^{k} = 0$ in the case $k > m$ and
we have $c_{1}(L)^{\nu} \cdot c_{1}(A_{f})^{n-\nu-k} \equiv0$ in the case $\nu+(n-\nu-k)>n-m$ (i.e.\,$m>k$). 
Thus, the binomial expansion shows that 
\begin{align}
0 < c_{1}(L)^{\nu} \cdot (c_{1}(A_{f}) + \{f^{*}\omega_{Y}\})^{n-{\nu}}  
= c_{1}(L)^{\nu} \cdot c_{1}(A_{f})^{n-\nu-m} \cdot \{f^{*}\omega_{Y}\}^{m} \cdot \binom{n-{\nu}}{m}.
\end{align}
Note that the left inequality follows since $c_{1}(A_{f}) + \{f^{*}\omega_{Y}\}$ is a K\"ahler class. 
By Fubini's theorem, we deduce that 
\begin{align}
c_{1}(L)^{\nu} \cdot c_{1}(A_{f})^{n-\nu-m} \cdot \{f^{*}\omega_{Y}\}^{m} 
= f^{*}(c_{1}(L)^{\nu} \cdot c_{1}(A_{f})^{n-\nu-m}) \cdot \{\omega_{Y}\}^{m}.
\end{align}
Moreover, we see that $f^{*}( c_{1}(L)^{\nu} \cdot c_{1}(A_{f})^{n-\nu-m} )$  
is the constant defined by the fiber integral 
\begin{align}
f^{*}(c_{1}(L)^{\nu} \cdot c_{1}(A_{f})^{n-\nu-m}) 
= \int_{F} c_{1}(L|_{F})^{\nu} \cdot c_{1}(A_{f}|_{F})^{n-\nu-m}.
\end{align}
This yields the desired inequality 
$
\nu(L) \leq \nu(L|_{F}).
$
\end{proof}



\subsection{On generic nefness of tangent bundles}\label{subsec-gen}

In this subsection, we prove Theorem \ref{nefness}, and Theorem \ref{thm-second}, 
which generalizes results in \cite{zbMATH07720766, IM22} respectively to compact K\"ahler manifolds. 

The proof is based on the following result, which immediately follows from Cao-P\u aun's recent work \cite[Thm 1.2]{arXiv:2502.02183}. 

\begin{thm}[{\cite[Thm 1.2]{arXiv:2502.02183}}]
\label{new positivity*}
Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a fibration between compact K\"ahler manifolds. 
Let $\theta$ be a $d$-closed smooth $(1,1)$-form on $Y$ and $L$ be a $\QQ$-line bundle on $X$. 
Assume that the following conditions hold$:$
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbullet]
    \item $L$ admits a singular Hermitian metric $h_{L}$ such that 
    $\sqrt{-1}\Theta_{h_L} \geq f^{*}\theta$ and $\mathcal{I}(h_{L}) = \mathcal{O}_{X}$. 
    \item $(K_X + L)|_{X_y}$ is pseudo-effective for a general fiber $X_{y}$.
    \item There exist a K\"ahler metric $\omega_X$ and a holomorphic $2$-form $\sigma$ on $X$ such that $\omega_X + \sigma + \bar{\sigma}$ represents a rational cohomology class, and the restriction of $\sigma$ to a general fiber $X_{y}$ of $f$ vanishes.
\end{enumerate}
Then, the $\QQ$-line bundle $K_{X/Y} + L - D(f)$ is $\theta$-positively curved, in other word, there is a $(1,1)$-current $T\in c_1(K_{X/Y}+L-D(f))$ satisfying $T\geq f^*\theta$. 
\end{thm}
Here $D(f)$ is the ramification divisor defined by 
$
D(f) = \sum_{i=1}^k (m_i - 1) W_i,
$
where $W_i$ runs over the irreducible divisors on $X$ such that $f(W_i) = Z_i$ is a divisor and $m_i$ denotes the multiplicity of $f^\ast Z_i$ along $W_i$. 

\begin{proof}
The proof is almost the same as that of \cite[Theorem 1.2]{arXiv:2502.02183}.  
We therefore highlight only the minor modifications.  
Let $y$ be a general point of $Y$ and choose a small neighborhood $U\ni y$ on which $\theta = dd^{c}\varphi$.  
Apply \cite[Lemma 3.3 and Theorem 3.4]{arXiv:2502.02183} to the (local) semi-positive metric $h_{L}\,e^{f^{*}\varphi}$, 
which induces a semi-positive metric $h_{\min,\varphi}$ defined over $U$.  
Note that \cite[Lemma 3.3 and Theorem 3.4]{arXiv:2502.02183} also apply to $\mathbb{Q}$-line bundles.
In our setting, the local metrics with minimal singularities $h_{\min,\varphi}$ on the open sets $f^{-1}(U)$ 
do not glue to a global metric on $K_{X/Y}+L$.  
However, as in the proof of \cite[Theorem 1.2]{arXiv:2502.02183}, we can check that the twisted metrics $h_{\min,\varphi}\,e^{-f^{*}\varphi}$ glue together on a Zariski-open subset of $X$ and satisfy the same lower curvature bound by the same local computation.  
Finally, the usual arguments extend this singular metric on $K_{X/Y}+L$.
\end{proof}


We now prove the following result: 
\begin{thm}[cf.~{{\cite[Theorem~1.4]{zbMATH07720766}}}]\label{nefness}
Let $X$ be a smooth compact K\"ahler manifold such that $-K_X$ is nef. 
Let $\alpha$ be a movable class $($see \cite[Definition~6.2]{arXiv:2502.02183}$)$. 
Then, for any  torsion-free quotient sheaf $\mathcal{Q}$ of $T_X$, we have 
\[
c_1(\mathcal{Q}) \cdot \alpha \ge 0.
\]
\end{thm}


\begin{proof}
Suppose, for contradiction, that $\mu_{\alpha,\min}(T_X) < 0$. 
Here $\mu_{\alpha, \min}(T_X)$ denotes the \textit{minimal slope} 
defined as the infimum of $\mu_{\alpha}(\mathcal{Q})$, 
where $\mathcal{Q}$ ranges over all non-zero torsion-free quotients $T_X \rightarrow \mathcal{Q}$.
This implies that $T_X$ is not $\alpha$-semistable since $-K_X \cdot \alpha \geq 0$. 
Thus, we obtain the Harder--Narasimhan filtration:
\[
0 = \mathcal{E}_0 \subsetneqq \mathcal{E}_1 \subsetneqq \cdots \subsetneqq \mathcal{E}_r = T_X
\]
with $r \geq 2$. 
Since $\mu_{\alpha}(T_X / \mathcal{E}_{r-1}) = \mu_{\alpha,\min}(T_X) < 0$ and $\mu_{\alpha}(T_X) \geq 0$, 
we can take  the minimal index  $k \in \{1, \dots, r-1\}$ such that $\mu_{\alpha}(T_X / \mathcal{E}_k) < 0$. Set $\mathcal{F} := \mathcal{E}_k$, then $K_X \cdot \alpha > K_{\mathcal{F}} \cdot \alpha$

Now consider the exact sequence:
\[
0 \rightarrow \mathcal{E}_k / \mathcal{E}_{k-1} \rightarrow T_X / \mathcal{E}_{k-1} \rightarrow T_X / \mathcal{E}_k \rightarrow 0.
\]
By the minimality of $k$, we have $\mu_{\alpha}(T_X / \mathcal{E}_{k-1}) \geq 0$, which implies
\[
\mu_{\alpha,\min}(\mathcal{F}) = \mu_{\alpha}(\mathcal{E}_k / \mathcal{E}_{k-1}) > 0.
\]
In particular, this implies that $\calF$ is a foliation on $X$ by standard observation. 
Then, \cite[Corollary 1.5]{arXiv:2502.02183} shows that $\mathcal{F} \subset T_X$ is algebraically integrable such that the closure of its leaves are rationally connected. Let $f \colon X \dashrightarrow Y$ be the associated dominant meromorphic map 
to a smooth variety $Y$, and take an elimination  
$\pi \colon \tilde{X} \to X$ of indeterminacies of $f$ with the following diagram: 
\[
\xymatrix{
\tilde{X} \ar[r]^{\pi} \ar[dr]_{\tilde f} & X \ar@{-->}[d]^{f} \\
 & Y.
}
\]
where $\pi:\tilde X\to X$ is bimeromorphic. Then, we have
$K_{\tilde{X}} = \pi^* K_X + E_1$ for some effective $\pi$-exceptional $E_1$, and 
\begin{align}\label{a}
K_{\tilde{X}} \cdot \pi^* \alpha > K_{\tilde{\mathcal{F}}} \cdot \pi^* \alpha,
\end{align}
where $\tilde{\mathcal{F}}:=T_{\tilde X/Y} \subset T_{\widetilde{X}}$ is the foliation on $\tilde{X}$ induced by $\mathcal{F}$ via $\pi$.


By \cite[Theorem 8.5]{Ou25}, 
there exist a K\"ahler form $\omega_{\tilde{X}}$ and a holomorphic $2$-form $\sigma$ on $\tilde{X}$ such that 
\[
[\omega_{\tilde{X}} + \sigma + \bar\sigma] \in H^2(\tilde{X}, \mathbb{Q}).
\]
Since a general fiber $F$ of $\tilde{f}$ is rationally connected, it follows that $\sigma|_F = 0$.
By Claudon--H\"oring's projectivity criterion \cite[Corollary 4.2]{CH24}, the morphism $\tilde{f}$ is projective. Hence, there exists a $\tilde{f}$-ample line bundle $L'$ on $\tilde{X}$. Then, up to rescaling $\omega_{\tilde X}$, we can take a smooth metric $h'$ on $L'$ such that 
\[
\sqrt{-1} \Theta_{h'} + \tilde{f}^* \omega_{Y} \geq \omega_{\tilde{X}}
\]
for some K\"ahler metric $\omega_Y$.
Since $-K_{\tilde{X}} + E_1 = \pi_X^*(-K_X)$ is nef, for every $\varepsilon>0$ we can take a smooth metric $g_{\varepsilon}$ on $-K_{\widetilde{X}} + E_1$ such that 
\[
\sqrt{-1} \Theta_{g_\varepsilon} \ge -\varepsilon \omega_{\widetilde{X}}.
\]
Then, for every $m \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$, we set
\[
L_m := -K_{\tilde{X}} + E_1 + \frac{1}{m} L', \quad
h_{L_m} := g_{\varepsilon_m} \cdot (h')^{1/m}. 
\]
By choosing $\varepsilon_m < \frac{1}{m}$ small enough so that 
\[
\sqrt{-1} \Theta_{h_{L_m}} \geq -\frac{1}{m} \tilde{f}^* \omega_{Y},
\]
Theorem~\ref{new positivity*} applied to $(L_m,h_{L_m})$ yields a closed $(1,1)$-current
\[
T_m \in c_1(K_{\tilde{X}/Y} + (-K_{\tilde{X}} + E_1) + \frac{1}{m}L')
\]
such that 
\[
T_m - [D(\tilde{f})] \geq -\frac{1}{m} \tilde{f}^* \omega_{Y}.
\]
Since the mass $\int_{\tilde{X}} T_m \wedge \omega_{\tilde{X}}^{n-1}$ is uniformly bounded, we may take a weak limit as $m \to \infty$ to obtain a positive current
\[
T \in c_1(K_{\tilde{X}/Y} + (-K_{\tilde{X}} + E_1) - D(\tilde{f})).
\]
By standard result (see, for example, \cite[Lemma 2.31]{CKT16}), we have
$
K_{\tilde{\mathcal{F}}} = K_{\tilde{X}/Y} - D(\tilde{f}).
$
Thus, we deduce that 
$
K_{\tilde{\mathcal{F}}} - K_{\tilde{X}} + E_1
$
is pseudo-effective, contradicting \eqref{a}.
\end{proof}






The following is a slight generalization of the structure theorem \cite[Corollary 1.7]{IM22} and \cite[Theorem 1.8]{zbMATH07720766}.
\begin{thm}[{\cite[Corollary 1.7]{IM22}, \cite[Theorem 1.8]{zbMATH07720766}}]\label{thm-second}
Let $X$ be a compact K\"ahler manifold of dimension $n$ such that $-K_X$ is nef. 
Then, 
we have the following Miyaoka-Yau inequality for any K\"ahler forms $\omega_1, \cdots, \omega_{n-2}$,
$$c_2(T_X ) \cdot \{\omega_1\}\cdot \cdots  \cdot \{\omega_{n-2} \} \geq 0.$$
Moreover, the following conditions are equivalent$:$
\begin{enumerate}
 \item[$(1)$]  there are K\"ahler forms $\omega_1, \cdots, \omega_{n-2}$ such that
$$c_2(T_X ) \cdot \{\omega_1\}\cdot \cdots  \cdot \{\omega_{n-2} \}= 0;$$
\item[$(2)$]  there is a finite \'etale cover $\tilde{X} \to X$ such that $\tilde{X}$ is either isomorphic to a torus or isomorphic to a $\mathbb{P}^1$-bundle over a torus $($equivalently, $\PP (V)$ for some vector bundle $V$ of rank $2$ by \cite[Lemma 7.4]{CP91}$)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
We refer to \cite[Theorem~6.1]{Miy87}, \cite[Corollary~1.5]{zbMATH07720766}, and \cite[Remark~5.4.11]{Cao13} for the first statement concerning the Miyaoka--Yau inequality via generic nefness (see Theorem~\ref{nefness}).

The implication from (1) to (2) is obvious.  
Hence, it suffices to prove that (1) implies (2).  
To this end, we will show that under Condition~(1), the augmented irregularity of $X$ is at least $n-1$, which clearly implies (2).  
This is proved via an approximation argument based on Ou's result \cite[Theorem~1.8]{zbMATH07720766} for the projective case.  

First, note that by \cite[Lemma~5.1]{zbMATH07720766}, we may replace the classes $\omega_1, \dots, \omega_{n-2}$ with any  K\"ahler class.  
Although \cite[Lemma~5.1]{zbMATH07720766} is stated for ample classes, its proof works for arbitrary K\"ahler classes.

By \cite[Theorem~D]{NW23}, together with Theorem~\ref{thm-main}, there exists an algebraic approximation of $X$ in which the anti-canonical bundle remains nef.  
Although small deformations of compact K\"ahler manifolds remain K\"ahler, the deformation morphism is not necessarily a K\"ahler morphism.  
However, the construction in \cite[Theorem~D]{NW23} guarantees that the algebraic approximation of $X$ in our situation is a K\"ahler morphism.  
Indeed, we consider the MRC fibration $f \colon X \to Y$, which is shown to be a locally constant fibration by Theorem~\ref{thm-main}.  
We construct an algebraic approximation of $X$, as a deformation of the complex structure, via the coarse moduli space of polarized Calabi--Yau manifolds constructed in \cite{FS90}, as follows:  
There exists an algebraic approximation $\mathfrak{Y} \to \mathbb{D}$ (with $\mathbb{D}$ a small disc in $\mathbb{C}$) of $Y$, which is a K\"ahler morphism by \cite{FS90}.  
By the proof of \cite[Proposition~2.5]{MW}, there exists a flat vector bundle $E$ over $Y$ such that $X$ is relatively embedded in $\mathbb{P}(E)$ over $Y$.  
Since $\pi_1(Y) \simeq \pi_1(\mathfrak{Y})$, the bundle $E$ extends to a flat vector bundle $\mathfrak{E}$ over $\mathfrak{Y}$.  
Moreover, the defining equations of $X$ in the universal cover of $\mathbb{P}(E)$ are independent of the universal cover of $Y$ (see the proof of \cite[Proposition~2.5]{MW}), so the same equations define a projective submersion $\mathfrak{X} \to \mathfrak{Y}$ via the relative embedding $\mathfrak{X} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{P}(\mathfrak{E})$.  
Thus, the composition $\mathfrak{X} \to \mathbb{D}$ is a K\"ahler morphism.

Both the augmented irregularity and Condition~(1) for this chosen relative K\"ahler class are invariant under deformation.  
Hence, applying \cite[Theorem~1.8]{zbMATH07720766} to a projective fiber in the algebraic approximation yields the desired conclusion.
\end{proof}

\section{Appendix: Chern classes of coherent sheaves}\label{sec-app}

In this appendix, we collect some basic facts about determinant sheaves and Chern classes of coherent sheaves.  
Although these results may be known to experts,  
we include the proofs for the reader's convenience, as they are not readily available in the literature.







We begin by defining the determinant sheaf of a sheaf $\mathcal{E}$ on a normal analytic variety $X$.  
Our definition agrees with the standard one given in Section~\ref{subsec-notation} when $\mathcal{E}$ is torsion-free. 
For the corresponding definition on a smooth complex manifold, we refer the reader to \cite[Section 6, Chap.~V]{Kob87}.

\begin{defi}
\label{det-sheaf}
Let $\mathcal{E}$ be a coherent sheaf on a normal analytic variety $X$. 
Consider the short exact sequence
\[
0 \to \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}} \to \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{E}/\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}} \to 0, 
\]
where $\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}}$ denotes the maximal torsion subsheaf of $\mathcal{E}$.
Since $\mathcal{E}/\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}}$ is torsion-free, its determinant sheaf is defined as
\[
\det(\mathcal{E}/\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}}) := \wedge^{[\mathrm{rk}\,\mathcal{E}]}(\mathcal{E}/\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}}).
\]
By \cite[Proposition 6.14, Chap.~V]{Kob87}, the determinant line bundle $\det \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}}|_{X_{\mathrm{reg}}}$ (defined only on the regular locus $X_{\mathrm{reg}}$) admits a non-trivial section whose zero locus is contained in the support of $\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}}$.
By the Remmert--Stein theorem (for example, see \cite[Theorem 8.7, Chap.~II]{agbook}) and the normality of $X$, 
this zero locus extends to a Weil divisor across the singular locus $X_{\sing}$.
Using this extended Weil divisor $D_{\mathrm{tor}}$, we define
\[
\det(\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}}) := \mathcal{O}_X(D_{\mathrm{tor}}).
\]
We then define the determinant sheaf of $\mathcal{E}$ as
\[
\det(\mathcal{E}) := \det(\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}}) [\otimes] \det(\mathcal{E}/\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}}).
\]
Note that $c_{1}(\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{tor}})$ is represented by an effective Weil divisor by definition. 
\end{defi}


This definition agrees with the usual one when $X$ is smooth 
(see \cite[Lemma~6.3, Proposition~6.10, Proposition~6.14, Chap.~V]{Kob87}).  
By Definition~\ref{det-sheaf}, we immediately obtain the following propositions: 


\begin{prop}
\label{det-tor}
Let $\mathcal{E}$ be a torsion coherent sheaf on a strongly $\mathbb{Q}$-factorial analytic variety $X$.
Then, the determinant sheaf $\det \mathcal{E}$ is $\mathbb{Q}$-linearly equivalent to an effective $\mathbb{Q}$-Weil divisor whose support is contained in the support of $\mathcal{E}$.
\end{prop}


\begin{prop}
\label{det-open}
Let $\mathcal{E}$ be a coherent sheaf on a normal analytic variety $X$.  
Let $U \subset X$ be an open subset with the open immersion $i_U \colon U \to X$.  
Then, we have  
$$
i_U^*\mathrm{det}(\mathcal{E}) = \mathrm{det}(\mathcal{E}|_{U}).
$$

\end{prop}

The determinant line bundle is additive with respect to short exact sequences of 
locally free sheaves on a smooth complex manifold (for example, see \cite[Proposition 6.9, Chap.~V]{Kob87}). 
This additivity extends to determinant sheaves on normal analytic varieties as well. 


\begin{prop}\label{det-exact}
Let
$$
0 \to \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{Q} \to 0
$$
be a short exact sequence of coherent sheaves on a normal analytic variety $X$.  
Then, we have
$$
\det(\mathcal{E}) = \det(\mathcal{S}) [\otimes] \det(\mathcal{Q}).
$$
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
Since $X$ is normal, it suffices to verify the equality on the regular locus of $X$.  
By Proposition \ref{det-open}, the desired equality then follows from \cite[Proposition 6.9, Chap.~V]{Kob87}.
\end{proof}


We now check a compatibility property for Chern characters.

\begin{prop}\label{topo}
Let $\mathcal{E}$ be a coherent sheaf on a compact complex manifold $X$,  
and let $U \subset X$ be a Zariski open subset with the open immersion $i_U \colon U \to X$.  
Then, we have 
$$
i_{U}^{*}\!\operatorname{ch}(\mathcal{E}) \;=\; \operatorname{ch}\bigl(\mathcal{E}|_{Y_{0}}\bigr)
$$
in de Rham cohomology.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
The statement follows directly from the construction of Chern classes via locally free resolutions;  
see \cite[Remark 2.11]{Wu23} for details.
\end{proof}



The proposition below is often used as an extension theorem for positive currents
representing Bott-Chern cohomology classes (see \cite{Dem85} for currents on analytic varieties).
\begin{prop}[{\cite[Proposition 4.6.3]{BEG13}}] \label{BEG}
Let $\alpha \in H^{1,1}_{BC}(X, \mathbb{C})$ be a Bott-Chern cohomology class on a normal analytic variety $X$, and let $T$
be a semi-positive current on $X_{\rm{reg}}$ 
representing the restriction 
$\alpha|_{X_{\rm{reg}}} \in H^{1,1}_{BC}(X_{\rm{reg}}, \mathbb{C})$.
Then $T$ admits a unique extension to a semi-positive current with local potentials on $X$ representing 
$\alpha \in H^{1,1}_{BC}(X, \mathbb{C})$.
\end{prop}



