The~action of the gauge group $\H_\text{loc}$ on $b$ is obtained by pullback of the result of the action of $\H$ on $\beta$:
$b^\gamma \defeq \s^*(\beta^\upgamma)$.
So, for $\gamma \in \H_\text{loc}$  we have that $\upphi^\gamma$ is:
\begin{align}
\label{GT-trsf-fields}
A^\gamma = \gamma\-  A \gamma  + \gamma\-d\gamma, 
\quad 
\vphi^\gamma  \defeq \rho(\gamma)\-\vphi, 
\quad \text{and} \quad
e^\gamma\defeq \gamma\-e \ \rarrow\ 
g^\gamma\defeq g.
\end{align}
Similarly, or consequently, we have $(D\vphi)^\gamma = \rho(\gamma)\-D\vphi$ and $F^\gamma=\gamma\- F \gamma$, as well as  $\b F^\gamma=\gamma\- \b F \gamma$ with $T^\gamma =\gamma\- T$. %\footnote{
Consider that in \eqref{GT-trsf-fields}, one should  typically understand that $\H_\textbf{loc}=\H_\textbf{loc}^{\text{\tiny {YM}}} \times \H_\textbf{loc}^\text{grav}$: 
The Yang-Mills gauge group $\H_\textbf{loc}^{\text{\tiny {YM}}}$ acting only on  the Yang-Mills gauge potential $A=A'$ and  matter fields $\vphi$, 
but trivially on the gravitational potential $\b A=A+e $ (thus~on $g$), 
while the gravitational gauge group $\H_\textbf{loc}^\text{grav}$ acts trivially on a Yang-Mills potential $A=A'$, 
but non-trivially on matter fields $\vphi$ -- which are  \emph{spinorial} representations of $\H_\textbf{loc}^\text{grav}$ -- and on gravitational potential $\b A =A +e$ (yet still trivially on $g$ if $\eta$ is $\H_\textbf{loc}$-invariant).\footnote{In established physics $\H_\textbf{loc}^\text{grav}$ is the Lorentz gauge group, so $g$ is indeed invariant as $\eta$ is the Minkowski form on $V=\RR^n$. 
In~speculative theories the action of $\H_\textbf{loc}^\text{grav}$ on $g$ may be  non-trivial:  e.g. in conformal geometry/gravity, it induces a conformal transformation, $g\mapsto g^\gamma = z^2g$ for $z\in C^\infty(U, \RR/\{0\})$. 
}

