Consider a concrete gauge theory, e.g. QED. The Lagrangian of QED is

\begin{equation}
\label{QED}
{\cal L_{QED}}=-\frac{1}{4}F_{\mu \nu}^2 + i \bar\psi \gamma^\mu \partial_\mu \psi - e \bar\psi \gamma^\mu A_\mu \psi  - m \bar\psi  \psi       .     
\end{equation}

Here $A_\mu$ is the gauge field, namely the photon field, and  $\psi$ is the matter field, namely the electron-positron field, $\gamma^\mu $ are Dirac matrices, and $m$ is the mass of the electron or positron. Importantly, ${\cal L_{QED}}$ is invariant under the following gauge transformations. 

\begin{align}
\label{Gauge}
 A_\mu (x) \rightarrow A_\mu (x) + \partial_\mu f(x) && \psi (x) \rightarrow e^{-i e f(x)} \psi (x). 
\end{align}

In this concrete theory, $A_\mu$ is what we call the \emph{bare} gauge field. As we saw in the previous section, the corresponding principal bundle (the connection on which represents  $A_\mu$) falls victim to the hole argument. We call this principal bundle the \emph{bare} principal bundle. However, since $A_\mu$ and  $\psi$ transform under \ref{Gauge}, from the perspective of manifestly gauge-invariant approaches (see Berghofer et al. 2023), these bare fields should not be considered as physically real quantities of \ref{QED}. Thus, from this perspective, if $A_\mu$ does not qualify as physically real, it does not come as a surprise that the corresponding bare principal bundle cannot be considered real either.

