\begin{abstract}
In this paper we propose a dynamic output-feedback controller synthesis method  for discrete-time linear time-invariant systems. The synthesis goal is to render closed-loop system dissipative with respect to a given generic unstructured quadratic supply rate, while  the system dynamics is partially represented by input-state data corrupted by a bounded disturbance.
 The controller synthesis is performed with respect to all systems which are consistent with the available data, and it is formulated  in terms of a linear matrix inequality parametrized by a scalar variable, so that the synthesis can be performed using line search and convex optimization. Within the considered setting, the proposed synthesis procedure is non-conservative in a sense that it is based on conditions which are both necessary and sufficient. 
\end{abstract}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{INTRODUCTION}
In this paper, we propose a controller synthesis method which belongs to the direct data-based approaches (\textit{informativity approach} in particular), which have gained significant attention during past years, see
\cite{R27} for an overview. Before continuing with the literature review, we first briefly present the results of this paper, so that we can more clearly point out the differences with respect to the related work.

We are concerned with dynamic output-feedback controller synthesis for discrete-time linear time-invariant (LTI) systems with the goal to render the closed-loop system dissipative with respect to a given generic unstructured quadratic supply rate. We assume that the matrices that describe the system dynamics are unknown, except for the matrix that determines the subspace through which the disturbance affects the system dynamics. Furthermore, we assume that the matrices of the output channel for performance (i.e., channel on which we want to impose dissipativity for the closed-loop) and control are known. To compensate for a partially unknown model, we assume to have one recorded system trajectory. A specific feature of our problem definition is that we assume that the known system's trajectory consists of the control input data and the corresponding system's state trajectory. The recorded data is possibly corrupted by an unknown but bounded disturbance. We emphasize that the input-state data from the recording setting is only required for the purpose of controller design, while the designed controller is a dynamic output-feedback controller rather than a state-feedback controller.
We provide a convexification procedure which results in an efficiently solvable controller synthesis method and is non-conservative in a sense that it is based on \emph{necessary} and \emph{sufficient} conditions for all systems consistent with the data. Although in some parts the convexification procedure mimics the procedure from the model-based synthesis, see e.g. \cite{R25}, it includes several novel elements. To the best of our knowledge, the results of this paper present the first approach where the dynamic output-feedback controller is designed from noisy input-state data for a generic LTI state-space model with generic performance channel (disturbance - controlled output channel).

There are indeed several approaches\footnote{Here we consider only controller synthesis methods for synthesizing an off-line controller, i.e., we do not consider predictive type of controllers, which are also extensively studied in the literature.} that deal with \emph{output-feedback} (stabilizing, $H_2$, $H_\infty$, quadratic performance) controller synthesis  \cite{R26, c5, a1, zz2, R23}, but they are limited to auto-regressive models 
in which the disturbance affects the system in a specific way (with no associated dynamics) and cannot describe an arbitrary LTI system. 
Furthermore, these results require restriction regarding the relation between system lag, order and number of outputs, or rely on disturbance during data recording process.
In the case of no disturbance, this restriction can be bypassed by constructing special non-minimal realization using the data as shown in \cite{R33}.
 Also, mentioned restriction is addressed in \cite{R22},  for the case of synthesis of a stabilizing controller.
 In contrast to \cite{R22}, where the data is corrupted by measurement error, in this paper we consider generic process disturbances  that affect the system during the data recording process.
   In \cite{R26}, \cite{c4}, \cite{c6}, \cite{R24}, \cite{zz1}, \cite{zz66}, data of input-state trajectories were used to derive (stabilizing, $H_2$, $H_\infty$, quadratic performance) controller synthesis methods, all of which differ from this paper as they are concerned with \emph{static state-feedback} controller synthesis.  

On the more practical side, one obvious question which comes to mind regarding the problem setting considered in this paper is the following one: if we have access to measurements of the full state vector, why bother with dynamic output-feedback controller synthesis at all (and not use static state-feedback, for which data-driven synthesis methods exist)? One might imagine situations where all states can be measured in some scenarios (that is the “data recording setting”, as we refer to it in the paper), but using all state measurements in real-time for control might not be convenient or allowed.
For example, in power systems, both angles and frequencies are available for measurement, but in frequency control, only frequency measurements are typically used. In general, there might be a relatively large number of states, but the user desires to use only some (relatively small number) of them for control. Measurements of all states during the operation might be too expensive (many sensors are required to be integral part of the system) and requires suitable communication links form (possibly many) sensors to the controller, which might lead to fragility (i.e., to failures in sensing and communication) of the overall control system. Additionally, if full state measurements are not available, one could use subspace identification methods to obtain state estimates from input-output data recordings, but the state estimation error should be taken into account.
Finally, note that considered problem setting is the same as for most of the above mentioned methods for the synthesis of a static state-feedback controller.
Thus, the presented controller synthesis method is a generalization of these methods for obtaining a dynamic output-feedback controller, using the same assumptions. 


