\section{Preliminaries}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Dissipativity}
\label{SecDissipativity}
Consider a discrete time LTI system 
\begin{equation}
\label{Sys1}
\begin{pmatrix} x_{k+1} \\ z_k \end{pmatrix} =
\begin{pmatrix} A & B \\ C & D \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} x_k \\  w_k \end{pmatrix} 
\end{equation}
where $k \in \mathbb{N}_{\geq 0}$ is a time step, $x_k \in \mathbb{R}^n$, $w_k \in \mathbb{R}^p$ and $z_k \in \mathbb{R}^q$ represent the system state, input and output, respectively. We say that \eqref{Sys1} is strictly dissipative with respect to a quadratic supply function 
\begin{small}
$s(w_k,z_k)=
\begin{pmatrix}
w_k \\ z_k
\end{pmatrix}^\top
\begin{pmatrix}
-Q & -S \\
-S^\top & -R
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
w_k \\ z_k
\end{pmatrix}$ \end{small} if the following inequality holds
\begin{equation}
\label{Dissip2}
\begin{pmatrix}
I & 0 \\
A & B \\
0 & I\\
C & D  \\
\end{pmatrix}^\top
\begin{pmatrix}
-P & 0 & 0 & 0  \\
0& P & 0 & 0   \\
0 & 0 & Q & S \\
0 & 0 & S^\top & R\\
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
I & 0 \\
A & B \\
0 & I\\
C & D  \\
\end{pmatrix}
\prec 0, 
\end{equation}
in which case $V(x_k)=x_k^\top P x_k$ acts as a storage function.
If in addition we have $P\succ 0$ and $R\succeq 0$, then \eqref{Dissip2} implies stability of system \eqref{Sys1} since the Lyapunov inequality $A^\top P A-P\prec 0$ is incorporated in \eqref{Dissip2}.
