$
S_K:=\{ Z\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times n} | \begin{pmatrix}I & Z^\top \end{pmatrix} K \begin{pmatrix} I & Z^\top \end{pmatrix}^\top \succeq 0 \}.
$
Assume that inequalities $K_{22}\prec 0$ and $K_{11}-K_{12}K_{22}^{-1} K_{12}^\top \succeq 0$ hold.
Then, we have that
$
\begin{pmatrix}
I &
Z^\top
\end{pmatrix}
M
\begin{pmatrix}
I &
Z^\top
\end{pmatrix}^\top \succ 0 \text{ for all } Z\in S_K$,
if and only if there exist a real $\alpha \geq 0$  such that
$
M
-\alpha
K \succ 0$. 
\end{theorem}
 Note that we express Theorem~\ref{Theorem1} differently than Theorem 4.10 in \cite{c21}, and that if $K_{11}-K_{12}K_{22}^{-1} K_{12}^\top \succeq 0$ holds, then the set $S_K$ is a nonempty set.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Problem definition}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{The plant}
Consider the following discrete-time LTI system
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:3:11}
\begin{pmatrix}
x_{k+1} \\
z_k \\
y_k
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
A & B_1 & B \\
C_1 & D_1 & E  \\
C & F & 0 
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x_k\\
w_k\\
u_k \\
\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $x_k \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the state, $w_k \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the disturbance input, $u_k \in \mathbb{R}^m$ is the control input, $z_k\in \mathbb{R}^q$ is the controlled output and $y_k \in \mathbb{R}^l$ is the measurement signal which is available for control.
Next, consider the following dynamic output-feedback controller
\begin{equation}
\label{controller}
\begin{pmatrix}
x_{c,k+1} \\
u_k
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
A_{c} & B_c \\
C_c & D_c
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x_{c,k} \\
y_k
\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
where the controller is of the same order as the plant, i.e., we assume that  $x_{c,k} \in \mathbb{R}^n$. 
The system \eqref{eq:3:11} and the controller \eqref{controller} 
form the closed-loop system
\begin{equation}
\label{closed_loop}
\begin{pmatrix}
\xi_{k+1} \\
z_k
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\tilde{A} & \tilde{B} \\
\tilde{C} & \tilde{D} 
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\xi_k \\ w_k
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
where $\xi_k=\text{col}(x_k,x_{c,k})$  and
\begin{equation}
\label{closed_loop_matrices}
\begin{pmatrix}
\tilde{A} & \tilde{B} \\
\tilde{C} & \tilde{D} 
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\begin{array}{cc|c}
A+BD_cC & BC_c & B_1+BD_cF \\
B_cC & A_c & B_cF \\ \hline
C_1+ED_cC & EC_c & D_1+ED_cF 
\end{array}
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Problem definition and the data recording setting}
In our problem setting we assume that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the matrices $A$ and $B$ are unknown,
\item the matrices $B_1$, $C_1$, $D_1$, $E$, $C$ and $F$ are known.
\end{enumerate}
To compensate for the unknown model parameters, we assume that we have one recorded  finite time trajectory. This trajectory is obtained in the following data recording setting:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the system model structure \eqref{eq:3:11} is known; 
\item the unknown disturbance $w_k$ is bounded;
\item the control input $u_k$ and the state $x_k$ are known.
\end{enumerate}  
More precisely, we have knowledge of the following data matrices
\begin{equation}
\label{RecordedData}
\begin{split}
X_{+}&:=
\begin{pmatrix}
x_{1} & x_{2} & \cdots & x_{N}
\end{pmatrix}, \\
X_{-}&:=
\begin{pmatrix}
x_{0} & x_{1} & \cdots & x_{N-1}
\end{pmatrix}, \\
U_-&:=
\begin{pmatrix}
u_0 & u_1 & \cdots & u_{N-1}
\end{pmatrix}, 
\end{split}
\end{equation} 
which satisfy the equation
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:2:8}
X_{+}
= A X_-+BU_-+B_1W_-, 
\end{equation}
where the disturbance matrix $W_-$ represents bounded unknown noise data   
\begin{equation}
\label{NoiseData}
W_{-}
:=
\begin{pmatrix}
w_{0} & w_{2} & \cdots & w_{N-1} \\
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Note that this data recording setting that uses state data is only required  for the controller design procedure. Once the controller is designed, it uses only measurements of $y_k$ for control, as defined in \eqref{controller}. Furthermore, matrices $B_1$ and $F$ are used to model the influence of disturbance, while $C_1$, $D_1$ and $E$ are the user's choice for performance (as stated in \cite{c4}). Matrices of the output channel available for control are known, as is also the case for the state-feedback control synthesis methods presented in the literature.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{The unknown disturbance bound}
The disturbance\footnote{We prefer to use the term \emph{disturbance} instead of \emph{noise}, as the channel $w \rightarrow z$ is the performance channel on which we desire to impose a certain dissipativity condition, e.g. $H_\infty$ gain or passivity. In practice, during the operation of the system, the input $w$ is then often not only noise (which is typically assumed to be of a relatively small magnitude), but indeed disturbances (which can be relatively large in magnitude), e.g., unknown forces acting on a mechanical system. During the data recording we desire to isolate the system in a sense that the bound on the unknown disturbance $w$ is as small as possible (ideally, so that only small magnitude ``noise'' remains in $w$).} $w_k$ affecting data recording is assumed to be bounded. This is modelled using a quadratic matrix inequality imposed on $W_-$, as it is done in \cite{c6}. 
\begin{assumption}
\label{Assumption1}
The matrix $W_-$ from \eqref{NoiseData} satisfies the inequality  
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:2:6}
\begin{pmatrix}
\star
\end{pmatrix}
\underbrace{
\begin{pmatrix}
B_1 \Phi_{11}B_1^\top & B_1 \Phi_{12} \\
\Phi_{12}^\top B_1^\top & \Phi_{22}  \\
\end{pmatrix}}_{\displaystyle \Phi}
\begin{pmatrix}
I  \\ 
 W_-^\top B_1^\top\\
\end{pmatrix} \succeq 0,
\end{equation}
where $\Phi=\Phi^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{(n+N)\times(n+N)}$ is a known matrix with $\Phi_{22} \prec 0$.
 ~$\hfill{\Box}$   
\end{assumption}
Note that $\Phi_{22} \prec 0$ ensures that the set of matrices $W_-$ which satisfy \eqref{eq:2:6} is bounded, and that \eqref{eq:2:6} is equivalent to
\begin{small}
$
\begin{pmatrix}
\star
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\Phi_{11} & \Phi_{12} \\
\Phi_{12}^\top & \Phi_{22}  \\
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
I  \\ 
W_-^\top \\
\end{pmatrix} \succeq 0$
\end{small}
according to Remark~2 in \cite{c6}.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{The set of plants consistent with the data}
Let $\Sigma$ be the set of all matrices ($A$,$B$) which can explain the data\footnote{Loosely speaking, a smaller bound on $W_-$ implies that the set $\Sigma$ is smaller.} ($X_+$,$X_-$,$U_-$), that is
\begin{equation}
\label{SystemSet}
\begin{split}
\Sigma :=
\{(A,B) \,| 
 \eqref{eq:2:8} \text{ holds for some $W_-$ satisfying } \eqref{eq:2:6} \}. 
\end{split}
\end{equation}

\begin{assumption}
\label{Assumption2} The set $\Sigma$ is a nonempty set and we have
$\text{rank}(\text{col}(X_-,U_-))=n+m.$~$\hfill{\Box}$   
\end{assumption}
According to \cite{c1}, the  rank condition above is achieved if the input $(w_k, u_k)$ is persistently exciting of order $n+1$ and the pair $(A, \begin{pmatrix} B_1 & B \end{pmatrix})$ is controllable.
\begin{lemma}
\label{Lemma3}
Suppose that Assumption~\ref{Assumption2} holds. Then, the set $\Sigma$ is the set of all matrices ($A$,$B$) that satisfy the following inequality
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:2:3}
\begin{pmatrix}
\star
\end{pmatrix}
\underbrace{
\begin{pmatrix}
\tilde{\Phi}_{11} & \tilde{\Phi}_{12} & \tilde{\Phi}_{13} \\ 
\tilde{\Phi}_{12}^\top & \tilde{\Phi}_{22} & \tilde{\Phi}_{23} \\ 
\tilde{\Phi}_{13}^\top & \tilde{\Phi}_{23}^\top & \tilde{\Phi}_{33}
\end{pmatrix}}_{\displaystyle \tilde{\Phi}}
\begin{pmatrix}
I \\
A^\top \\
B^\top
\end{pmatrix}
\succeq 0
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\label{PhiDef}
\tilde{\Phi}
:=
\begin{pmatrix}
\star
\end{pmatrix}
\Phi
\begin{pmatrix}
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
I & 0 & 0\\
X_+^\top & -X_-^\top & -U_-^\top
\end{array}
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}~$\hfill{\Box}$  
\end{lemma}  
\begin{proof}
Substituting $B_1W_-$ from \eqref{eq:2:8} into \eqref{eq:2:6}, we obtain 
\eqref{eq:2:3}.
\end{proof}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{The control problem}
\label{TheControlProblem}
Finally, we present the considered control problem.
\begin{problem}
\label{Problem1}
Consider the system \eqref{eq:3:11}, and suppose that we have the following knowledge: the recorded data 
($X_+$,$X_-$,$U_-$) defined as in \eqref{RecordedData}; matrices $B_1$, $C_1$, $D_1$, $E$, $C$ and $F$; the disturbance bound specified by $\Phi$ in Assumption~\ref{Assumption1}; some $(A_s,B_s) \in \Sigma$ which can be calculated using expression \eqref{set_element} presented in the Appendix,  Section~\ref{AppendixB}. 
Suppose that Assumption~\ref{Assumption2} holds.
Let the supply function \begin{small}
$s(w_k,z_k)=
\begin{pmatrix}
w_k \\ z_k
\end{pmatrix}^\top
\begin{pmatrix}
-Q & -S \\
-S^\top & -R
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
w_k \\ z_k
\end{pmatrix}$ \end{small} be given with $R\succeq 0$.
Design a dynamic output-feedback controller \eqref{controller} such that the closed-loop system \eqref{closed_loop} is strictly dissipative\footnote{And therefore also stable, since $R\succeq 0$. See Section~\ref{SecDissipativity}.} with respect to the supply function $s(w_k,z_k)$ for all  $(A,B)\in \Sigma$.~$\hfill{\Box}$.      
\end{problem}
Note that if the desired dissipativity condition is achived in the performance channel $w \rightarrow z$, it indeed holds for all disturbance input signals $w$ (not only for $w$ which satisfy the bounds during the recording of data). Disturbances in practice can be relatively large, but during the data recording we desire to keep them small to reduce the set $\Sigma$ and therefore to possibly achieve better performance levels of the closed-loop (e.g., in the case of $H_\infty$ design).    
In the following section we present a solution to Problem~~\ref{Problem1}. 
