\noindent \emph{Step 4.}
Inequality \eqref{eq:600:301} implies that \begin{small}
$\begin{pmatrix}
X & I\\
I & Y
\end{pmatrix}\succ 0$\end{small}. Then if we use the Schur complement with respect to the matrix $X$ on the previous inequality, we obtain the equivalent statement $Y-X^{-1}\succ 0$ and $X\succ 0$, which implies that $I-XY$ is a non-singular matrix. Thus, the equation \eqref{eq:rekonstrukcija} implies that the matrix $UV^\top$ has full rank, which further implies that matrices $U$ and $V$ have full rank. Recall that $U$ can be an arbitrary full rank matrix according to different controller realization argumentation shown in 
the Appendix, Section~\ref{AppendixA}, which proves necessity. Since we can choose a full rank matrix $U$ and reconstruct the matrix $V$ and controller matrices using \eqref{eq:3:18},  we can reverse the argumentation to prove sufficiency, which establishes equivalence between statements \emph{i}) and \emph{ii}).
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
The inequality $\eqref{eq:600:301}$ is not a LMI in the decision variables, because of the term $\alpha X$. However, by fixing $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$, $\alpha > 0$, we get an LMI that can be solved multiple times in a line search procedure (as stated in \cite{R25} and  \cite{c4}).
In the case of $H_\infty$ control, a line search for $\alpha$ (e.g. using bisection) can be performed while minimizing the $H_\infty$ norm of the closed-loop system. In the case of a general supply rate, the zero on the right hand side in  \eqref{eq:600:301} can be replaced with $-\varepsilon I$ and a line search can be performed while minimizing the scalar $\varepsilon$ until $\varepsilon <0$ is eventually found, in which case \eqref{eq:600:301} is feasible.
 In this sense, the proposed solution of the considered controller synthesis problem is given in a form of convex optimization problems and therefore can be efficiently numerically solved. 
The matrix size in an LMI for the analogous model-based dynamic output-feedback controller synthesis is $4n + p +\tilde{q}$ (see e.g. \cite{R25}, Chapter 4), while the matrix in (14) is of size $(4n + p + \tilde{q}) + (n + m + \tilde{n})$.  
\end{remark}
