\section{EXAMPLE}
To illustrate the results we use the $H_\infty$ control example from \cite{c4}, along with the same data recording setting.
However, we assume that not all states variables are available for control, and $C=\begin{pmatrix} I_2 & 0_{2\times 1} \end{pmatrix}$, $F=0_{2\times 3}$ see \eqref{eq:3:11}.
To solve the corresponding semi-definite program, we used the Yalmip \cite{c22} environment in MATLAB, with Mosek as a LMI solver.
The results for both dynamic output and state $H_\infty$ feedback controllers  are presented in Figure~\ref{fig10},
where the latter is consistent with results reported in \cite{c4}. 
In a line search procedure using \eqref{eq:600:301}, we have determined the scalar variable $\alpha$ values $67.5$ and $71.5$ for the dynamic output and state feedback case, respectively, see Figure~\ref{fig11}.
The presented results show that: (i) the developed method is achieving good robust performance in the 
case when the output is not the full state, and (ii) it recovers the performance of the static state-feedback 
robust data-based control solutions when the output coincides with the full state.

