\begin{example}
Derivation of an alternate form for equation (2.1.2.4) in \cite{brychkov_m} in terms of the finite series involving the Hurwitz-Lerch zeta function.  This is an example of being able to evaluate the finite series over a wider range of values without having to consider the singularity of the Mellin transform. In this example we use equation (\ref{eq:thm2}) and set $k\to 0,v\to 1,b\to -\frac{1}{a},n\to n-1,m\to s$ and simplify;
\begin{multline}\label{eq:6.15}
\int_0^{\infty } \frac{x^{-1+s}}{(a-x)^n} \, dx
=-\sum _{j=0}^n \sum _{l=0}^j \frac{(-1)^{-j+l} i^{-l}
   \left(-\frac{1}{a}\right)^{-s} a^{-n} e^{i \pi  \left(\frac{1}{2}-n+s\right)} (2 \pi )^{1-l} (1-s)^{j-l}
   \binom{j}{l} }{(-1+n)!}\\ \times
\Phi \left(e^{2 i \pi  s},l,\frac{\pi -i \log \left(-a^2\right)}{2 \pi }\right) (1-l)_l
   S_{-1+n}^{(j)}\\
=-\frac{\left(\pi  (-a)^{s-n}\right) \left((1-n+s) (2-n+s)_{-1+n}\right)}{((n-1)! \sin (s
   \pi )) s}
\end{multline}
where $0 < Re(s) < n; n = 1,2,.., |\arg(-a)|<\pi$.
\end{example}
