We fix $(n-2)$-th column of $A$ and  for $i=1,2,\cdots,{n-3}$, we define $2\times 2$ sub matrix $A_i$ of $A$ such that first column of $A_i$ is the $i$-th column of $A$ and second column of $A_i$ the $(n-2)$-th column of $A$. Denote $\det(A_i)=\a_i$. Also, for $1 \leq i < j \leq n-3$, let $C_{ij}$ be the $2 \times 2$ submatrix of $B(\varphi'')$ such that the first and second columns of $C_{ij}$ are the $i$-th and $j$-th columns of $B(\varphi'')$. Denote $c_{ij}=\det(C_{ij})$.
Clearly $I_2(A)=(\a_1,\a_2,\cdots,\a_{n-3})+(\{c_{ij} ; 1\leq i <j \leq n-3 \})$.


