We aim at solving the partial integro differential equations appearing in Section 6.2.1, Section 6.4, Section 6.5.

\section{Section 6.2.1.}

We aim at solving the following PIDE:
\[\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} -\xi(\lambda-\lambda_0)\frac{\partial V}{\partial \lambda} + \lambda(V(t,\lambda+\beta,h)-V(t,\lambda,h)) + (v-S(h,v))\mathbb{E}[\eta]\lambda + \frac{1}{2\gamma}\left(e^{-\rho t}\frac{\partial V}{\partial h}-\delta \right)^2=0, \quad V(T,y)=0,\]
the optimal control is given by:
$z^*=\frac{e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}-\delta}{\gamma}$.

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\lambda_t\equiv 0$. It means we have no jumps, i.e. no attack arrives. 
    \[\frac{\partial V}{\partial t}  + \frac{1}{2\gamma}\left(e^{-\rho t}\frac{\partial V}{\partial h}-\delta \right)^2=0, \quad V(T,h)=0.\]
    The solution is given by:
    $V(t,\lambda, h)=-\frac{\delta^2}{2\gamma}t + \frac{\delta^2}{2\gamma}T$ and the optimal control by $z^*=-\delta/\gamma$. The interpretation is that as there is no attack, it means we are interested in maximizing
    \[\sup_{z\in \mathcal{Z} } \mathbb{E}\left[\int_0^t -\delta z_t -\frac{\gamma}{2} z_t^2 \rmd t\right].\]
    It means exactly, minimizing the cost function.

    \item $\lambda_t\equiv \lambda, \xi=0$.
\[\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} +  (v-S(h,v))\mathbb{E}[\eta]\lambda + \frac{1}{2\gamma}\left(e^{-\rho t}\frac{\partial V}{\partial h}-\delta \right)^2=0, \quad V(T,h)=0.\]

\item What if $S(v,h)=v$, i.e. we have no advantage in investing in security.
\[\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} -\xi(\lambda-\lambda_0)\frac{\partial V}{\partial \lambda} + \lambda(V(t,\lambda+\beta,h)-V(t,\lambda,h)) +  \frac{1}{2\gamma}\left(e^{-\rho t}\frac{\partial V}{\partial h}-\delta \right)^2=0, \quad V(T,y)=0.\]

It is equivalent to solve \[\sup_{z\in \mathcal{Z} } \mathbb{E}\left[\int_0^t -\delta z_t -\frac{\gamma}{2} z_t^2 \rmd t\right],\]
thus we will end up having a solution only depending on the cost function and time.

\end{itemize}

\section{Section 6.4.} We aim at solving the following:
\begin{align*}
&\frac{\partial V}{\partial t}  -\xi(\lambda-\lambda_0)\frac{\partial V}{\partial \lambda} + \lambda((1-(v-S(h,v)) (V(t,\ell,\lambda+\beta,h)-V(t,\ell,\lambda,h)) \\
&+(v-S(h,v))\int_0^\infty  (V(t,\ell+x_1,\lambda+\beta,h)-V(t,\ell,\lambda,h)) f_\eta(\rmd x_1))\\
&+ (c')^{-1} \left( e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}\right)e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}-c\left((c')^{-1} \left( e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}\right)\right)=0 \\
&V(T,\ell,\lambda,h)=U(\ell,\lambda,h).
\end{align*}

The PIDE is:
\begin{itemize}
\item First-order, time-dependent.
\item Three dimension + time.
\item Non-linear.
\end{itemize}
As a first tentative, we try to solve the PIDE using method of lines. See \url{https://reference.wolfram.com/language/tutorial/NDSolveMethodOfLines.html},\emph{''The numerical method of lines is a technique for solving partial differential equations by discretizing in all but one dimension and then integrating the semi-discrete problem as a system of ODEs or DAEs. A significant advantage of the method is that it allows the solution to take advantage of the sophisticated general-purpose methods and software that have been developed for numerically integrating ODEs and DAEs. For the PDEs to which the method of lines is applicable, the method typically proves to be quite efficient.''}

The idea is the following: discretize the derivative/ integral in time and then get a system of ODEs. The system is solved through classical methods.

\paragraph{First experiments.}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\lambda\equiv 0$. Mathematically, it means that we restrict to a one dimensional, non-linear, parabolic PDE (one spatial dimension + time). There is no integral part. 
\begin{align*}
&\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} + (c')^{-1} \left( e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}\right)e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}-c\left((c')^{-1} \left( e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}\right)\right)=0 \\
&V(T,\ell,0,h)=U(\ell,0,h).
\end{align*}

Practically, it means there are no cyber-attacks.
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $U(\ell, 0, h)$ only depends on $\ell$ - {\color{red} reasonable?} - then, we have that the solution has a multiplicative form. 
    $V(t,\ell, h)=U(\ell) \mathcal{V}(t,h)$, where $\mathcal{V}(t,h)$ solves

    \begin{align*}
&\frac{\partial  \mathcal{V}}{\partial t} + (c')^{-1} \left( e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial  \mathcal{V}}{\partial h}\right)e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial  \mathcal{V}}{\partial h}-c\left((c')^{-1} \left( e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial  \mathcal{V}}{\partial h}\right)\right)=0 \\
& \mathcal{V}(T,h)=1.
\end{align*}

For $c$ quadratic, $c(z)=\delta z + \frac{\gamma}{2} z^2, (c')^{-1}(z)=\frac{z-\delta}{\gamma}, c((c')^{-1}(z))=\frac{z^2-\delta^2}{2\gamma}$, 

\begin{align*}
&\frac{\partial  \mathcal{V}}{\partial t} + \frac{e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial  \mathcal{V}}{\partial h}^2-\delta e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial  \mathcal{V}}{\partial h}}{\gamma} - \frac{e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial  \mathcal{V}}{\partial h}^2-\delta^2}{2\gamma}=0 \\
& \mathcal{V}(T,h)=1,
\end{align*}
and the solution is given by $\mathcal{V}(t)=-\frac{-\delta^2}{2\gamma} t + \frac{-\delta^2}{2\gamma} T + 1$. There is dependency only on the time. Moreover, we have that the optimal control is constant and equal, when admissible, to $z^*=\frac{-\delta}{\gamma}$.
\end{itemize}

The solution applying the method of lines, approximating the derivative in $h$ with a forward increment.
\item $\lambda_t\equiv \lambda$, i.e. $\xi=0, \beta=0$. It corresponds to the case where the attacks arrivals are distributed as a Poisson process (no self-exciting features). In that case, the PIDE becomes 
\begin{align*}
&\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} + \lambda((v-S(h,v))\int_0^\infty  (V(t,\ell+x_1,\lambda,h)-V(t,\ell,\lambda,h)) f_\eta(\rmd x_1))\\
&+ (c')^{-1} \left( e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}\right)e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}-c\left((c')^{-1} \left( e^{-\rho t} \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}\right)\right)=0 \\
&V(T,\ell,\lambda,h)=U(\ell,\lambda,h).
\end{align*}
Solving the PIDE with the method of lines, in the case where $f_\eta(\rmd x_1)=\eta \delta_{0}(\rmd x_1)$ and in which we apply a forward scheme to approximate the derivatives, we end up getting many oscillations (instability). Let us approximate the derivative backward in space. It still oscillates.
\end{enumerate}



