\textit{Theoretical Foundation and validity}---In this context, 'well-defined bands' denotes models characterized by all bands intersecting the Fermi surface and featuring exclusively effective bilinear terms in the electron field (such as the Rashba model), apart from superconducting interactions. Our analysis shows that the fundamental diagnostic criterion governing the emergence of intrinsic SDE are established through the study of these models.

To describe the SDE phenomenologically, Ginzburg-Landau (GL) theory employs the free energy functional $F\left(\boldsymbol{q}\right)=\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}|\Delta_{\boldsymbol{q}}|^2+\left(\beta_{\boldsymbol{q}}/2\right)|\Delta_{\boldsymbol{q}}|^4$, with $\Delta_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ denoting the finite-momentum ($\boldsymbol{q}$) pairing order parameter. Non-reciprocity necessitates that $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ and $\beta_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ possess contributions odd in $\boldsymbol{q}$ \cite{VictorMEdelstein1996,RN98}. Our goal is to establish the diagnostic criterion permitting the necessary $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ term, because $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ constitutes the minimal term for SDE \cite{RN81,RN98}. Note that the microscopic origins of $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ and $\beta_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ are consistent. Decided by specific Hamiltonian model $H$, $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}=1/V-\chi(\boldsymbol{q})$. Here, $V$ is the intensity of superconducting interaction. The $\boldsymbol{q}$-dependent superconductivity susceptibility plays a decisive role in it, 
\begin{align}
	\chi(\boldsymbol{q})=k_BT\sum_{\boldsymbol{k},i\omega_n}&Tr\left[\mathscr{T}^{\dagger}(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})\mathcal{G}\left(\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},i\omega_n\right) \right. \notag \\
	&\left.\times\mathscr{T}(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})\mathcal{G}^T\left(-\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},-i\omega_n\right)\right]. \label{sym}
\end{align}
Within the phenomenological theory of superconductivity \cite{RN77,RN76,RN79}, the form factor for Cooper pairs, denoted by $\mathscr{T}(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})$, depends on the unit vector $\hat{\boldsymbol{k}}$ in momentum space. The Matsubara Green's function for the electron system described by $H(\boldsymbol{k})$ is given by $\mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{k},i\omega_n) = \left[i\omega_n - H(\boldsymbol{k})\right]^{-1}$, where $\omega_n = (2n+1)\pi k_B T$ is the fermionic Matsubara frequency at temperature $T$.

In evaluating Eq. (\ref{sym}), we note that the superconducting pairing strength $\Delta_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ in physical systems is significantly smaller than the band splitting $\Delta_{Spl}$ [i.e., $\Delta_{\boldsymbol{q}}\ll \Delta_{Spl} $, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig1} (c)]. As a result, bands far from the Fermi surface contribute negligibly to superconductivity, leading to an incomplete trace in the calculation of Eq. (\ref{sym}) [see Sec. S2 of the Supplementary Material (SM) \cite{SMs}].
To circumvent this limitation, we consider the opposite unphysical limit ($\Delta_{\boldsymbol{q}}\gg \Delta_{Spl} $), in which the split bands are approximated by a single Fermi surface. This allows for a complete trace computation in Eq. (\ref{sym}) (Sec. S4 in SM \cite{SMs}), and enables us to derive a general diagnostic criterion. Importantly, for the purpose of idensifying the SDE, the two limits are qualitatively equivalent. Using the latter approach, we establish a universal diagnostic criterion for the SDE.

In the band basis, the nonreciprocity-contributing term in $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ to the linear-$\boldsymbol{q}$ order can be expressed as a weighted sum over all bands (Sec. S3 in SM \cite{SMs}) $[N_{F,s}v_{F,s}(k_{F,s}) \Delta E_s]$ \cite{note1}, where $N_{F,s}$ is the density of states (DOS), $v_{F,s}(k_{F,s})$ is the Fermi velocity at Fermi wave vector $k_{F,s}$, and $\Delta E_s$ is the energy difference between 
$\pm \boldsymbol{k}$ on band $s$. Using the Rashba model as an example [with $\Delta_{\boldsymbol{q}}\ll \Delta_{Spl} $, Fig.  \ref{fig1} (c)], the SDE originates from the band antisymmetry about zero momentum.  In Fig.  \ref{fig1} (c), the net nonreciprocity arises from the imbalance in $N_{F,s}$ between two split bands \cite{RN81}. Since $v_{F,+}(k_{F,+})\approx v_{F,-}(k_{F,-})$, $\Delta E_{+}\approx-\Delta E_{-}$ and  $N_{F,s} \propto k_{F,s}/v_{F,s}(k_{F,s})$,
the dominant linear-$\boldsymbol{q}$-dependent contribution to $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ is proportional to $\sum_s k_{F,s}\Delta E_s$ and is governed by the lower band with the larger $k_F$ [Fig. \ref{fig1}(c)].

Extending analysis to the opposite limit $\Delta_{\boldsymbol{q}}\gg \Delta_{Spl} $ limit [Fig.  \ref{fig1} (d)], the approximation of a single Fermi surface [dashed parabolic band in Fig. \ref{fig1} (d)] simplifies the linear-$\boldsymbol{q}$-dependent term in $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ (referred to here as the fake '$\alpha _{\boldsymbol{q}}$'), making it proportional to $\sum_s v_{F,s}(k_{F})\Delta E_s$. Then, the higher band with the larger $v_F$ dominates [Fig. \ref{fig1}(d)]. This behavior arises because the minimization of free energy drives the system toward a finite-momentum pairing state, where the pairing momentum $\boldsymbol{q}$ takes non-zero value to compensate for the energy differences between eleactrons at  $\pm \boldsymbol{k}$ [manifested as the evolution from open-circle to solid-circle pairings in Fig. \ref{fig1} (d)].

Despite differences in the specific expressions where true $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ [Fig. \ref{fig1}(c)] and fake '$\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$' [Fig. \ref{fig1}(d)] may differ in magnitude and even sign (Sec. S2.A and S4 in SM). Both regimes exhibit qualitatively similar behavior. In both cases, the system evolves from zero-momentum to finite-momentum pairing to minimize the free energy. Therefore, the fake  '$\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$' provides a qualitatively valid foundation for deriving a general diagnostic criterion for the emergence of the SDE. 

