\textit{Criterion of SDE for well-defined bands.}---Most studies focus on s-wave spin-singlet states with form factor $\mathscr{T} = i\sigma^y$. When this factor is commuted to the right of the trace, it reveals a connection between SDE and time-reversal symmetry, typically defined as $\mathcal{T} = i\sigma^y\mathcal{K}$ (where $\mathcal{K}$ is complex conjugation). However, this specific form is not universally applicable, as $\mathcal{T}$ operator may act nontrivially on additional degrees of freedom (DOF), such as orbitals or valleys. Consequently, the properties of an $i\sigma^y$ superconducting state are not uniquely determined by $\mathcal{T}$ symmetry alone. To generalize the analysis, we adopt a general pairing form factor $\mathscr{T}(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})=i\Phi(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})\sigma^y$ to demonstrate our statement. Our analysis applies only when $\hat{T}=i\Phi(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})\sigma^y\mathcal{K}$ constitutes a well-defined transformation on the model's Hilbert space that possesses eigenvalues $\pm1$, analogous to conventional $\mathcal{T}$ symmetry. We term this operation effective time-reversal (ETR). The Hamiltonian decomposes into ETR-symmetric and antisymmetric components: $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H(-\boldsymbol{k})\hat{T}=H^{TS}(\boldsymbol{k})-H^{TA}(\boldsymbol{k})$. The $\boldsymbol{q}$-expansion of $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$ additionally depends on the symmetry $\hat{I}: \boldsymbol{k}\to-\boldsymbol{k}$, defined as the effective inversion (EI) operation. Assuming a general power-series expansion, $H(\boldsymbol{k})=\sum_{n_x,n_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}_{n_x,n_y}$, where $n_x,n_y$ are integers. $\mathcal{A}$ is $\boldsymbol{k}$-independent, $\{n_x,n_y\}$-dependent matrix, whose dimension is equal to that of effective spinor space. The product inside the trace of Eq. (\ref{sym}) can then be expressed as: $[i\omega_n-E^{TS,IS}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\right)-\left(\delta_1+\delta_2\right)]^{-1}[i\omega_n+E^{TS,IS}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\right)-\left(\delta_1-\delta_2\right)]^{-1}$. Refering to ETR and EI symmetries, the Hamiltonian can totally decompose into four parts: $H^{TS,IS}$, $H^{TS,IA}$, $H^{TA,IS}$ and $H^{TA,IA}$, where $S$($A$) means symmetric(antisymmetric). For well-defined models, we can always isolate a nonzero term from $H^{TS,IS}$. This term is proportional to the identity matrix $\boldsymbol{1}$ and defines $E^{TS,IS}$, which is the component yielding a single symmetric Fermi surface. The other terms are absorbed into the following, 
\begin{align}
	\delta_{\nu}=&\sum_{\substack{n_x,n_y\\l_x+ly=2m+\nu}}q_x^{l_x}q_y^{l_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}^{TS}_{n_x,l_x;n_y,l_y} \notag \\
	&+\sum_{\substack{n_x,n_y\\l_x+ly=2m+\nu-1}}q_x^{l_x}q_y^{l_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}^{TA}_{n_x,l_x;n_y,l_y}, \label{term}
\end{align}
where $\nu\in\{1,2\}$, and $m$ are integers. By defining Green's functions $\mathcal{G}^{e(h)}=[i\omega_n-(+)E^{TS,IS}]^{-1}$, they are also proportional to $\boldsymbol{1}$, the full product becomes $\mathcal{G}^h[1-\mathcal{G}^e\left(\delta_1+\delta_2\right)-\left(\delta_1-\delta_2\right)\mathcal{G}^h+\mathcal{G}^e\left(\delta_1+\delta_2\right)\left(\delta_1-\delta_2\right)\mathcal{G}^h]^{-1}\mathcal{G}^e$. The nonzero value must be expanded in even powers of $\mathcal{G}$, therefore our task reduces to determining whether $\left(\delta_1+\delta_2\right)^{n_+}\left(\delta_1-\delta_2\right)^{n_-}$ contributes any odd-order term in $\boldsymbol{q}$, where $n_+$ and $n_-$ are integers satisfying $n_+ + n_- = \text{even}$.
 If such terms exist, they signal the emergence of nonreciprocity and the presence of the SDE.

In systems respecting ETR symmetry, $\mathcal{A}^{TA} = 0$. Terms proportional to $\delta_1^2$ or $\delta_2^2$ cannot yield odd-order $\boldsymbol{q}$ contributions. To analyze contributions from mixed terms $\delta_1\delta_2$, we exploit the decomposition of the ETR operation via EI symmetry: $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H(-\boldsymbol{k})\hat{T}=\hat{T}^{\dagger}\hat{I}H(\boldsymbol{k})\hat{I}\hat{T}$. This implies the transformation properties, $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H^{TS,IS}\hat{T}=H^{TS,IS}$ and $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H^{TS,IA}\hat{T}=-H^{TS,IA}$. Consequently, traces containing odd powers of $H^{TS,IA}$ vanish. Combined with the requirement that $n_x + n_y$ must be even for nonzero Fermi surface averaging, we conclude that SDE cannot emerge without ETR symmetry breaking. Similarly, systems that preserve EI symmetry contribute only even-order momentum terms in the expansion. Notably, in Eq. (\ref{sym}), the orders of $\boldsymbol{q}$ and $\boldsymbol{k}$ consistently share the same parity (both even or both odd). Hence, SDE also cannot emerge without EI symmetry breaking. Thus, simultaneous co-breaking of both ETR and EI symmetries is a necessary (though not sufficient) condition for intrinsic SDE. Within Eq. (\ref{term}), we distinguish two types of contributions: (a) Single symmetry-breaking terms $H^{TS,IA}$ (EI-breaking) and $H^{TA,IS}$ (ETR-breaking); (b) Double symmetry-breaking terms, $H^{TA,IA}$ (ETR- and EI-breaking). We summarize the general criterion using the following two inequalities:
\begin{align}
	Tr\left[\prod_{\zeta,\zeta'\in\{S,A\}}\left(H^{T\zeta,I\zeta'}\right)^{2n_{\zeta\zeta'}}H^{TS,IA}H^{TA,IS}\right]\neq0, \label{Eq1} \\
	Tr\left[\prod_{\zeta,\zeta'\in\{S,A\}}\left(H^{T\zeta,I\zeta'}\right)^{2n_{\zeta\zeta'}}H^{TS,IS}H^{TA,IA}\right]\neq 0. \label{Eq2}
\end{align}
The integers $n_{\zeta\zeta'}$ are model-dependent parameters that generate all possible matrix product combinations \cite{note2}. For most simple models (e.g., those listed in Table \ref{table}), setting $n_{\zeta\zeta'}=0$ suffices. Importantly, $H^{TS,IS}$ here differs from $E^{TS,IS}$, since it may include additional ETR- and EI-symmetric terms not proportional to $\boldsymbol{1}$. 

The inequalities in Eqs. (\ref{Eq1}) and (\ref{Eq2}) constitute a central result of this work: intrinsic SDE can emerge if either condition is satisfied. We emphasize that this criterion is based on ETR and EI symmetries, rather than physical symmetries $\mathcal{T}$ and $\mathcal{P}$, though they coincide in minimal models. Crucially, ETR symmetry is defined in relation to the specific pairing form. For example, while an Ising SOC term in valley systems preserves $\mathcal{T}$ and violates $\mathcal{P}$, we classify it as $H^{TS,IS}$ under our ETR definition $\hat{T} = \eta^y\sigma^y\mathcal{K}$ (See Sec. S1 of SM \cite{SMs} for details), where $(\eta_0,\boldsymbol{\eta})$ span valley space. Consequently, these inequalities show valley-dependent and valley-independent terms typically contribute through distinct channels. As demonstrated in Ref. \cite{RN290}, SDE cannot arise from Ising SOC and a Zeeman field alone.

\textit{SDE for effective bands.}---Beyond the well-defined bands discussed previously, exceptional cases may arise, typically in multi-orbital or multi-band systems like the unconventional Rashba model (URM) \cite{PhysRevB.109.195419,PhysRevB.110.134517}. In such systems, the Fermi surfaces intersect only a subset of bands, rendering our earlier analysis inapplicable. To demonstrate the microscopic origin of SDE through band asymmetry, we examine the URM, a perspective already used to validate our criterion. This model is described by Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
	H^{UR}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\right)=\xi_{\boldsymbol{k}}\sigma^0\tau^0+\lambda_{R}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\times\boldsymbol{\sigma}\right)\cdot\hat{\boldsymbol{z}}\left(\tau^0+\varepsilon\tau^x\right)+\lambda\sigma^z\tau^y, 
\end{equation}
where the Pauli matrices $\left(\tau^0,\boldsymbol{\tau}\right)$ span the orbital space. When the system is placed in an in-plane magnetic field, we add the Zeeman term $\boldsymbol{B}\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}$ and get the eigenenergies to the first order of $B$ as
\begin{align}
	E^{UR}_{s_1,s_2}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{B}\right)=&\xi_{\boldsymbol{k}}+s_1\sqrt{\lambda^2+\lambda_{R}^2k^2}+s_2\varepsilon\lambda_{R}k \notag \\
	&+s_1\frac{\lambda_{R}\boldsymbol{k}}{\sqrt{\lambda^2+\lambda_{R}^2k^2}}\cdot\left(\boldsymbol{B}\times\hat{\boldsymbol{z}}\right).
\end{align}
The signs $\left(s_1,s_2\right)\in\{+,-\}$ label the four splitting bands. If we choose the Fermi energy to cross all the bands, we can see the upper two bands $\left(+,\pm\right)$ and lower two bands $\left(-,\pm\right)$ are shifted by the field in opposite directions, $\delta \boldsymbol{k}_{F}^{s_1,s_2}=-s_1[\lambda_{R}k_F^{s_1,s_2}/\sqrt{\lambda^2+\lambda_{R}^2\left(k_F^{s_1,s_2}\right)^2}]\left(\boldsymbol{B}\times\hat{\boldsymbol{z}}\right)/v_F^{s_1,s_2}$. The offset of the Fermi momenta is caused by the response to the field of the bands' spin textures, $\boldsymbol{S}_{s_1,s_2}=s_1(\lambda_{R}/\sqrt{\lambda^{2}+\lambda_{R}^{2}k^{2}})(k_{x}\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-k_{y}\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})$. 

We now turn to the case of URM, $\xi_{\boldsymbol{k}}=tk^2-\mu+\lambda$ ($\mu<\lambda$), where the chemical potential lies below the upper two bands, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig2}(a). Analogical to conventional Rashba model, electron states at $\pm \boldsymbol{k}$ still forms spin-singlet pairs, but obviously exhibit a nonzero Fermi center offset. This suggests that spin-singlet superconducting states should display SDE. Rather than standard computation (detailed in SM \cite{SMs}), we employ a simplified approach to consider only the intra-band superconductivity interaction, and write the model in band base
\begin{align}
	H_{UR,SC}=&\sum_{\boldsymbol{k},s}E^{UR}_{-,s}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{B}\right)c^{\dagger}_{\boldsymbol{k}s}c_{\boldsymbol{k}s} \notag \\
	&-V\sum_{\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{k'},s}c^{\dagger}_{\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},s}c^{\dagger}_{-\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},s}c_{-\boldsymbol{k'}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},s}c_{\boldsymbol{k'}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},s}.
\end{align}
This Hamiltonian yields the GL free energy for the order parameter $\Delta_{\boldsymbol{q}}=-V\sum_{\boldsymbol{k},s}\langle c_{-\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},s}c_{\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},s}\rangle$, with coefficient $\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}=1/V-k_BT\sum_{\boldsymbol{k},i\omega_n,s}\mathcal{G}_s(\boldsymbol{k}+\boldsymbol{q}/2,i\omega_n)\mathcal{G}_s(-\boldsymbol{k}+\boldsymbol{q}/2,-i\omega_n)$. Here, $\mathcal{G}_s(\boldsymbol{k},i\omega_n)=1/\left[i\omega_n-E^{UR}_{-,s}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{B}\right)\right]$ is the electron Matsubara Green's function. To the first order of $\boldsymbol{B}$, the coefficient is
\begin{align}
	\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}=&\frac{1}{V}+N_F\left(\tilde{\chi}_2+\frac{1}{4}\tilde{\chi}_4v_F^2q^2\right) \notag \\
	&-N_F\left(\tilde{\chi}_4+\frac{1}{8}\tilde{\chi}_6v_F^2q^2\right)\frac{\lambda_Rk}{\sqrt{\lambda^2+\lambda_R^2k^2}}v_F\boldsymbol{q}\cdot\left(\boldsymbol{B}\times\hat{\boldsymbol{z}}\right). \label{alpha1}
\end{align}
Comparing this result with the one for the Rashba systems, one can notice that the only difference is to replace $\lambda_{R}$ (See SM \cite{SMs} and \cite{RN81}) with $(\lambda_Rk/\sqrt{\lambda^2+\lambda_R^2k^2})v_F$. Thus, the URM can exist SDE with efficiency $\gamma\propto(\lambda_Rk/\sqrt{\lambda^2+\lambda_R^2k^2})v_FB$. Critically, in conventional Rashba model, the Fermi surface offset  is $\delta\boldsymbol{k}_s=-s(\boldsymbol{B}\times\hat{\boldsymbol{z}})/v_F$, and SDE arises from the difference between the shifts of different bands. In contrast, in URM, SDE arises from the sum of identical-direction Fermi surface shifts. This difference stems from the opposite directionality of Fermi surface responses in conventional versus unconventional Rashba systems.

