	In this supplemental material, we use the valleytronic model for an example to demonstrate the superconducting diode effect (SDE) criterion in Sec. \ref{valley}. Then we show the detailed derivations for the SDE free energy in the standard (Sec. \ref{standard}) and simplified (Sec. \ref{simplified}) methods, respectively. Finally in Sec. \ref{fake}, we show the fake '$\alpha_{\boldsymbol{q}}$' calculated by Eq. (1) in the main text, for the Rashba system.
	\section{Criterion for Valleytronic SDE\label{valley}}
	The valleytronic Hamiltonian decomposes into symmetric and antisymmetric components:
	\begin{align}
		H_{\text{valley}}(\boldsymbol{k}) = H_o(\boldsymbol{k}) + \eta^z H_v(\boldsymbol{k}),
	\end{align}
	where, $H_o(\boldsymbol{k})$ represents valley-symmetric terms, $H_v(\boldsymbol{k})$ contains valley-antisymmetric contributions, $\eta^z$ ($=\pm 1$) is the valley Pauli matrix indexing states near $\pm\boldsymbol{K}$ valleys. Cooper pairs in these systems are constructed by electrons between valleys, such as the singlet configuration $c_{-\boldsymbol{K}-\boldsymbol{k},\downarrow}c_{\boldsymbol{K}+\boldsymbol{k},\uparrow}-c_{-\boldsymbol{K}-\boldsymbol{k},\uparrow}c_{\boldsymbol{K}+\boldsymbol{k},\downarrow}-c_{\boldsymbol{K}-\boldsymbol{k},\uparrow}c_{-\boldsymbol{K}+\boldsymbol{k},\downarrow}+c_{\boldsymbol{K}-\boldsymbol{k},\downarrow}c_{-\boldsymbol{K}+\boldsymbol{k},\uparrow}$. If we write the electron field as $c_{\boldsymbol{k},\eta,\sigma}\equiv c_{\eta\boldsymbol{K}+\boldsymbol{k},\sigma}$, this pairing form is denoted by $-\eta^y\sigma^y$, where Pauli matrix $\sigma^y$ is in the spin space. We can still use Eq. (1) in the main text to decide the symmetry requirement for SDE, 
	\begin{align}
		k_BT\sum_{\boldsymbol{k},i\omega_n}Tr\left[\mathscr{T}^{\dagger}(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})\mathcal{G}\left(\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},i\omega_n\right)\mathscr{T}(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})\mathcal{G}^T\left(-\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},-i\omega_n\right)\right], \label{syms}
	\end{align}
	and the pairing form factor should be changed to $\mathscr{T}(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})=\Phi(\hat{\boldsymbol{k}})\eta^y\sigma^y$. Now the Green function is $\mathcal{G}\left(\boldsymbol{k},i\omega_n\right)=\left[i\omega_n-H_{\text{valley}}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\right)\right]^{-1}$. Considering the effective time reversal (ETR) transformation $\hat{T}=\mathscr{T}\mathcal{K}$ with eigenvalues $\pm1$, where $\mathcal{K}$ is the complex conjugation, the transformation is defined as $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H\left(-\boldsymbol{k}\right)\hat{T}=H^{TS}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\right)-H^{TA}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\right)$. The superscripts $TS$ and $TA$ denote the symmetric and antisymmetric components under ETR, respectively. We get
	\begin{align}
		\mathscr{T}\mathcal{G}^T\left(-\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2},-i\omega_n\right)\mathscr{T}^{\dagger}=-\left[i\omega_n+H^{TS}\left(\boldsymbol{k}-\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2}\right)-H^{TA}\left(\boldsymbol{k}-\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2}\right)\right]^{-1}.
	\end{align}
	An arbitrary Hamiltonian expands to infinite series of momentum: $H(\boldsymbol{k})=\sum_{n_x,n_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}_{n_x,n_y}$, where $n_x,n_y$ are integers and $\mathcal{A}_{n_x,n_y}$ are matrices acting on spinor space. Further, the $\boldsymbol{q}$-dependence is written as
	\begin{align}
		H\left(\boldsymbol{k}+\frac{\boldsymbol{q}}{2}\right)&=\sum_{l_x,l_y,n_x,n_y}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{l_x+l_y}\frac{\left(n_x+l_x\right)!}{l_x!n_x!}\frac{\left(n_y+l_y\right)!}{l_y!n_y!}q_x^{l_x}q_y^{l_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}_{l_x+n_x,l_y+n_y} \notag \\
		&\equiv\sum_{l_x,l_y,n_x,n_y}q_x^{l_x}q_y^{l_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}_{l_x,n_x;l_y,n_y}.
	\end{align}
	Therefore, the summation (\ref{syms}) becomes to
	\begin{align}
		-k_BT\sum_{\boldsymbol{k},i\omega_n}Tr\left\{\left[i\omega_n-E^{TS,IS}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\right)-\left(\delta_1+\delta_2\right)\right]^{-1}\left[i\omega_n+E^{TS,IS}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\right)-\left(\delta_1-\delta_2\right)\right]^{-1}\right\},
	\end{align}
	in which the terms are
	\begin{align}
		&E^{TS,IS} \in H^{TS,IS} = \sum_{n_x+n_y=2m}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}^{TS},\quad E^{TS,IS}\propto\boldsymbol{1}, \\
		\delta_{1}&=\sum_{\substack{n_x,n_y\\l_x+ly=2m+1}}q_x^{l_x}q_y^{l_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}^{TS}+\sum_{\substack{n_x,n_y\\l_x+ly=2m}}q_x^{l_x}q_y^{l_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}^{TA}, \\
		\delta_{2}&=\sum_{\substack{n_x,n_y\\l_x+ly=2m}}q_x^{l_x}q_y^{l_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}^{TS}+\sum_{\substack{n_x,n_y\\l_x+ly=2m+1}}q_x^{l_x}q_y^{l_y}k_x^{n_x}k_y^{n_y}\mathcal{A}^{TA}.
	\end{align}
	Here, $\boldsymbol{1}$ is the unit matrix. We omit the subscripts $l_x,l_y,n_x,n_y$ in the tersor matrices $\mathcal{A}$, since we only concern about the qualitative result. $m$ is nonnegtive integer, while the first term in $\delta_{2}$ should exclude the terms in $E^{TS,IS}$ when $m=0$. Notably, "$IS$" here is not the physical inversion $\mathcal{P}$ [$\mathcal{P}H(\boldsymbol{k})\mathcal{P}=\eta^xH(-\boldsymbol{k})\eta^x$] symmetric notation, but the notation for effective inversion (EI) $\hat{I}$ [$\hat{I}H(\boldsymbol{k})\hat{I}=H(-\boldsymbol{k})$]. We choose this defination, to make the SDE criterion inequality be in the same form as the main text. The following is the consideration of the contribution from $\left(\delta_1+\delta_2\right)^{n_+}\left(\delta_1-\delta_2\right)^{n_-}$, where $n_++n_-$ is an arbitrary even integer.

	Firstly, we decompose the ETR into two sequent operations $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H(-\boldsymbol{k})\hat{T}=\hat{T}^{\dagger}\hat{I}H(\boldsymbol{k})\hat{I}\hat{T}$. This leads to the following relations: $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H^{TS,IS}\hat{T}=H^{TS,IS}$, $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H^{TA,IA}\hat{T}=H^{TA,IA}$,  $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H^{TS,IA}\hat{T}=-H^{TS,IA}$, $\hat{T}^{\dagger}H^{TA,IS}\hat{T}=-H^{TA,IS}$. These relations are useful to considerate if there is nonzero contribution from the trace. We list the terms from $\delta_{1}^2$, $\delta_{2}^2$ and $\delta_{1}\delta_{2}$ in Table \ref{Ts}. The terms in Table \ref{Ts} are complete for arbitrary $\left(n_+,n_-\right)$, since the latter ones all in forms of the former ones times $\prod_{\zeta,\zeta'\in\{S,A\}}(\mathcal{A}^{T\zeta,I\zeta'})^{2m_{\zeta\zeta'}}$, where $m_{\zeta\zeta'}$s are arbitrary integers. [For example, a term in $\delta^4$ (\{o,e\}) can come from the product of $\mathcal{A}^{TS,IA}\mathcal{A}^{TA,IS}$ (\{o,e\}) and $\mathcal{A}^{TS,IS}\mathcal{A}^{TA,IS}$ (\{e,e\}).] For the sake that the trace property only depends on spinor space, we can replace the tenser matrices $\mathcal{A}$ with Hamiltonian terms. The contribution to SDE should come from the terms with odd number of $l_x+l_y$ and even number of $n_x+n_y$, thus we conclude the criterion to be the satisfication of either of the following inequalities,
	\begin{align}
		Tr\left[\prod_{\zeta,\zeta'\in\{S,A\}}\left(H^{T\zeta,I\zeta'}\right)^{2n_{\zeta\zeta'}}H^{TS,IS}H^{TA,IA}\right]\neq 0, \\
		Tr\left[\prod_{\zeta,\zeta'\in\{S,A\}}\left(H^{T\zeta,I\zeta'}\right)^{2n_{\zeta\zeta'}}H^{TS,IA}H^{TA,IS}\right]\neq 0.
	\end{align}
	Here, the integer $n_{\zeta\zeta'}$s are definite numbers for the give model, which produces enough forms of the matrices. 

