\section{Introduction}

\noindent Let $X$ be a Banach space with a Schauder basis. In \cite{R2}, R. Ryan raised the question of determining under what conditions the monomials of degree $n$ with respect to this basis form a Schauder basis for the space of $n$-homogeneous polynomials on $X$, denoted by $\mathcal{P}(^nX)$. Ryan proved that if the given basis is shrinking, and if $X$ satisfies the Dunford-Pettis property, then for each $n$, the monomials of degree $n$, with their natural ordering, form a Schauder basis for the Banach space $\mathcal{P}(^nX)$, where the norm is defined by uniform convergence on the unit ball of $X$. The Banach space $c_0$, with its standard basis, is an example to which this result applies. Furthermore, in \cite{R2}, R. Ryan also showed that for any Banach space $X$ with a Schauder basis, the monomials of degree $n$, with the natural ordering, form a Schauder basis for $(\mathcal{P}(^nX), \tau_0)$, where $\tau_0$ denotes the compact-open topology on $\mathcal{P}(^nX)$.

Considering $X$ as a Banach space with a shrinking basis, V. Dimant and S. Dineen proved in \cite{DD} that the monomials of degree $n$, with the square order, form a Schauder basis for $(\mathcal{P}_\omega(^nX), \norma{.})$, where $\mathcal{P}_\omega(^nX)$ denotes the subspace of $\mathcal{P}(^nX)$ consisting of all polynomials that are weakly continuous on bounded subsets of $X$.

Let $X$ be a Banach space with an unconditional basis $(e_j)$ and biorthogonal functionals $(e^*_j)$. In \cite{DK}, A. Defant and N. Kalton proved that for all $n\in \mathbb{N}$, the monomials form an unconditional basic sequence in $\mathcal{P}(^nX)$ if and only if $\mathcal{P}(^nX)$ is isomorphic to a Banach lattice. They also established that for every $n\in \mathbb{N}$, the monomials form an unconditional basic sequence in $\mathcal{P}_{A}(^nd(w,1))$, and consequently, $\mathcal{P}(^nd(w,1))$ is isomorphic to a Banach lattice. Here, $\mathcal{P}_{A}(^nd(w,1))$ denotes the space of all $n$-homogeneous polynomials on $d(w,1)$ that are approximable (as defined in \cite{D2}).

This topic has received significant attention in the literature.  R. Ryan \cite{R1} showed that the monomials form an equicontinuous unconditional Schauder basis for the space $(\mathcal{H}(\ell_1), \tau_0)$ of holomorphic functions on $\ell_1$, endowed with the topology of uniform convergence on compact sets. To prove this, Ryan used a simple characterization of the compact subsets of $\ell_1$: a set $K\subset \ell_1$ is relatively compact if and only if $\lim_{n\rightarrow \infty} \sum^{\infty}_{k=n}\modulo{z_k}=0$ uniformly for $z\in K$. Using this characterization, a fundamental system of compact sets in $\ell_1$ was defined, which allowed the achievement of the result. 




In $2012$, Dineen and Mujica \cite{DM} proved that the monomials form a Schauder basis for the space $\mathcal{H}(c_0)$ and $\mathcal{H}_b(c_0)$, both with their natural topologies. So, it is natural to ask whether analogous results hold for more general Banach spaces.


We refer to the papers \cite{DK}, \cite{DD}, \cite{DM}, \cite{GMR}, \cite{R1} and \cite{V} for the background on the topic of monomial basis for the holomorphic functions.


In the present article, we extend the previously mentioned result to two classes of Banach spaces. Specifically, we consider
$Z=c_0\left(\bigoplus^\infty_{i=1}\ell^i_p \right)$ for some $p\in [1, \infty)$, and $Z=d_*(w,1)$, where $d_*(w,1)$ denotes the predual of the Lorentz sequence space $d(w,1)$ and prove that the monomials form a Schauder basis for the space $(\mathcal{H}(Z), \tau_0)$. For this, we use the $\mathcal{S}_{*}$-absolute decompositions of locally convex spaces, finite dimensional decompositions of a Banach space and a Schauder basis. We show that the basis constant relative to the monomial basis on the spaces of $n$-homogeneous polynomials on $Z$ have at most exponential growth. The main result is obtained by applying  \cite[Theorem 1]{DM}.

The paper is organized as follows: in $\S 2$ we outline our notation and definitions and recall some properties of Banach spaces and locally convex spaces. $\S 3$ is concerned with decompositions of the spaces of holomorphic functions and the monomial basis. $\S 4$ we characterize the compact subsets of the complex Banach spaces $c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right)$, $p\in[1,\infty)$ and the pre-dual of the Lorentz sequence space $d_{*}(w,1)$. This made it possible to define families of semi-norms that generates the topologies $\tau_0$ on different spaces of holomorphic functions. The main result is proved in $\S5$.

\section{Notation and definitions}

All the spaces considered are complex Banach spaces or, more generally, complex locally convex spaces. Let $X$ be a Banach space. A function $f:X\rightarrow \mathbb{C}$ is holomorphic if there exists a sequence $(P_n)\subset \mathcal{P}(^nX)$ such that the series $\sum^{\infty}_{n=1}P_n(x)$ converges to $f(x)$ for every $x\in X$. $\mathcal{H}(X)$ denotes the vector space of holomorphic functions from $X$ into $\mathbb{C}$. $\mathcal{H}_b(X)$ denotes the subspace of $\mathcal{H}(X)$ consisting of holomorphic functions of bounded type, that is, holomorphic functions that are bounded in every bounded subset of $X$. We denote by $B(z,\varepsilon)$ the open ball with center in $z$ and radius $\varepsilon$ and by $B$ the closed unit ball of $X$. The closure of a subset $A\subset X$ will be denoted by $\hbox{cl}(A)$.

We now consider the locally convex topologies on $\mathcal{H}(X)$. $\tau_0$ will denote the locally convex topology of uniform convergence on compact subsets of $X$. The Nachbin topology, $\tau_\omega$, is the locally convex topology generated by the seminorms ported by the compact subsets of $X$. $\tau_b$ denotes the locally convex topology of uniform convergence on the bounded subsets of $X$. We refer the book \cite{D2} for the details concerning the topologies $\tau_0$, $\tau_\omega$ and $\tau_b$. 





We denote by  $\mathbb{N}^{(\mathbb{N})}=\left\{m=(m_k)^{\infty}_{k=1}: \, m_k\in \mathbb{N}, \, m_k=0\,  \text{for}\, k\, \text{sufficiently large} \right\}$ the set of the multi-indices. For $m\in \mathbb{N}^{(\mathbb{N})}$, the degree of $m$ is $\modulo{m}=\sum^{\infty}_{k=1}m_k$ and the lenght of $m$ is $l(m)=\sup\left\{k\in \mathbb{N}: \, m_k\neq 0 \right\}$. 

A sequence $(e_j)$ in a Banach space $X$ is called a Schauder basis for $X$ if for each $x\in X$ there is a sequence of scalars $(x_n)$ such that $x=\sum^{\infty}_{n=1}x_ne_n$.

Let $X$ be a Banach space with Shauder basis $(e_j)$. The sequence $(e^{*}_j)$ denote the coefficient functionals, i. e., $e^{*}_j\left(\sum^{\infty}_{i=1}z_ie_i \right)=z_j$, for all $j\in \mathbb{N}$ and $\sum^{\infty}_{i=1}z_ie_i\in X$. Finite products of coefficient functionals are called monomials relative to the basis $(e_j)$ or simply monomials. That is, for $m\in \mathbb{N}^{(\mathbb{N})}$ a monomial is the mapping $\sum^{\infty}_{i=1}z_ie_i\mapsto z^m=(e^{*}_1)^{m_1}\ldots (e^{*}_1)^{m_s}(z)=z^{m_1}_1 \ldots z^{m_s}_s$, where $m_k=0$, for all $k>s$. For each positive integer $k$ and $n$, we denote by $\mathcal{P}_k(^nX)=\left\{ \sum a_mz^m: \, m\in \mathbb{N}^{(\mathbb{N})}, \, \modulo{m}=n,\, l(m)=k \right\}$ the finite dimensional subspace of $\mathcal{P}(^nX)$ generated by the monomials $z^m$ of lenght $k$ and degree $n$.

We say that a Schauder basis $(e_i)$ for $X$ is schrinking if $(e^*_i)$ is a basis for $X^*$. 

We denote by $c_0$ the Banach space of all scalar sequences that converge to zero endowed with the sup norm and $c_0^+=\{ (z_j)_{j=1}^\infty\in c_0: \, z_j\geq 0\, \hbox{for all}\, j\}$.

Let $p\in [1, \infty)$. We define the Banach space 
\begin{equation*}
c
_0\left(\bigoplus^\infty_{i=1}\ell^i_p \right)=\left\{ (z_i)\in \mathbb{C}^\mathbb{N}: \, \left( \left( \sum_{i\in I(n)}\modulo{z_i}^p\right)^{1/p}\right)_n\in c_0 \right\},
\end{equation*}
with the norm $\norma{z}=\sup \left\{ \left( \sum_{i\in I(n)}\modulo{z_i}^p\right)^{1/p}: \, n \in \mathbb{N} \right\}$, where for every $n\in \mathbb{N}$, $I(n)=\{l\in \mathbb{N}: s(n-1)+1\leq l\leq s(n) \}$ with $s:\mathbb{N}_0\rightarrow \mathbb{N}_0$ the auxiliar function defined by $s(n)=0$ if $n=0$ and $s(n)=1+2+\ldots+n$ if $n\neq 0$. Each $z\in c
_0\left(\bigoplus^\infty_{i=1}\ell^i_p \right) $ can be represented by $z=\sum^\infty_{n=1}\sum_{k\in I(n)}z_ke_k$ where $(e_j)$ is the standard basis of $c
_0\left(\bigoplus^\infty_{i=1}\ell^i_p \right)$.

Let $w=(w_i)$ be a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers such that $w\in c_0\setminus \ell_1$. The Lorentz sequence space $d(w,1)$ is the Banach space of all scalar sequences $z=(z_i)$ such that $\norma{z}=\sup\{\sum^\infty_{i=1}\modulo{z_{\pi(i)}}w_i: \, \pi \}<\infty$, where the supremum is taken over all permutations $\pi$ of natural numbers. 

Let $z=(z_i)$ be a sequence. We denote by $([z]_i)$ the decreasing rearrangement of the sequence $(\modulo{z_i})$. The predual of the Lorentz sequence space $d(w,1)$ is the Banach space defined by
\begin{equation*}
    d_*(w,1)=\left\{(z_i)\in c_0: \lim_{k\rightarrow \infty}\frac{\sum^{k}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{k}_{i=1}w_i}=0\right\},
\end{equation*}  
with the norm $\norma{z}_*=\sup \left\{ \frac{\sum^{k}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{k}_{i=1}w_i}: \, k \in \mathbb{N} \right\}$.

\section{Monomial basis and decompositions}

In this section, we study a specific type of decomposition for a locally convex space $E$, referred to as the $\mathcal{S}_*$-absolute decomposition. We will present a result, proven by S. Dineen and J. Mujica in \cite{DM}, which guarantees, under certain conditions, that a locally convex space $E$ admits a Schauder basis. The concept of square ordering on monomials will be introduced at the end of the section.

\begin{definition}\cite{DM}
 Let $E$ be a locally convex space. A sequence of subspaces $\{E_n\}^{\infty}_{n=1}\subset E$ is called 
\begin{itemize}
     \item[a)] a decomposition for $E$ if for every $x\in E$ there exists a unique sequence  $(x_n)$ with $x_n\in E_n$ for all $n\in \mathbb{N}$, such that $x=\sum^{\infty}_{n=1}x_n$.
     \item[b)] an $\mathcal{S}_{*}$-absolute decomposition for $E$ if is a decomposition for $E$ that admits a fundamental system  of semi-norms $\{p_\lambda: \, \lambda \in I \}$ such that for any sequence of scalars $(\alpha_n)$ with $\lim_{n\rightarrow \infty}\sup{\modulo{\alpha_n}^{\frac{1}{n}}}<\infty$, the semi-norm $q_\lambda:E\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ defined by $q_\lambda\left( \sum^{\infty}_{n=1}x_n\right)=\sum^{\infty}_{n=1}\modulo{\alpha_n}p_\lambda (x_n)$ is continuous, for every $\lambda \in I$.  
 \end{itemize}
 \end{definition} 

The concept of $\mathcal{S}_{*}$-absolute decomposition coincides with the global Schauder decomposition for a locally convex space. This notion is studied in \cite{V, GMR} and represents a variation of the $\mathcal{S}$-absolute decomposition introduced in \cite{D2}.

In \cite{V}, Venkova proved that the space of holomorphic functions defined on a locally convex space $E$, endowed with the natural topologies, admits an $\mathcal{S}_*$-absolute decomposition.

\begin{examples}\cite[Proposition 4.1, Proposition 4.2]{V}
    Let $E$ be a locally convex space. Then
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[a)] $\{(\mathcal{P}(^nE), \tau_0)\}^{\infty}_{n=1}$ is an $\mathcal{S}_{*}$-absolute decomposition for $(\mathcal{H}(E), \tau_0)$.
        \item[b)] $\{(\mathcal{P}(^nE), \tau_\omega)\}^{\infty}_{n=1}$ is an $\mathcal{S}_{*}$-absolute decomposition for $(\mathcal{H}(E), \tau_\omega)$. 
        \item[c)] $\{(\mathcal{P}(^nE), \tau_b)\}^{\infty}_{n=1}$ is an $\mathcal{S}_{*}$-absolute decomposition for $(\mathcal{H}_b(E), \tau_b)$.   
    \end{itemize}
\end{examples} 

It is important to observe the following property of a locally convex space $E$ that admits an $\mathcal{S}_{*}$-absolute decomposition.

\begin{remark}\cite[\S 3.3]{D2}
      If $E$ has an $\mathcal{S}_{*}$-absolute decomposition $\left\{E_n \right\}$, then it admits a fundamental system of semi-norms $\{p_\lambda: \, \lambda \in I \}$ such that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq4}
   p_\lambda \left(\sum^{\infty}_{n=1}x_n \right)=\sum^{\infty}_{n=1}p_\lambda (x_n), 
\end{equation}
 for every $\lambda \in I$, $\sum^{\infty}_{n=1}x_n\in E$ with $x_n\in E_n$ for all $n\in \mathbb{N}$. 
 \end{remark}





The sequence $\{E_n\}^{\infty}_{n=1}$ is called a finite-dimensional decomposition if each $E_n$ is a finite-dimensional space for all $n\in \mathbb{N}$. An example, if $X$ is a Banach space with a Schauder basis then for each $n\in \mathbb{N}$, the sequence $\{\mathcal{P}_k(^nX)\}^{\infty}_{k=1}$ forms a finite-dimensional decomposition of $\mathcal{P}(^nX)$ (see \cite[Section 4.1]{D2}).



When $(E, \norma{.})$ is a normed linear space, the decomposition constant is defined as the infimum of $C>0$ such that $\norma{\sum^{s}_{j=1}x_j}\leq C\norma{\sum^{t}_{j=1}x_j}$, for all $s,t\in \mathbb{N}$ with $s<t$ and all $x_j\in E_j$.

If each $E_n$ has a Schauder basis $(e_{n,m})_m$ for every $n\in \mathbb{N}$ then, an ordering of the family $(e_{n,m})_{n,m}$ into a sequence is determined by a bijective mapping $\varphi: \mathbb{N}\times \mathbb{N}\rightarrow \mathbb{N}$. The ordering is said to be compatible if $\varphi(n,m_1)<\varphi(n,m_2)$ whenever $m_1<m_2$, for all $n\in \mathbb{N}$. In other words, for each $n\in \mathbb{N}$, the map $\varphi$ induces the original order on $E_n$.

In 2012, S. Dineen and J. Mujica \cite{DM} provided conditions under which a locally convex space admits a Schauder basis. Before state this result, we recall that a sequence $(x_n)$ in a locally convex space $(E, \tau)$ is called a Schauder basis for $(E, \tau)$ if, for each $x\in E$, there exists a unique sequence of scalars $(\alpha_n)$ such that $x=\sum^{\infty}_{n=1}\alpha_n x_n$, and if each of the maps $f_n:E\rightarrow \mathbb{C}$, defined by $f_n(x)=\alpha_n$, is continuous.


\begin{proposition}\cite[Theorem 1]{DM}
    Let $E$ be a locally convex space that admits an $\mathcal{S}_{*}$-absolute decomposition $(E_n)$, with a fundamental system $\mathcal{N}$ of semi-norms $p$ satifying (\ref{eq4}) and that each $E_n$ has a Schauder basis $(e_{n,m})_{m}$. Then $(e_{n,m})_{n,m}$, with any compatible ordering, is a basis for $E$ if the basis constant $c_{p,n}$ of $(E_n, p)$ for $(e_{n,m})_{m}$ have at most exponential growth, i.e., $\displaystyle \limsup_{n\rightarrow \infty}c^{\frac{1}{n}}_{p,n}<\infty$.
\end{proposition}

The authors applied this result to prove that, under a compatible ordering, the monomials form Schauder bases for the spaces$(\mathcal{H}(c_0), \tau_0)$, $(\mathcal{H}(c_0), \tau_\omega)$ and $(\mathcal{H}_b(c_0), \tau_b)$.



 The square ordering is introduced here in the same way as in \cite{D2}. Let $X$ be a Banach space with Schauder basis. To obtain a monomial basis for $\mathcal{P}(^nX)$ is necessary to define an order on the monomials. Let $m, \overline{m}\in \mathbb{N}^{(\mathbb{N})}$. We say that $m<\overline{m}$ if $l(m)<l(\overline{m})$. Afterwards, we order the monomial basis in each $\mathcal{P}_k(^nX)$, by induction, in such a way that the required convergence properties are easily verified. For $n=1$ we have the natural order inherited from the basis in $X$. If $z^m\in \mathcal{P}_k(^{n+1}X)$ then there exist a unique $m'\in \mathbb{N}^{(\mathbb{N})}$ such that $z^m=z^{m'}.z_k$ and $z^{m'}\in \mathcal{P}_{i}(^{n}X)$ for some $i\leq k$. Suppose we have ordered the monomial basis in  $\mathcal{P}_k(^{n}X)$ for all $k\in \mathbb{N}$. If $z^m$, $z^{\overline{m}}\in \mathcal{P}_k(^{n+1}X)$, $z^m=z^{m'}.z_k$ and $z^{\overline{m}}=z^{\overline{m}'}.z_k$ let $m\leq \overline{m}$ if $m'\leq \overline{m}'$. Thus the ordered monomial basis for for $\mathcal{P}_k(^{n+1}X)$ is obtained from $\bigoplus_{i\leq k}\mathcal{P}_i(^{n}X)$ by multiplying each monomial by $z_k$ and keeping the inherited order. This is the square ordering on the monomials. Now we present a formal definition of the square ordering. If $m=(m_i)$ and $\overline{m}=(\overline{m}_i)$ then $m<\overline{m}$ if (i) $l(m)<l(\overline{m})$ or (ii) $l(m)=l(\overline{m})$ and for some $i\leq l(m)$, $m_i<\overline{m}_i$ and $m_j=\overline{m}_j$ for $j>i$.

Before stating the next example, we recall that a Banach space $X$ has the Dunford-Pettis property if every continuous weakly compact operator $T:X\rightarrow Y$ from $X$ into another Banach space $Y$ transforms weakly compact sets in $X$ into norm-compact sets in $Y$.

\begin{examples}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[a)]\cite[Corollary 5.1]{R2} Let $X$ be a Banach space with a shrinking basis, and suppose that $X$ has the Dunford-Pettis property. Then for every $n$ the monomials of degree $n$, with the square order, form a Schauder basis for $(\mathcal{P}(^nX), \tau_b)$.
        \item[b)] \cite[Proposition 5.2]{R2} Let $X$ be a Banach space with a Schauder basis. Then for every $n$, the monomials of degree $n$ with the square order, form a Schauder basis for $(\mathcal{P}(^nX), \tau_0)$.
        \item[c)] \cite[Proposition 10]{DD} If the Banach space $X$ has a shrinking basis then the monomials of degree $n$ with the square order form a Schauder basis for $(\mathcal{P}_\omega(^nX), \norma{.})$.
    \end{itemize}
\end{examples}









\section{Compact sets and semi-norms}


In this section, we provide a fundamental system for the compact subsets of the Banach spaces $c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right)$, where $p\in [1,\infty)$, and $d_*(w,1)$. This characterization enables us to define families of seminorms that generate the topology $\tau_0$ on the spaces $\mathcal{H}\left(c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right) \right)$, for $p\in [1,\infty)$, and $\mathcal{H}\left(d_*(w,1) \right)$.

A family $\left\{A_\lambda: \, \lambda \in I \right\}$ is called a fundamental system for the compact subsets of a Banach space $X$ if, for every compact subset $K\subset X$, there exists $\lambda \in I$ such that $K\subset A_\lambda$. Furthermore, a subset $A\subset X$ is said to be totally bounded if, for every $\varepsilon>0$, there exist points $x_1, \ldots, x_n\in X$ such that $A\subset \bigcup^{n}_{i=1}B(x_i, \varepsilon)$. In this context, a subset $K\subset X$ is compact if and only if it is complete and totally bounded.


\begin{proposition}\label{p1}
    Let $\lambda\in c^{+}_{0}$, let $Z=c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right)$ for some $p\in[1,\infty)$ and define 
\begin{equation*}
A_\lambda=\left\{(z_i)\in Z:\,  \left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq \lambda_m,  \,  m\in\mathbb{N}\right\}.
\end{equation*}
 Then 
 $\left\{A_\lambda:\, \lambda\in c^{+}_{0} \right\}$ is a fundamental system of compact sets of  $Z$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}



   \,  Let  $p\in[1,\infty)$, $\lambda\in c^{+}_{0}$ and $\varepsilon>0$.  We claim that 
 $A_\lambda$ is a closed set in $Z$. 
If $z\in \hbox{cl}(A_\lambda)$ then there is a sequence $(z^j)\subset A_\lambda$ such that $z^j\rightarrow y$ whenever $j\rightarrow \infty$. Thus, there exists $j_0\in \mathbb{N}$ such that  $\|z^j-z\|\leq \epsilon$, for all $j\geq j_0$. Thus, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}-\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z^j_i|^p\right)^{1/p}&\leq&  \sup\left\{\left( \sum_{i\in I(m)}\modulo{z^j_i-z_i}^p\right)^{1/p}: \, m\in \mathbb{N} \right\}\\
    &=&\norma{z^j-z}\leq \varepsilon,
\end{eqnarray*}
for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$ and $j\geq \j_0$. Hence, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}&\leq&  \left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z^j_i|^p\right)^{1/p}+ \varepsilon\leq \lambda_m+\varepsilon,
\end{eqnarray*}
for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$. When $\varepsilon \rightarrow 0$, we conclude that 
$\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq  \lambda_m, \quad \text{for all} \quad m\in \mathbb{N}$. That is, $A_\lambda$ is a closed set in $Z$.


To show that $A_\lambda$ is a compact set, it is sufficient to prove that  $A_\lambda$ is a totally bounded set in $Z$. As $\lambda \in c^+_0$, there is  $m_0\in \mathbb{N}$ such that  
 $\lambda_m\leq \frac{\epsilon}{2}$ for all $m\geq m_0$.
We define $A^{m_0}_{\lambda}=\{(z_i)\in A_\lambda: z_i=0 \quad \text{for all}\quad i\in I(m) \quad  \text{and}\quad m> m_0 \}$. Let $n=\frac{m_0(m_0+1)}{2}$ and $T:\mathbb{C}^n\longrightarrow Z$ be the continuous mapping defined by 
\begin{equation*}
T(z_1, ..., z_n)= \left(\begin{array}{ccccccc}z_1 & z_2 & z_4 & \ldots & z_k & 0 & \ldots \\ & z_3 & z_5 &  &  & 0 & \\ &  & z_6 &  &  & 0 & \\ &  &  & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots &\\ &  &  &  & z_n & 0 & \\ &  & &  &  & 0 &\end{array}\right),
\end{equation*} 
and $F=\left\{z\in \mathbb{C}^n: \left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p} \leq \lambda_m,  \quad 1\leq m\leq m_0 \right\}$ a compact set of  $\mathbb{C}^n$. Thus, $A_\lambda^{m_0}=T(F)$ is compact.  Therefore, there are $z^1, ..., z^l\in A^{m_0}_{\lambda}$, such that $A^{m_0}_{\lambda}\subset \bigcup^{l}_{j=1}B(z^j, \frac{\varepsilon}{2})$. We claim that $A_\lambda\subset \bigcup^{l}_{j=1}B(z^j, \varepsilon)$. Let $z \in A_\lambda$ and decompose  $z=u+v$, where 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u=\left(\begin{array}{ccccccc}z_1 & z_2 & z_4 & \ldots & z_k & 0 & \ldots \\ & z_3 & z_5 &  &  & 0 & \\ &  & z_6 &  &  & 0 & \\ &  &  & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots &\\ &  &  &  & z_m & 0 & \\ &  & &  &  & 0 &\end{array}\right), \,  v=\left(\begin{array}{ccccccc}0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots & 0 & z_{m+1} & \ldots \\ & 0 & 0 &  &  & z_{m+2} & \\ &  & 0 &  &  & z_{m+3} & \\ &  &  & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots &\\ &  &  &  & 0 &  & \\ &  & &  &  &  &\end{array}\right).
\end{eqnarray*}
Since $u\in A^{m_0}_{\lambda}$ there is  $1\leq j_0\leq l$ such that $u\in B(z^{j_0}, \frac{\varepsilon}{2})$. This means that $\norma{u-z^{j_0}}< \frac{\varepsilon}{2}$. Notice that $v\in A_\lambda$, since $z\in A_\lambda$. Therefore,
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \norma{w}&=&\sup\left\{\left( \sum_{i\in I(m)}\modulo{v_i}^p\right)^{1/p}: \, m\in \mathbb{N} \right\}\\
    &=& \sup\left\{\left( \sum_{i\in I(m)}\modulo{v_i}^p\right)^{1/p}: \, m>m_0\right\}\\
    &=& \sup\left\{\left( \sum_{i\in I(m)}\modulo{z_i}^p\right)^{1/p}: \, m>m_0\right\}\\
    &\leq&  \sup\left\{\lambda_m: \, m>m_0\right\}\leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2}.
\end{eqnarray*}
Thus, $\norma{z-z^{j_0}}=\norma{u+v-z^{j_0}}\leq \norma{u-z^{j_0}}+\norma{v}<\frac{\varepsilon}{2}+\frac{\varepsilon}{2}=\varepsilon$. Then $z\in B(z^{j_0}, \varepsilon)$, and we conclude that $A_\lambda$
is a totally bounded set in $Z$. Therefore, $A_\lambda$ is a compact set of $Z$.


Finally, we prove that $\{A_\lambda: \, \lambda\in c_0^+\}$ is a fundamental system  of compact sets of  $Z$.
Let $K\subset Z$ a compact subset. We define $\lambda=(\lambda_m)$ by
$\lambda_m= \sup \left\{\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}: \, z\in K\right\}$, for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$. We claim that 
 $\lambda\in c^{+}_0$.  Indeed, there are $z^1, ..., z^l\in K$ such that 
$K\subset \bigcup^{l}_{j=1}B(z^j,\frac{\varepsilon}{2}).$ 
 As $z^j\in c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right)$, then  for each $j=1,...,l$, there is $m_j\in \mathbb{N}$ such that
$\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z^j_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2}, \quad \text{for all}\quad m\geq m_j.$ If 
  $N=\displaystyle \max \{ m_j: 1\leq j\leq l\}$, then
$\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z^j_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2}, \quad \text{for all}\quad m\geq N$. Then, if $z\in K$, there is $1\leq j_0\leq l$, such that $ \lVert z-z^{j_0}\rVert\leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2}.$
 That means,
$$\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}-\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z^{j_0}_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i-z^{j_0}_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2},
$$
for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$. Then, 
$\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}
\leq\varepsilon \quad \text{for all}\quad m\geq N.$
This implies that,
\begin{equation*}
    \sup_{z\in K}\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq\varepsilon, \, \, \text{for all} \, \,  m\geq N. 
\end{equation*}
As a consequence,
$|\lambda_m|= \sup_{z\in K}\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq \varepsilon$, for all $m\geq N,$ and 
 $\lambda\in c^{+}_0$. Now, if $u\in K$,  we have 
$\left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|u_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq \sup_{z\in K} \left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}=\lambda_m$, for every $m\in \mathbb{N}$.  Then,
  $u\in A_\lambda$ and $\{A_\lambda: \, \lambda\in c^{+}_0\}$ is a fundamental system  of compact sets of  $Z$.
\end{proof}

In the next proposition, we give a fundamental system for compact sets of $d_*(w,1)$.


\begin{proposition}\label{p2} Let  $w\in c_0\setminus \ell_1$  be a decreasing  sequence of positive real numbers such that $w_1=1$, let $\lambda\in c^+_0$, let $Z=d_{*}(w,1)$ and define
\begin{equation*}
A_\lambda=\left\{z\in  Z : \, \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\leq \lambda_m,\, \,   m\in\mathbb{N}\right\}.
\end{equation*}
  Then $\left\{A_\lambda:\, \lambda\in c^+_0 \right\}$ is a fundamental system of compact sets of   $Z$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} \, Let  $\lambda\in c^+_0$, $\varepsilon>0$ and $w\in c_0\setminus \ell_1$ a decresing sequence of positive real numbers such that  $w_1=1$.
We claim that  $A_\lambda$ is a closed set in $Z$. If $z\in \hbox{cl}(A_\lambda)$, then there is a sequence $(z^j)\subset A_\lambda$ such that $z^j\rightarrow z$ when $j\rightarrow \infty$. Thus, there exists $j_0\in \mathbb{N}$ such that  $\|z^j-z\|_{*}\leq \varepsilon$, for all $j\geq j_0$. This implies that 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}-\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z^j]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}&\leq& \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z^j-z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\\
&\leq& \sup\left\{\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z^j-z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}:\, m\in \mathbb{N} \right\}\\
&=&\norma{z^j-z}_*\leq \varepsilon, 
\end{eqnarray*}
for all $j\geq j_0$ and $m\in \mathbb{N}$. This means that
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}&\leq&  \varepsilon+\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z^j]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\leq \varepsilon+\lambda_m, 
\end{eqnarray*}
for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$. When $\varepsilon \rightarrow 0$, we get that $\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\leq \lambda_m$, for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$. Therefore, $z\in A_\lambda$ and $A_\lambda$ is a closed set in $Z$.

To show that $A_\lambda$ is a compact set, it is sufficient to prove that  $A_\lambda$ is a totally bounded set in $Z$. As 
 $\lambda\in c^+_0$, there exists $m_0\in \mathbb{N}$ such that $\lambda_m\leq \frac{\epsilon}{2}$, for all $m\geq m_0$. Consider 
$A^{m_0}_{\lambda}=\left\{z\in A_\lambda: z_m=0,  \quad m>m_0\right\}$ and 
let $T:\mathbb{C}^{m_0}\longrightarrow Z$ be the continuous linear mapping defined by  $T(z_1, ..., z_{m_0})=(z_1, ..., z_{m_0}, 0, 0, ...)$.
The set  $F=\{z\in \mathbb{C}^{m_0}:\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\leq \lambda_m,\quad  1\leq m\leq m_0 \} $ is compact in $\mathbb{C}^{m_0}$, then $T(F)=A^{m_0}_{\lambda}$ is compact. That is, there are  $z^1, ..., z^l\in A^{m_0}_{\lambda}$ such that  $A^{m_0}_{\lambda}\subset \bigcup^{l}_{j=1}B(z^j, \frac{\varepsilon}{2})$. We claim that $A_\lambda \subset \bigcup^{l}_{j=1}B(z^j, \varepsilon)$. Let $z \in A_\lambda$ and decompose  $z=u+v$, where $u=(z_1, \ldots, z_{m_0}, 0, 0, \ldots)$ and $v=(0, \ldots, 0, z_{m_0+1},z_{m_0+2}, \ldots )$. Since $u\in A^{m_0}_\lambda$ there is $1\leq j_0\leq l$ such that $u\in B(z^{j_0}, \varepsilon)$. This means that $\norma{u-z^{j_0}}_*<\frac{\varepsilon}{2}$. As $y\in A_\lambda$, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \norma{v}_*&=&\sup \left\{ \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[v]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}: \, m\in \mathbb{N}\right\}\\
    &=& \sup \left\{ \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}: \, m>m_0\right\}\\
    &\leq& \sup \left\{ \lambda_m: \, m>m_0\right\}\leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2}.    
\end{eqnarray*}
Then, $\norma{z-z^{j_0}}_*=\norma{u+v-z^{j_0}}_*\leq \norma{u-z^{j_0}}_*+\norma{v}_*< \frac{\varepsilon}{2}+\frac{\varepsilon}{2}=\varepsilon$. Then $z\in B(z^{j_0}, \varepsilon)$ and $A_\lambda$
is a totally bounded set in $Z$. Therefore, $A_\lambda$ is a compact set of $Z$.

Finally, we prove that $\left\{A_\lambda:\, \lambda\in c^+_0 \right\}$ is a fundamental system of compact sets of  $Z$. Let  $K$ a compact subset of $Z$. We define
 $\lambda_m=\sup\left\{ \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}: \, z\in K\right\}$, for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$. We claim that $\lambda=(\lambda_m)\in c^+_0$. Let $\{B(z,\frac{\varepsilon}{2}): \, z\in K \}$ an open cover of $K$. Thus, there are $z^1, \ldots, z^l\in K$ such that $K\subset \bigcup^{l}_{j=1}B(z^j,\frac{\varepsilon}{2})$. As $z^j\in Z$ for all $j=1, \ldots, l$, there is $m_j\in \mathbb{N}$ such that $\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z^j]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2}$, for all $m\geq m_j$ and $j=1\ldots, l$. If $N=\max \{m_j: \, j=1, \ldots, l \}$ then $\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z^j]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2}$, for all $m\geq N$. Let $z\in K$, there is $j_0\in \{1, \ldots, l \}$ such that $z\in B(z^{j_0}, \frac{\varepsilon}{2})$. This implies that
 \begin{eqnarray*}
     \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}-\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z^{j_0}]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\leq \sup \left\{ \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z-z^{j_0}]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}: \, z\in K \right\}\leq \norma{z-z^{j_0}}_*<\frac{\varepsilon}{2},
 \end{eqnarray*}
for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$. Considering $m\geq N$, we see that
\begin{eqnarray*}
     \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}< \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z^{j_0}]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}+\frac{\varepsilon}{2}\leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2}+\frac{\varepsilon}{2}=\varepsilon.
 \end{eqnarray*}
 Then, $\lambda_m=\sup\left\{ \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}: \, z\in K\right\}\leq \varepsilon$, for all $m\geq N$. Therefore, $\lambda\in c^+_0$.Thus, if $z\in K$ and $\lambda=(\lambda_m) \in c^+_0$ is an element defined below, then
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\leq \sup \left\{ \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}: \, z\in K \right\}=\lambda_m,
\end{equation*}
for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$. That is, $K\subset A_\lambda$. This shows that $\{A_\lambda: \, \lambda\in c^{+}_0\}$ is a fundamental system  of compact sets of $Z$.
\end{proof}



\begin{remark}
    Let $Z$ be the complex Banach space $c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right)$ with  $p\in [1,\infty)$ (resp., $d_*(w,1)$), $A\subset Z$ be a bounded polydisc and $Q_k\in \mathcal{P}_k(^nZ)$. Then, for all positive integers $s,t$ with $s<t$, 
\begin{equation}\label{i6}
    \norma{\sum^{s}_{k=1}Q_k}_A\leq \norma{\sum^{t}_{k=1}Q_k}_A.
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark} 

a) Let $Z=c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right)$ with  $p\in [1,\infty)$.  By \cite[Proposition 3.18]{DM} and Proposition \ref{p1}, the family of seminorms $\left\{p_\lambda: \, \lambda \in c^{+}_{0} \right\}$ defined by
\begin{equation*}
    \displaystyle p_\lambda \left(f \right)= \sum^{\infty}_{n=1}\sup \left\{\modulo{P_n(z)}:\,  z=(z_i)\in Z:\,  \left(\sum_{i\in I(m)}|z_i|^p\right)^{1/p}\leq \lambda_m,  \,  m\in\mathbb{N} \right\},
\end{equation*}
where $\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}P_n$ is the Taylor series of $f$ at the origin and $\lambda=(\lambda_m)$ ranges over $c^{+}_{0}$, generates $(\mathcal{H}(Z), \tau_0)$.


b) Let $Z=d_{*}(w,1)$. By \cite[Proposition 3.18]{DM} and Proposition \ref{p2}, the family of seminorms $\left\{p_\lambda: \, \lambda \in c^{+}_{0} \right\}$ defined by
\begin{equation*}
    \displaystyle p_\lambda \left(f \right)= \sum^{\infty}_{n=1}\sup \left\{\modulo{P_n(z)}:\, z=(z_i)\in  Z : \, \frac{\sum^{m}_{i=1}[z]_i}{\sum^{m}_{i=1}w_i}\leq \lambda_m,\, \,   m\in\mathbb{N} \right\},
\end{equation*}
where $\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}P_n$ is the Taylor series of $f$ at the origin and $\lambda=(\lambda_m)$ ranges over $c^{+}_{0}$, generates $(\mathcal{H}(Z), \tau_0)$.

\end{remark}

We notice that the family of seminorms defined above satisfies (\ref{eq4}).





\section{Main result}
Now, we will show the main result.  Let $Z$ be the complex Banach space $c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right)$ with  $p\in [1,\infty)$ (resp., $d_*(w,1)$). To simplify the notation, the sequence $(P_{n,m})_m$ will denote the monomials on $\mathcal{P}(^nZ)$ with the square order. Also, we suppose that on the set of all monomials a compatible ordering is defined.
\begin{theorem}
    Let $Z$ be the complex Banach space $c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right)$, $p\in [1,\infty)$ or $d_{*}(w,1)$.  The monomials $(P_{n,m})^{\infty}_{n,m=1}$ with a compatible order form a Schauder basis for $(\mathcal{H}(Z), \tau_0)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}

\, We consider the Banach space $Z=c_0\left(\bigoplus^{\infty}_{i=1}\ell^{i}_{p} \right)$ for some $p\in [1,\infty)$, and let $A\subset Z$ be a bounded polydisc . Our goal is to show that the sequence $(c_n)$, where $c_n$ denotes the basis constant of the monomial basis $(P_{n,m})^{\infty}_{m=1}$ in the space $\mathcal{P}(^nZ)$ endowed with the supremum norm $\norma{.}_A$, satisfies $\displaystyle \limsup_{n\rightarrow \infty}c^{\frac{1}{n}}_{n}<\infty$. The conclusion will follow from \cite[Theorem 1]{DM}.


Let $s,t$ positive integers with $s<t$ and consider $(P_{n+1,m})^{\infty}_{m=1}$ the monomial basis for the space $(\mathcal{P}(^{n+1}Z), \norma{.}_A)$. We use the following notation: the linear combination $\sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}$ will be expressed as a sum of $n+1$-homogeneous polynomials of the same lengh. That is, 
\begin{equation*}
    \displaystyle \sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}=\sum^{k+1}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}\, \,  \text{with}\, \,  Q_{n+1,u}=\displaystyle \sum^{s}_{\substack{m=1\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=u}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}\in \mathcal{P}_u(^{n+1}Z), 
\end{equation*}
for all  $1\leq u\leq k+1$. 


By applying the triangle inequality, we obtain the estimate
\begin{eqnarray}\label{i5}
\norma{\sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A&=& \norma{\sum^{k+1}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}}_A\nonumber\\
&\leq& \norma{\sum^{k}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}}_A+\norma{Q_{n+1,k+1}}_A.
\end{eqnarray}

In a similar way, we consider the linear combination
$\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}$ and decompose it as a sum of $n+1$-homogeneous polynomials of the same length. Before proceeding with this decomposition, observe that, since $P_{n+1, m}$ is a monomial of degree $n+1$ and if $m_1<m_2$ then $l(P_{n+1, m_1})\leq l(P_{n+1, m_2})$. Thus, 




\begin{align}\label{eq1}
    \sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}&=\sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}+\sum^{t}_{m=s+1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}\nonumber \\
    &\hspace{-1.6cm}=\sum^{k+1}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}+\sum^{k_1}_{u=k+1}R_{n+1,u}\nonumber\\
    &\hspace{-1.6cm}= \sum^{k}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}+(Q_{n+1,k+1}+R_{n+1,k+1})+\sum^{k_1}_{u>k+1}R_{n+1,u},
\end{align}
with  $R_{n+1,u}=\displaystyle \sum^{t}_{\substack{m>s\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=u}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}\in \mathcal{P}_u(^{n+1}Z)$ for all $k< u\leq k_1$, for some positive integer $k_1$. 

Let $m_0=\inf \left\{j:\, P_{n+1,j}\in \mathcal{P}_{k+1}(^{n+1}Z) \right\}$. If $l(P_{n+1, m})\leq k$ for $m<m_0$ and $l(P_{n+1, m})=k+1$ then
\begin{align}\label{e1}
    Q_{n+1, k+1}&=\sum^{s}_{\substack{m=1\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}\nonumber \\
    &\hspace{-1.6cm}=\sum^{m_0-1}_{\substack{m=1\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m} +\sum^{s}_{\substack{m=m_0\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}\nonumber \\
    &\hspace{-1.6cm}= \sum^{s}_{\substack{m=m_0\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}= e^{*}_{k+1}.\sum^{s}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1},
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}\label{e2}
    Q_{n+1, k+1}+R_{n+1, k+1}&=\sum^{s}_{\substack{m=1\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}+\sum^{t}_{\substack{m>s\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}\nonumber \\
    &\hspace{-3.3cm}=\sum^{m_0-1}_{\substack{m=1\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m} +\sum^{s}_{\substack{m=m_0\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}+\sum^{t}_{\substack{m>s\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}\nonumber \\
    &\hspace{-1.6cm}=\sum^{s_1}_{\substack{m=m_0\\ l(P_{n+1,m})=k+1}}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}=e^{*}_{k+1}.\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1},
\end{align}
 with $s_1=\max\left\{j:\, P_{n+1,j}\in \mathcal{P}_{k+1}(^{n+1}Z) \right\}$.

 We claim that there exists a positive integer $p_0\in \mathbb{N}$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{i4}
    \norma{e^{*}_{k+1}}_A.\norma{\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}}_A\leq p_0 \norma{e^{*}_{k+1}.\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}}_A.
\end{equation}
In fact, as $\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}\in \mathcal{H}(Z)$, then for at least one $z\in \partial(A)$ we have 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq2}
\modulo{\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}(z)}&=&\norma{\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}}_A\\ \nonumber &=&\norma{\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}}_{\partial(A)}.
\end{eqnarray}
Morover, there exists $\Tilde{z}\in \partial(A)$ such that
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq3}
    \modulo{e^{*}_{k+1}(\Tilde{z})}=\norma{e^{*}_{k+1}}_A=\norma{e^{*}_{k+1}}_{\partial(A)}.
\end{eqnarray}
It follows from $(\ref{eq2})$, $(\ref{eq3})$ and the Archimedean property of the real numbers that
\begin{eqnarray}
     \norma{e^{*}_{k+1}}_A.\norma{\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}}_A&=&\norma{e^{*}_{k+1}}_{\partial(A)}\norma{\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}}_{\partial(A)}\nonumber\\
     &=&\modulo{e^{*}_{k+1}(\Tilde{z})}\modulo{\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}(z)}\nonumber\\
     &\leq&\modulo{e^{*}_{k+1}(\Tilde{z})}p_0\modulo{\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}(\Tilde{z})}\nonumber\\
      &=&p_0\modulo{e^{*}_{k+1}(\Tilde{z}).\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}(\Tilde{z})}\nonumber\\
      &\leq&p_0\norma{e^{*}_{k+1}.\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n+1, m}}_A,
\end{eqnarray}
for some $p_0\in \mathbb{N}$. Thus, the claim follows.

We define $a:=1+2p_0$ and proceed to prove by induction that $c_n\leq a^n$, for all $n\in \mathbb{N}$. For the base case $n=1$, the result follows directly from the natural order inherited by the standard basis $(e_j)$ in $Z$. That is,
\begin{eqnarray*}
        \norma{\sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{1,m}}_A&=&\sup \left\{ \modulo{\sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{1,m}\left(\sum^{\infty}_{j=1}z_je_j \right)}: \, \sum^{\infty}_{j=1}z_je_j\in A\right\}\\
        &=& \sup \left\{ \modulo{\sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{1,m}\left(\sum^{s}_{j=1}z_je_j \right)}: \, \sum^{s}_{j=1}z_je_j\in A\right\}\nonumber \\
    &=& \sup \left\{ \modulo{\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{1,m}\left(\sum^{s}_{j=1}z_je_j \right)}: \, \sum^{s}_{j=1}z_je_j\in A\right\}\\
    &=&\norma{\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{1,m}}_A,
\end{eqnarray*}
where, in the third equality, we use the property  $\sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{1,m}\left(\sum^{s}_{j=1}z_je_j \right)=\\ \sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{1,m}\left(\sum^{s}_{j=1}z_je_j \right)+\sum^{t}_{m=s+1}\alpha_mP_{1,m}\left(\sum^{s}_{j=1}z_je_j \right)$. Then we conclude that $c_1=1\leq 1+2p_0=a$.

Now, suppose that $c_n\leq a^n$ for some positive integer $n$. We aim to prove that $c_{n+1}\leq a^{n+1}$. 

Furthermore,
\begin{eqnarray}\label{i7}
    \norma{\sum^{k}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}}_A&=&\sup \left\{ \modulo{\sum^{k}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}\left(\sum^{\infty}_{i=1}z_ie_i \right)}:\, \sum^{\infty}_{i=1}z_ie_i\in A\right\}\nonumber \\
    &=&\sup \left\{ \modulo{\sum^{k}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}\left(\sum^{k}_{i=1}z_ie_i \right)}:\, \sum^{k}_{i=1}z_ie_i\in A\right\}\nonumber \\
    &=&\sup \left\{ \modulo{\left(\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}\right)\left(\sum^{k}_{i=1}z_ie_i \right)}:\, \sum^{k}_{i=1}z_ie_i\in A\right\}\nonumber \\
    &\leq&\sup \left\{ \modulo{\left(\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}\right)\left(\sum^{\infty}_{i=1}z_ie_i \right)}:\, \sum^{\infty}_{i=1}z_ie_i\in A\right\}\nonumber \\
    &=&\norma{\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A,
\end{eqnarray}
where, in the third equality, we use (\ref{eq1}). By (\ref{e1}), induction, (\ref{i4}), (\ref{e2}), (\ref{i6}), (\ref{i7}) and (\ref{eq1}) we see that
\begin{eqnarray}
    \norma{Q_{n+1,k+1}}_A&=&\norma{e^{*}_{k+1}.\sum^{s}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}}_A\nonumber\\
    &=&\norma{e^{*}_{k+1}}_A \norma{\sum^{s}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}}_A\nonumber \\
     &\leq&\norma{e^{*}_{k+1}}_A a^n\norma{\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n, m-m_0+1}}_A\nonumber \\
     &\leq& a^n p_0\norma{e^{*}_{k+1}.\sum^{s_1}_{m=m_0}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A\nonumber \\
     &=&a^n p_0\norma{Q_{n+1,k+1}+R_{n+1,k+1}}_A\nonumber \\
      &\leq&a^n p_0\norma{Q_{n+1,k+1}+R_{n+1,k+1}+\sum^{k_1}_{u>k+1}R_{n+1, u}}_A\nonumber \\
      &\leq&a^n p_0\left( \norma{\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A+\norma{\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A\right)\nonumber \\
       &=&2a^n p_0\norma{\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A.
\end{eqnarray}
This implies that,
\begin{eqnarray}
    \norma{\sum^{s}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A&=&\norma{\sum^{k+1}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}}_A\nonumber\\
    &=&\norma{\sum^{k}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}+Q_{n+1,k+1}}_A\nonumber\\
    &\leq&\norma{\sum^{k}_{u=1}Q_{n+1,u}}_A+\norma{Q_{n+1,k+1}}_A\nonumber\\
    &\leq&\norma{\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A+2a^n p_0\norma{\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A\nonumber\\
    &=&\left(1+ 2a^n p_0 \right)\norma{\sum^{t}_{m=1}\alpha_mP_{n+1,m}}_A.
\end{eqnarray}

Therefore, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
    c_{n+1}&\leq& 1+ 2a^n p_0\nonumber \\
    &\leq& a^n + 2a^n p_0\nonumber \\
    &=&a^n(1+2p_0)\nonumber \\
    &=&a^n a=a^{n+1}.
\end{eqnarray*}

Finally, 
\begin{equation*}
\limsup_{n\rightarrow \infty}c^{\frac{1}{n+1}}_{n+1}\leq \limsup_{n\rightarrow \infty}(a^{n+1})^{\frac{1}{n+1}}=a=1+2p_0<\infty,
\end{equation*}
and our proof is complete. 

The proof in the case $Z=d_*(w,1)$ follows by similar arguments.

\end{proof}





