In previous studies, star topology has been shown to be disadvantageous to the emergence of cooperation. Accordingly, the critical threshold for the fixation of cooperation is $\infty$ if we do not consider self-loop~\cite{allen2017evolutionary}. This is because of the equivalence between one-step and three-step expected coalescence time. Our proposed self-interaction landscape can buffer the random walk, as well as the replacement of strategy. If three-step expected coalescence time is greater than one-step, i.e., $(N-1)\pi_L\eta_L(p_{LL}+p_{LL}^{(2)})+\pi_H\eta_H(p_{HH}+p_{HH}^{(2)})-2>0$, the condition for cooperation is positive. Thm.~\ref{Theorem: 2} highlights that a proper self-interaction landscape can enhance the fixation of cooperation in stars. But the self-interaction of the hub agent is very harmful because the strategy replacement of the leader itself is likely to induce the defection of the entire system.  However, the self-interaction of the leaves is crucial and helpful for the system to overcome the defection. 

Additionally, researchers often consider the graph surgery to promote cooperation in stars. Here, we study the effect of self-interaction in two modified stars. The first one is a graph containing two stars joined by their hubs. The second one is a ceiling fan where each leaf has one edge to another leaf. In the following, we consider a joined star topology composed of two connected star graphs via the hub vertices. 
