\section{How to handle other shapes rather than 0-centered disk?}

\section{One variable independence -- Old part}

Let $Z(G;\lambda)$ be the independence polynomial of a graph $G$. In the heart of the interpolation method we have that the $m$th Taylor polynomial of $\log(Z(G;\lambda)))$ at $\lambda_0$ is a good multiplicative approximation of $Z(G,\lambda_0)$. To be precise,
\[
    \left|\log(Z(G;\lambda_0)-T_m(\lambda_0)\right|\le \frac{n\delta^m}{1-\delta},
\]
where $\delta=\frac{\lambda_0}{\lambda_c}$, where $\lambda_c=\frac{(\Delta-1)^{\Delta-1}}{\Delta^\Delta}$. Thus to have $\varepsilon$ multiplicative approximation of $Z(G,\lambda_0)$ it is sufficient to take  $m=C\log(\varepsilon/n)$. 

Next we will argue how to obtain the coefficient of $T_m$ that is arguably simpler than given in \cite{viresh-guus}.
Instead calculating the coefficient of the Taylor series of $\log(Z(G,\lambda))$ we will focus of on its derivative 
\[
R_G(\lambda)=\lambda\frac{\partial}{\partial\lambda}\log(Z(G,\lambda))=\sum_{v\in V}\frac{Z^\textrm{in}_v(G,\lambda)}{Z(G;\lambda)}.
\]
It is easy to see that $k$th coefficient of the Taylor-series of $\log(Z(G;\lambda)))$ is $k$ times the $k$th coefficient of $R_G(\lambda)$. For the rest we will consider the ratio 
\[\frac{Z_v^{\textrm{in}}(G,\lambda)}{Z(G,\lambda)}=\frac{Z^{\textrm{in}}_v(G,\lambda)}{Z^{\textrm{out}}_v(G,\lambda)+Z^{\textrm{in}}_v(G,\lambda) }=\frac{R_{G,v}(\lambda)}{1+R_{G,v}(\lambda)) }=\sum_{k\ge 0} (-1)^{k}R_{G,v}(\lambda)^{k+1},\]
where $R_{G,v}=\frac{Z^\textrm{in}_v(G,\lambda)}{Z^{\textrm{out}}_v(G,\lambda)}$.

For $S \subseteq V(G)$ and $U\subseteq V(G)\setminus S$ let us define
\[
R_{S,U}(\lambda)=\frac{Z(G[S],\lambda)}{Z(G[S\cup tU],\lambda)}
\]
If $U=\{u_1,\dots,u_\ell\}$, then
\[
R_{S,U}(\lambda)=\prod_{i=1}^{\ell} R_{S\cup\{u_1,\dots,u_{i-1}\},u_{i} }(\lambda),
\]
Using this identity we see that $R_{S,U}(\lambda)$ can be computed with $|U|$ many multiplication of function of the form $R_{S',\{u'\}}$.

For any $S\subseteq V(G)$ and $u\notin S$ we have
\[
    R_{S,u}(\lambda)=\frac{Z(G[S])}{Z(G[S])+\lambda Z(G[S\setminus N(u)])}=\frac{1}{1+\lambda R_{S\setminus N(u),S\cap N(u)}(\lambda)}=\sum_{k\ge 0}(-\lambda\cdot R_{S\setminus N(u),S\cap N(u)}(\lambda))^k
\]
The $m$th Taylor serios of this function can be computed from the $(m-1)$th Taylor-series of
$R_{S\setminus N(u),S\cap N(u)}(\lambda)$ using $m$ many polynomial multiplication (e.g. using Horner-scheme). 
In order to measure how many terms we have to collect for the Taylor polynoimal we use the notation
$R_{S,U}^{(s)}(\lambda)$ for the $s$th Taylor polynomial of $R_{S,U}(\lambda)$.


Then
\[
\frac{Z_v^\textrm{in}(G,\lambda)}{Z(G,\lambda)}=\sum_{k\ge 0}(-1)^k \left(\lambda\cdot R_{V\setminus N[v],N(v)}(\lambda)\right)^{k+1}
\]
The $m$th Taylor serios of this function can be computed from the $(m-1)$th Taylor-series of $R_{V\setminus N[v],N(v)}(\lambda)$ using $m$ many polynomial multiplication (e.g. using Horner-scheme). 

Denote by $R_{S,U}^{(s)}(\lambda)$ the $s$th Taylor polynomial of $R_{S,U}(\lambda)$.


