\textbf{Output sensitive} \feri{old fast approach but less intuitive}
For the completeness let us describe a simple backtracking algorithm that will generate  trees that contains specific vertex (or an edge). 

To be precise, let $v$ be a vertex then and let $\mathcal{F}_{v,\le k}=\{F~|~v\in F\in\mathcal{T}_G, |F|\le k, \}$ and assume we have an ordering on the edges of $G$. 

Now the idea is that we consider a graph with vertex set $\mathcal{F}_{v,\le k}$ and $T_1$ is connected to $T_2$ iff $||T_2|-|T_1||=1$ and either $T_1\subset T_2$ or $T_2\subset T_1$ and we perform a DFS search on this tree starting from $\emptyset\in\mathcal{F}_{v,\le k}$.
With a simple solution (that we will discuss in details) we could maintain an actual tree, the set of possible edges to extend it. It will be clear that we can maintain this structure in $poly(m)$ space and have delay $poly(m)$, thus the running time would be at most $O((e\Delta)^m poly(m))$ in $poly(m)$ space.


To perform the DFS algorithm on $\mathcal{F}_{v,\le k}$ we identify/index each tree $T$ in $\mathcal{F}_{v,\le k}$ by the BFS ordering of the edges of the $T$ that we denote by $BFS(T)$. So $BFS(T)$ is the order of the edges in which they are revealed by the breath first search algorithm applied to the tree $T$ starting from $v$.
Assume that we are given a current tree $T$. Clearly we have access to the last edge of $BFS(T)$, thus we know what are the possible edges that we could use to extend $T$. 


First let us describe a way to generate trees of size at most $m$ containing a vertex $v$.
% To be precise, let $v$ be a vertex then and let $\mathcal{F}_{v,\le k}=\{F~|~v\in F\in\mathcal{T}_G^<, |F|\le k, \}$ and assume we have an ordering on the edges of $G$. Then for each tree $T\in\mathcal{F}_{v,\le k}$ we associate a sequence of its edges $DFS(T)$, that is an ordering of the edges of $T$ based on their visit time of the DFS algorithm in $T$. Now 


We will maintain in the procedure a the vertices of an active tree $T$, a queue $L$ of incident edges to $V(T)$ and an index of $L$ for every edge of $T$ and an active index $i$ of $L$.
So $T=(\{v\},\emptyset)$, $F=[]$,   $S=\{v\}$,  $L=\textrm{sorted}([(e,\emptyset) ~|~v\in e])$ and $j=0$, where $\textrm{sorted}(.)$ denotes the lexicographic ordering of the list.

While $|F|\le m$ we increase $j$ by 1 and check is the edge $e=L[j][0]$ can be used to extend $F$, i.e. are the endpoints of $e$ in $S$ or not,  and $|F|<m$ and $j\le |L|$. If we can extend by $e$, then $F=F+[(e,j,V(e) \setminus S)]$, $S=S\cup V(e)$ and $L=L+\textrm{sorted}([(f,e)~|~f\cap e\neq \emptyset])$.
If $|F|=m$ or $j>|L|$, then we are forced to delete the last edge of $T$, i.e. if the last entry of $F$ is $(e,k,u)$, then $S=S\setminus\{u\}$, delete the last entry of $F$, set $j=k$ and delete all entries of $L$ that has second coordinate $e$. (If we cannot delete such an entry of $L$ and $L$ consists of the starting list, then we are done.) 

Otherwise, we do nothing and we start the cycle again.


Feature, it is easy to maintain the set of broken edges of $T$ in the algorithm.





\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Tree Construction with Backtracking}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $v \gets 0$ \Comment{starting vertex}
\State $m \gets 15$ \Comment{maximum depth}

\State $F \gets []$ \Comment{initialize empty tree}
\State $L \gets$ sorted list of $((v, u), [])$ for each neighbor $u$ of $v$ \Comment{possible extensions}
\State $\text{exit\_index} \gets \text{length of } L$ \Comment{exit condition}

\State $S \gets \{v\}$ \Comment{initialize component with starting vertex}
\State $j \gets -1$ \Comment{current index}

\While{True}
    \If{$\text{length of } F \leq m$}
        \State $j \gets j + 1$

        \If{$j = \text{exit\_index}$ \textbf{and} $j = \text{length of } L$}
            \State \textbf{break}
        \EndIf

        \If{$\text{length of } F = m$ \textbf{or} $j = \text{length of } L$}
            \State $(e, k, u) \gets F[\text{last element}]$
            \State Remove last element from $F$
            \State $S \gets S \setminus \{u\}$
            \State $j \gets k$

            \While{$L[\text{last element}][-1] = e$}
                \State Remove last element from $L$
            \EndWhile
        \Else
            \State $e \gets L[j][0]$
            \State $\text{new\_list} \gets [u \text{ for } u \text{ in } e \text{ if } u \notin S]$

            \If{$\text{length of new\_list} \neq 0$}
                \State $w \gets \text{new\_list}[0]$
                \State Append $(e, j, w)$ to $F$
                \State $S \gets S \cup \{w\}$

                \State $L \gets L + $ sorted list of $((w, u), e)$ for each neighbor $u$ of $w$ in $G$

                \State \textbf{return} $F$
            \EndIf
        \EndIf
    \EndIf
\EndWhile

\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

