In this Letter, we presented an experimentally feasible scheme for quantum state tomography of Rabi-dressed states using only the photoelectron spectra. This scheme is quite general and not limited to Rabi oscillations in ionic states of atoms, as it requires only a few conditions: (i) synchronized and delay-controlled ionizing pulses that are short enough such that $T_I \ll 2\pi/\Omega$, and (ii) an accessible transition for the dressing within a high-intensity laser pulse. Our work enhances the understanding of bipartite entanglement generation in the context of photoionization. We show that entanglement arises from ionization at different times, which leaves the ionic dressed parent in a different state superposition. The entanglement appears in the momentum imaging of the photoelectrons.      

