This paper introduced a novel defense framework using randomized smoothing to enhance the safety and robustness of LLM-driven MAS in safety-critical scenarios. We improved resilience against adversarial inputs and hallucination propagation, particularly in consensus-seeking tasks, and our simulations confirmed these expectations by demonstrating stable and reliable agent coordination under uncertainty. The proposed method offers a promising approach for deploying LLM-based MAS in real-world aerospace and autonomous systems where traditional safety guarantees fall short.
% This paper is undertaken to develop multiple-stage cooperative guidance strategies that enable a simultaneous attack from desired directions for multiple missiles without time-to-go estimation. First, a two-stage spatial-temporal guidance strategy in two dimensions is developed by dividing the whole guidance procedure into cooperative guidance and proportional navigation guidance (PNG) stages. At the former stage, the impact time consensus and impact angle constraints are ensured by designing and superimposing controllers for two subsystems. Then, multiple missiles switch to the PNG stage to fly towards the target with zero miss distance. Furthermore, the guidance strategy is extended to three dimensions by adding a planar pursuit guidance stage initially. Finally, sufficient numerical simulations demonstrate that the proposed results are effective and convenient for precisely realizing the complex spatial-temporal cooperative missions even under missile speed variation.

