\subsection{Message-propagating FSMs}

We have focused so far on state-reporting FSMs, which as shown for \textit{M45}, are equivalent on alternating steps to ECA rules. Message-propagating FSMs, in contrast, can act at a distance across the lattice and are thus not reproducible by synchronous cellular automata. In this section we investigate two interesting cases in this class of FSMs.

\subsubsection{M44: Billiard ball dynamics}

\Cref{m44} plots the trajectory of \textit{M44} over four hundred steps from an initial random lattice of cells. The dynamics of this rule are reminiscent of invertible cellular automata \citep{Toffoli1990}, which commonly have billiard-ball like dynamics. Indeed, \textit{M44} is reversible and can be inverted simply by flipping the lattice of states at any step in the trajectory.

