We perform the same procedure for the recombination of I and Br in bath gases of He, Ne, Kr, and Xe. Since most of the experimental data is available at or near room temperature across all combinations of species, we show the thermally averaged rate coefficients at $T$ = 300~K alongside experimentally determined rates in the temperature range of 280~K to 320~K in Fig.~\ref{fig:kt_all}. Here, we see that the DTBR mechanism properly predicts the rate constant as a function of the nature of the third body. The agreement is remarkable, independent of the halogen atom. These results imply that the underlying interaction potential is accurate enough to describe the three-body dynamics and that DTBR is an efficient reaction mechanism for atom recombination at room temperature. However, we notice some deviations when the third body is He.

